AB  - As artificial intelligence (AI) rapidly reshapes global health, one of the most fundamental universal principles, equity, risks being sidelined [1]. Despite decades of debate, the global health community still lacks conceptual clarity and operational consensus on what equity truly means in research, partnerships, and governance. The renewed push for decolonizing global health reflects long-standing concerns about inequitable power dynamics, extractive research practices, and disproportionate agenda-setting by institutions in the global north. However, without deliberate safeguards, the expansion of AI threatens to amplify these disparities on an unprecedented scale.
