PMID- 38879184
TI  - Artificial intelligence and inequality: insights from the Philippines
FAU - Alibudbud R.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/pubmed/fdae109 [doi]
AB  - In a recent editorial, it was highlighted that artificial intelligence (AI) holds substantial potential to improve public health by analyzing large datasets to inform and develop effective policies and programs.1 However, it also cautioned that AI can reinforce existing inequalities, leading to inequitable outcomes in terms of healthcare and social benefits.1 This issue is particularly relevant in the Philippines, where a digital and socioeconomic divide persists. Despite nearly universal 4G coverage at 99% and 5G penetration at 70%, the speed and affordability of internet connections remain barriers to widespread adoption.2 Internet access and speed are still low in lower income and rural regions.2 Since traditional AI technologies depend on internet access,3,4 and given the economic disparities in the Philippines, advancements in AI may disproportionately benefit only those in higher-income and urban areas. To ensure AI promotes health equity and the well-being of all communities, it is crucial to address the digital and socioeconomic divide. Improving the affordability and speed of internet access is essential, as is making internet-access devices more affordable.
