PMID- 38650580
TI  - Foundation models: the future of surgical artificial intelligence?
FAU - Lam K
FAU - Qiu J.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/bjs/znae090 [doi]
AB  - Most surgical artificial intelligence (AI) to date has been developed for a single specific task and trained only on data relevant to that task. However, foundation models (FMs) are trained on broad data sets and later adapted to a range of downstream tasks. It is the sheer scale of FMs that gives them unrivalled generalist intelligence, which multiplies the potential applications of AI within surgery1. Google’s Med-PaLM M can ingest data including clinical language, imaging, and genomics. It performs comparably to or outperforms specific models at tasks ranging from mammography interpretation to radiology report generation2. Endo-FM has surpassed existing state-of-the-art methods in disease recognition, polyp segmentation, and detection in endoscopy3. VisionFM has applications in ophthalmology, such as disease recognition, lesion and anatomic structure segmentation, and prognosis and biomarker prediction, and can generalize to imaging modalities and devices not seen in the training set4. The development of a surgical model similar to VisionFM would lead to a paradigm shift in the breadth of surgical AI. Instead of distinct surgical algorithms, an all-encompassing FM could be used for applications from risk prediction to anatomical segmentation. However, developing such a model within surgery presents unique challenges. VisionFM was trained on 3.4 million ophthalmic images from over 500 000 individuals and that volume of data is not yet available within surgery. Surgery is also highly contextual and FMs must be able to react to dynamic scenario changes. Early work has explored how these challenges could be met. SurgicalGPT (where GPT stands for Generative Pre-trained Transformer) can answer image questions concerning procedural phase or instrument location5. Google DeepMind’s Robotic Transformer 2 incorporates vision, language, and action, allowing the translation of text commands into robotic commands6, potentially revolutionizing the functionality of surgical robotics. Approaches to the development of a surgical FM to date have been made with a single domain in mind (for example endoscopic video) or incomplete domains (for example image or language). This is because the training of FMs requires huge volumes of data and computing power, meaning that focusing on particular domains makes development more manageable. However, such a narrow approach risks a fragmented landscape, with no single effective solution in place. Therefore, a holistic FM for surgery is proposed (Fig. 1), which, through its ability to process multimodal data, covers all aspects of surgery. This can be achieved either by building a giant model composed of many domain-specific FMs (‘mixture of experts’, where the model is able to decide which specific model to use) or by developing a single giant FM for surgery. In this way, FMs will no longer be stand-alone products, but be more equivalent to an operating system that can house multiple discrete applications. This FM layer would form the knowledge layer underpinning the model. A technical adaption layer sits on top, working through prompt engineering (crafting prompts to guide the model), supervised fine-tuning (adjusting the numerical values or weights associated with the connections between individual neurons across the layers of the model), or linear probing (extracting the relevant features using the model, for example locating instruments within a video frame). Finally, the top layer consists of specific applications for the model, ranging from advising safe areas of dissection to skill assessment through the tracking of surgical instruments. See Fig. 1. Foundation models in surgery: a vision of future surgical artificial intelligence FMs will ultimately open up the world of surgical AI for clinicians. Development of surgical AI currently requires coding of algorithms from scratch, which is not possible for most clinicians. FMs may soon allow clinicians to input their desired function into a template model and output a bespoke prototype model with fewer technical barriers. Timelines and costs for this technology are challenging to predict, as it requires buy-in from institutions and funders. However, the rapid advances in iterations of GPT models suggest that this technology, and the pressure to adopt it, will grow exponentially. Promising moves have been made by the UK government, with funding directed towards research hubs and shared infrastructure models7,8. The volumes of data and computing power required are vast. High-quality annotated data sets are scarce, largely due to the lack of existing infrastructure and cultural resistance to sharing data9. The development of surgical FM requires collaborative efforts to pool high-quality surgical data. However, promising progress has been made with regard to efforts to provide the scale of data required to feed the learning capacity of FMs. Pre-training of FMs has been successfully carried out on web-scale raw data10 and generative models are able to create high-fidelity synthetic data. Such instances may signal a move away from labour-intensive annotated data sets. However, non-traditional data sets are likely to create concerns around the ultimate reliability of FMs. This is a fundamental issue, as any errors in the FM layer could impact all subsequent applications. Therefore, validation and calibration methods for FMs must also be established. Finally, efficient computing solutions11 must be created for hospitals that may not be able to afford the large-scale computing power required to apply these models in practice. Surgical AI to date has failed to prove itself either reliable or clinically beneficial enough to be widely adopted. However, FMs could finally deliver the clinical benefit that AI has long promised. To ease public concerns, the use of FMs must start with low-risk applications alongside human decision-making12, thus augmenting, rather than replacing, clinician capability. Ethical translation of this technology, with patient safety at its core, is vital if FMs are to become a clinical reality. Significant concerns that centre around patient identification from training data persist, but methods to counter this are available13. Commercialization of data is another challenge and a surgical FM must therefore be open source to encourage collaboration and data pooling. If these conditions and concerns are recognized and met, FMs provide almost unlimited potential and may yet herald a new era of surgical AI applications. K.L. is supported by a National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR) Academic Clinical Fellowship and acknowledges infrastructure support from the NIHR Imperial Biomedical Research Centre. Kyle Lam (Conceptualization, Writing—original draft, Writing—review & editing), and Jianing Qiu (Conceptualization, Writing—original draft, Writing—review & editing) The authors declare no conflict of interest.
