PMID- 34999779
TI  - Artificial intelligence in thoracic oncology: moving from proof of concept to clinical practice
FAU - Ostberg NP
FAU - Elefteriades JA.
DP  - 2022
AID - 10.1093/ejcts/ezab536 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) has long been promised to revolutionize the way that medicine is practiced and the past several decades have demonstrated steady, perhaps slower than expected, progress. While AI has not been a panacea that replaces higher-order clinical reasoning, there have been several examples of robust applications of AI to multiple areas of medicine, with domains such as postsurgical prognostication and augmenting interoperative performance most applicable to cardiothoracic surgery [1]. Many of these successful applications of AI have occurred when clinical domain experts and AI engineers collaborate to solve a specific, well-defined clinical problem with clear outcomes. In this issue of the Journal, Kudo et al. [2] tackle another such clinical problem. Using high-resolution computed tomography imaging from 157 patients with clinical N0 non-small-cell lung cancer, they apply a commercial, semi-supervised AI-based tool that is able to segment the entire tumour nodule while differentiating between the ground glass and solid portions of the lesion, allowing for volumetric measurement of the lesion in 3 dimensions as well as calculation of the consolidation tumour ratio. They show via multivariate analysis that high solid tumour volume as determined by the AI algorithm is associated with poor recurrence-free survival, among other traditional prognostication factors such as SUVmax from PET and carcinoembryonic antigen. Ultimately, AI measurements can augment other imaging metrics to guide segmentectomy versus lobectomy for patients with small solid-predominant lung adenocarcinomas. The AI tool employed in this study utilized the state of the art UNet architecture and showed excellent performance identifying solid lesions (precision = 0.888, recall = 0.904) and reasonable test performance on the more difficult segmentation task of identifying ground glass lesions (precision = 0.676, recall = 0.698). The training set used to develop this model is relatively large (n = 357), although expanding the number of images and perhaps oversampling ground glass predominant lesions could dramatically improve performance. An interesting future direction could be to utilize pixel level data as inputs into an AI model rather than extracting features such as volume; using deep learning with this approach would create a model that would intrinsically consider some of the metrics assessed in this paper along with the three-dimensional shape and distribution of radiodensity within the nodules—possibly yielding more robust prognostic information [3]. There are several advantages that this AI method offers compared to more traditional measurement methods. The ability to assess three-dimensional metrics of nodules without the labor-intensive process of outlining lesions in multiple slices results in a more holistic and accurate assessment of tumour heterogeneity. In addition, the speed of the AI algorithm, while not reported in this paper, is certainly significantly faster than the laborious process of accurately outlining lesions by hand across multiple CT (computerized tomography) slices, thus saving radiologists valuable time. The authors mention standardization of measurement as another strength. While the algorithm does provide reproducible outputs, it can be easily fooled by unusual data (i.e. pneumonias producing similar ground glass opacities on CT). Care should be taken to occasionally audit the AI measurements, particularly when they deviate substantially from what would be expected from a cursory examination of the scan. Implementation of this tool into the clinical workflow is the next logical step, but challenges abound [4]. In particular, prospective multi-site validation should be performed that evaluates the ability of this AI tool to scale to other institutions and different practice settings while maintaining similar performance metrics. This raises several potential challenges, such as worsening performance with different disease prevalence, guarding against dataset shift and ensuring adequate performance across different imaging device manufactures and image storage formats. Of particular note for this study is ensuring interoperability of IT systems with the AI software, as the authors mention transmitting data with a proprietary imaging communications system. Key metrics such as AUROC stratified by site would be critical to identify early issues as well as robust error analysis when the AI algorithm fails to produce the expected outputs. Building an active learning pipeline would ensure that any algorithm errors are factored into training data for improved future performance. Ideally, use of the AI algorithm would be associated with better outcomes, such as better progression-free or overall survival for patients in whom the algorithm is used, although powering a randomized trial to detect this difference would be challenging. While the world of regulating AI-based software is still in its infancy, the sort of multi-site prospective validation described would improve chances of regulatory approval [5, 6]. This work represents a useful case study of both the promises of AI—improved prognostication—and its potential drawbacks—including challenges with prospective validation. Regardless, this is a novel and innovative approach for the management of patients with lung cancers and this work pushes thoracic oncology towards an AI-based precision medicine future.
