PMID- 39331456
TI  - Authors' Response: Ethical use of artificial intelligence in academic writing
FAU - Mondal H
FAU - Mondal S.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.4103/IJO.IJO_356_24 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editor, We thank the authors[1] for taking the time to read our article, “ChatGPT in academic writing: Maximizing its benefits and minimizing the risks,”[2] and sharing their thoughtful concerns regarding the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in academic or scientific writing. We acknowledge the valid points they raised regarding potential challenges and risks associated with AI-generated content from large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT, Google Bard, Microsoft Bing Chat, Perplexity, and Claude. The potential emergence of biased content in the generated output may come from biased training data. Hence, authors should exercise caution and refrain from unquestioningly relying on AI-generated content, recognizing that LLMs function as a tool and not as the primary author.[3] For example, a typist was instructed by an officer to type a letter to a friend to convey birthday wishes. Due to the habit of drafting official letters, the typist addressed the letter with the salutation “Respected friend.” However, when the officer checked it, the typist was asked to modify it to “Dear friend.” The amended letter was signed by the officer and was dispatched to the friend. The recipient experienced joy upon receiving the heartfelt wish as if the letter was written by her friend. In the contemporary era, AI takes on a role akin to the typist, allowing users to command its functions for content creation and editing in accordance with their preferences. In this context, we would like to share why the AI is not an author. According to the International Committee of Medical Journal Editors criteria, an author should satisfy at least one criterion from all four categories of tasks as shown in Table 1. While AI can satisfy the first and second criteria, it cannot satisfy the third and fourth criteria.[4]Table 1: International Committee of Medical Journal Editors criteria for authorship and capability of AIThe concern raised about overreliance on AI is an interesting and futuristic thought. When a new technology comes, we start using it and gradually it evolves. For example, the internet is such a technology. Can we think now about writing a research protocol without searching literature on the internet? However, think about the difficulty researchers faced before the introduction of PubMed in 1997.[5] Generative AI is evolving, and the future will tell us how much we will be dependent on that. In this context, we would like to share that leading publishers are not against the use of AI in medical writing. However, they suggest cautious use of the technology. Perspectives of three publishers are shown in Fig. 1.Figure 1: Portion of texts from guidelines laid by three publishers about the use of artificial intelligence in writingIn response to the suggestion for more concrete examples or case studies, we appreciate the feedback. We recognize the importance of providing practical guidance, and we will consider incorporating relevant examples and case studies in future publications to enhance the clarity of our recommendations. The insights regarding the vulnerability of residents under training and postgraduate practitioners to potential misuse of AI tools are particularly valuable. Any technology comes with its own set of advantages and disadvantages. As the technology is with us and comes with free access, any resident can access it for any purpose. Hence, we presume that a widespread training program on the ethical use of AI technology for such vulnerable users is the need of the hour. Financial support and sponsorship: Nil. Conflicts of interest: There are no conflicts of interest.
JT  - Indian J Ophthalmol
OT  - Medicine
OT  - Medical education

PMID- 40211784
TI  - Opportunities and challenges for young physicians in the era of artificial intelligence: current status and future perspectives
FAU - Gao B.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1093/postmj/qgaf052 [doi]
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) has witnessed rapid evolution in recent years and is gradually integrated into healthcare systems worldwide. For young physicians, navigating this landscape requires a delicate balance. They must weigh the potential benefits offered by AI-driven innovations against the intricate demands of patient care, ethical imperatives, and the evolving nature of their professional roles. This concise review aims to spotlight the key opportunities and challenges that young physicians encounter in this digital age. Additionally, practical recommendations are provided to guide young physicians in integrating AI knowledge seamlessly into their clinical practice. Finally, this review concludes with forward-looking suggestions,including incorporating AI training into medical education curricula and formulating policy initiatives. The overarching goal is to optimize the role of young physicians within the dynamic and evolving digital healthcare environment, ensuring they are well-equipped to leverage AI for the betterment of patient care.
