AID - 10.1093/cvr/cvab211 [doi]
AB  - Advancements in mobile digital technology, including wearable devices with mechanical, electrical, and optical sensors, have allowed for an unprecedented scale of measurement in the monitoring of human health. As well as tracking, there is the potential to promote cardiovascular health by encouraging positive lifestyle behaviours . With wearable devices enabling theoretically continuous measurement, we have moved from simple devices that can estimate step counts intermittently via accelerometry to a digital ecosystem where continuous daytime activity and night-time sleep tracking is facilitated by an array of sensors embedded in a single wrist worn device that can estimate heart rate and rhythm, measure oxygen saturation, monitor auditory signals, perform gait analysis, detect fall risk, and much more. This change in the sophistication of consumer devices has been rapid. Early devices focused entirely on health and fitness and were challenged with short battery life and suboptimal accuracy. With each new generation, battery life and algorithm accuracy improved, reaching clinical grade standards several years ago (at least for heart rate1). This has facilitated a move from health promotion to disease prevention with devices now incorporating algorithms to detect abnormal heart rhythms like atrial fibrillation from photoplethysmography (PPG) while adding functionality like the single-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) and oxygen saturation monitoring. Taking advantage of these technical advances, we initiated the My Heart Counts Cardiovascular Health Study in 2015 recruiting 40 000 individuals in the first month to a digital application that featured digital consent,2 smartphone and wearable monitoring of physical activity, fitness testing, genetic data sharing (via 23andMe), and digital return of results.3 We used the platform in 2019 to demonstrate the efficacy of light touch nudges for physical activity promotion in a fully digital randomized controlled trial.4 In addition to realizing the scale and potential of a digital cohort, we encountered challenges, particularly with long-term participant engagement.
