PMID- 37152057
TI  - Editorial: Methods in gastrointestinal cancers
FAU - Liu Y
FAU - Wang X
FAU - Wang J
FAU - Wu C
FAU - Li W
FAU - Song Q
FAU - Jin Z.
DP  - 2023
AID - 10.3389/fonc.2023.1193964 [doi]
AB  - As editors of this special issue, it was my pleasure to review a wide range of interesting, and innovative articles in the field of methods in gastrointestinal cancers. In this editorial, I express the application of articificial intelligence（AI） findings and perspectives of methods in gastrointestinal cancers. Articificial intelligence is a new technology in which researchers design and create relevant algorithms, process relevant data and draw corresponding conclusions without human assistance. I have summarized the perspectives detailed within each of the accepted articles about AI. Firstly, The high incidence of gastric cancer （GC）, which has increased the global burden. Because it is frequently diagnosed at a late stage, gastric cancer has a high mortality rate. EUS, MRI, CT, PET-CT and other imaging examinations are widely used in the diagnosis, treatment and prognosis of gastric cancer. With the development of endoscopic technology and the increasing awareness of early screening among the populations, the cure rate of most early gastric cancer has been significantly improved compared with the previous period, but the survival rate of advanced gastric cancer is still very low. Thus, for gastric cancer, early detection, diagnosis and treatment play a very important role, which is very beneficial for reducing mortality and improving survival rate. However, due to some objective reasons, such as the lack of professional knowledge and experience of endoscopists, the final diagnostic accuracy is biased. At the same time, with the continuous development of artificial intelligence technology, compared with the traditional imaging techniques, its diagnostic advantages are constantly expanding.Thus, through readed the literature published on this topic, I have found that convolutional neural network and deep learning are the current hot spots of artificial intelligence in the gastric cancer diagnosis and differential diagnosis. Jie feng et al. constructed an auxiliary EGC detection system based on DCNN and compared the diagnostic abilities of DCNN and endoscopists.1 It has good diagnostic sensitivity, rapid diagnosis characteristics, in aim to achieving the observation scheme. In the future, I think that the use of artificial intelligence technology can help endoscopists to find more suspicious early cancer, so that the general incidence of the population is greatly reduced. Considering the great potential and clinical application prospect of AI, the related application of AI combined with imaging examination will still be a research hotspot in the future. Secondly, PC (pancreatic cancer) is a common cancer in the digestive system. pancreatic cancer is often found when advanced, so it has a high fatality rate and is known as the "king of cancers". With the development of machine learning techniques, it has also showed a broad application prospect in the diagnosis, differential diagnosis, treatment evaluation and prediction of pancreatic diseases. For example, (1) the application of artificial intelligence in biology related to pancreatic malignant tumor mainly established a machine learning model from Stephen, which integrated the free DNA fragment pattern of the whole genome, with detection sensitivity ranging from 57% to more than 99% and specificity of 98%.2 This research makes it possible for early screening and diagnosis of pancreatic malignancies. (2) In the diagnosis of pancreatic malignant tumors, the technology combined with artificial intelligence can shorten the diagnosis time and improve the diagnosis accuracy compared with traditional CT/MRI and other imaging techniques. (3) In the treatment of pancreatic malignant tumors, the role played by artificial intelligence is mainly focused on predicting the possibility of surgery and the prognosis of patients. With the continuous development of algorithm technology, it is possible to predict postoperative complications of patients. (4) EUS-FNA/B have played an important role in the diagnosis of pancreatic malignancies. With the rapid development of artificial intelligence, it is possible to combine EUS-FNA/B with artificial intelligence, which makes it possible to reduce or even avoid misdiagnosis caused by inadequate diagnostic ability of physicians in some areas with underdeveloped technologies. In the future, as algorithms continue to improve, AI may be used as a screening tool for pancreatic EUS-FNA/B specimens. (5) In the treatment of pancreatic malignant tumor, artificial intelligence can even be involved. Based on the in-depth analysis of clinical examination and inspection results, it is possible to carry out personalized treatment for patients with pancreatic malignant tumor.Thirdly, Rectal cancer is one of the highest incidence of malignant tumors in the world. Because of the lack of awareness of patients in physical examination, Rectal cancer is often late at the time of diagnosis. Even after radical resection of rectal cancer, some patients will still develop liver metastases. Rectal cancer with liver metastasis often has a poor prognosis and a low 5-year survival rate. At present, with the incidence of rectal cancer increasing year by year, it has become one of the major problems that threaten human health. Liver is the most common site of distant metastasis of rectal cancer. The presence or absence of liver metastases is often a major consideration in treatment regimens. Through a retrospective study, Qiu et al. developed an XGB-based machine learning model to predict the risk of liver metastases in rectal cancer, which used a machine learning algorithm that combined seven clinical and pathological features.3It will be fascinating to observe the development of artificial intelligence. Although it may face large of challenges in the future, it has showed great prospects in the diagnosis and treatment of diseases. at the same time, it has also showed great prospects of methods in gastrointestinal cancers.
