PMID- 35075479
TI  - From blue lips to artificial intelligence: the history of a US centre of excellence
FAU - Ozkan J.
DP  - 2022
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehab909 [doi]
AB  - All correspondence relating to this article should be sent to cardiopulse@unicatt.it Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota, USA, has a high-profile reputation that belies its remote setting. Less well known is the story of Mayo's cardiac catheterization laboratory which was undocumented until a recent (non-clinical) intervention by senior figures set the record straight with a dedicated medical history book. Told over 18 chapters with contributions from 30 expert authors, The Mayo Clinic Cardiac Catheterization Laboratory; History, Research, and Innovations lays out a living record of the lab for past, present, and future generations of clinicians, scientists, and historians. It was put together by Drs David R. Holmes, Jr, Robert L. Frye, and Erik L. Ritman. They were joined in the project by Drs Paul A. Friedman, Donald J. Hagler, and Thomas M. Munger all of whom have many decades of first-hand experience between them. Dr Holmes (Figure 1) who headed up the cath lab for over 20 years says: ‘Histories are built upon the foundation of multiple layers, so they form the basis of the past as well as the foundations of the future’. Dr Holmes says an awareness of ‘standing on the shoulders of giants’ was the impetus for creating the book and recognizing some of the ‘giants’ involved in the history of the cath lab. David Holmes, MD. Mayo Clinic celebrated 155 years of contributions to clinical medicine in 2019. The history of the cath lab is more recent and is rooted in the 1940s with Earl Wood's experiments to prevent the loss of consciousness among US Air Force pilots during World War II. The result of Dr Wood's research using a giant human centrifuge at Mayo Clinic gave rise to the ‘G-Suit’—a protype flying suit with inflatable bladders in strategic areas so that pilots could maintain blood flow to the brain and prevent the loss of consciousness during combat manoeuvres. Dr Wood's legacy was a fledging cath lab which would build and develop his investigations into blood flow. By 1951, Mayo Clinic's first formal cath lab had been set up at St Mary's Hospital in Rochester and was investigating haemodynamics in different groups of patients. It was an auspicious start and the cross-pollinating ethos of scientists, clinicians, and engineers working together from Dr Wood's era characterizes the spirit of innovation on which the cath lab was built. Dr Holmes says: ‘What put Mayo Clinic at the forefront of the field was that the pre-eminent cardiac pathologist in the world, the pre-eminent physiologist in the world, the pre-eminent imaging experts in the world, and the pre-eminent cardiovascular surgeons in the world had offices that were all grouped together so during the breaks and cases being evaluated they talked about the issues to be addressed. It was that bringing together of people focused on the issues of how blood flow works normally, under stress, in an airplane or underneath the sea that fostered creativity and allowed us to move forward’. The book covers the major developments of different eras and tells the story of how the cath lab progressed with these innovations. It also offers valuable insights into how clinical practice was organized to meet patient needs and how training enabled the implementation of major new treatments and technologies as they occurred over the years. The story of the human centrifuge is one of the legendary tales captured in the book and the success of the experiments was due in part to the students and trainees of the time who were ‘volunteers’ in the study. Another tale involves the use of ‘Evans blue’ dye which was used to measure blood flow—often in budding cardiologists. ‘You could tell who was involved in cardiology because for a day or two after they’d been involved at the center in these observations their lips had a blue-ish tinge from the dye’, says Dr Holmes. Dr Holmes describes the publication as: ‘The work of many hands, many people and many patients, all of whom were incredibly important contributors in the past, the present and will be equally important in the future’. This overlapping process of the people who contributed to the success of the lab is reflected in the 30 authors who all bring their own perspective and expertise to the book. This collaborative, cross-pollinating approach is, Dr Holmes says, typical of the core ethos of Mayo Clinic. ‘That has been the ideal from the very beginning as far as I can tell from looking through the records. This is a place that is focused on problem solving and teams of people involved with solving those problems’ (Figure 2). Erik L. Ritman, MD, and Robert L. Frye, MD. The book, which is intended as a living history is aimed at people currently in the field, those who are planning futures in the field, those in related medical industries, medical historians, former members of the cath lab and department of cardiovascular disease, as well as other members of different departments such as physiology, pulmonology, and anatomy. As technology has changed, says Dr Holmes, so the number of issues has expanded, and the book allows for this by describing how to build a flexible and responsive system of care that allows professionals to address unmet needs. Dedicated to the pioneer Dr Earl H. Wood, the book covers specific areas of interest such as congenital heart disease, the introduction of coronary angiography with videometry, videodensitometry, haemodynamics, valvular heart disease, coronary and valvular intervention, and heart failure. Electrophysiology, which also has been a large part of the cath lab from the earliest days, is recognized for its past and current importance. Many of the chapters cover specific periods or decades and chart innovational trials, techniques, and technologies that developed over the years. Of his own journey, Dr Holmes says: ‘During the period of time that I was head of the cath lab, we went from taking diagnostic pictures to treating coronary stenosis by percutaneous procedures learned from people like Andreas Grüntzig, to using a variety of different techniques such as stent implantation and valve treatment options. It has been a case of identifying new approaches to treat previously untreatable or sub-optimally treated conditions. The pace of change continues to accelerate, and so the excitement of this journey continues unabated’. Writing the book has been an incredible journey and a chance to work with the 30 collaborators who, he says, ‘Were legends or are legends or who will be legends in the future’. Conflict of interest: none declared.
