AB  - Artificial intelligence transforms research, but the integrity norms do not changeArtificial intelligence creates opportunities for better science, from idea creation and automated insight harvesting to fluently written articles and more accessible science communication.It may revolutionize the way we do research.In a decade from now, our daily work might be unrecognizably changed in ways we cannot imagine today.However, the principles for research integrity do not change with the advent of artificial intelligence.When ChatGPT was introduced in late 2022, the scientific community became concerned about growing risks of non-authenticity of published work.Journals and learned societies responded with codes of conduct to regulate the use of artificial intelligence in research.The more detailed they were, the faster they were outdated and replaced.Indeed, it makes little sense to overregulate artificial intelligence, and certainly not in research.When the European Federation of Academies of Sciences and Humanities (ALLEA) updated the European Code of Conduct for Research Integrity in 2023 (https:// allea.org/ wpconte nt/ uploa ds/ 2023/ 06/ Europ ean-Code-of-Condu ct-Revis ed-Editi on-2023.pdf), a significant change was requiring disclosure of artificial intelligence use.This so-called ALLEA Code added as unacceptable practice, 'Hiding the use of artificial intelligence or automated tools in the creation of content or drafting of publications.'Researchers must disclose precisely how artificial intelligence was used, which tool was employed, and for which parts of the research or writing.That makes it a robust, future-proof norm: research integrity permits artificial intelligence use, but it is misleading to present your work as if you were not assisted by artificial intelligence.Authorship norms and criteria were well-established (https:// www.icmje.org/ recom menda tions/ browse/ rolesand-respo nsibi lities/ defin ing-the-role-of-autho rs-and-contr ibuto rs.html) before artificial intelligence.The possibilities of generative artificial intelligence have not changed them, but rather emphasized that the essence of authorship is responsibility and accountability.Because only humans can be held accountable for the content of a paper, ChatGPT and other non-humans cannot be an author (https:// wame.org/ page3.php
