AB  - Identifying drowning and estimating the postmortem interval (PMI) have long been challenging 7 problems in forensic medicine. Traditional examination methods involve observing physical signs, 8 such as white foam in the nose or mouth, lung contraction or overinflation, pulmonary edema, and the 9 presence of water in the stomach, to determine whether death was caused by drowning. were then amplified and sequenced using the 16s rDNA method. The research results indicated that 27 samples taken from the brain and liver between 5 to 14 days after death are optimal for analysis. 28Additionally, significant differences in the microbial communities were observed in the brain and liver 29 samples. As the PMSI increased, the dissimilarity in microbial communities between the liver and 30 brain of the drowning group and the post-mortem submersion group decreased. Therefore, this method 31 cannot be deemed reliable for determining drowning. Accurate PMSI estimation models were 32 developed for each organ based on their microbiota. The liver had a mean absolute error (MAE) of 33 1.282±0.189 days, the brain had a MAE of 0.989 ± 0.237d, and the cecum had a MAE of 0.818 ± 34 0.165d. 35Similarly, in Dmitrijs' study, it was demonstrated that microbial communities can be utilized to 36 determine the PMSI in juvenile swine (Dmitrijs et al., 2022). In Yu's study, it was shown that artificial 37 intelligence is better at automatically identifying diatoms in drowning cases (Yu et al., 2022
