PMID- 39763287
TI  - SCARLET: A custom artificial intelligence agent for practicing verbal patient communication skills
FAU - Stefanik DE
FAU - Amer RS.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1002/jdd.13824 [doi]
AB  - Effective chairside communication is a cornerstone of dental practice and essential in building patient trust.1 Conversational skills utilized between a patient and provider during care plan discussions can have a considerable impact on weighing options and making definitive treatment decisions. Traditionally, dental education has relied on a combination of didactic lessons, video demonstrations, and role-playing to teach dental students the skills required for effective patient communication. This project leveraged innovations in artificial intelligence (AI) to enhance dental students’ verbal communication skills. SCARLET (Simulated Clinical AI for Realistic Learning and Educational Training) is a proof-of-concept conversational AI agent that combines machine learning, large language models,2 and speech recognition to simulate authentic human interactions (Table 1). The initial creation of SCARLET began with a custom “prompt” (Table 2) that provided background and context to help guide the conversation between the AI agent (the simulated patient) and a preclinical dental student “provider.”3 ElevenLabs technology4 was then used to construct our AI-generated virtual “patient” with a human-like voice and responsiveness. Synchronous interaction with the AI agent occurs when the “provider” speaks into a microphone and asks questions or clarifies statements, resulting in an immediate spoken response from the “patient.” You are an AI agent designed to simulate a female dental patient for dental students. This patient is a mother of two young children, currently underemployed, and experiences difficulty commuting to dental appointments due to reliance on public transportation. She is seeking treatment for a lower right molar that has been sensitive to cold temperatures for the past 2 weeks. Key patient details: Age: 32 Occupation: Underemployed Family: Mother of two young children Chief complaint: Pain in lower right molar when drinking cold liquids. No abscess formation or hemorrhaging Duration of symptoms: 2 weeks Medical history: None significant Dental history: Only goes to dentist when in pain, no recent procedures Social history: Relies on public transportation for appointments Express concern: Genuine worry about the pain and inconvenience caused by the dental issue Our appraisal of the SCARLET project began with predefined evaluation questions, and after several iterations of AI agent testing, the “provider” questionnaire was modified to yield the most cogent responses from the “patient” (Table 3). The AI agent's voice responses were surprisingly realistic and the agent's ability to maintain a natural conversational flow made it easy to forget that we were talking to a computer and not interacting with a real patient over the phone. The responsiveness of the AI agent was seamless and timely, just as a conversation with a human patient would typically be. There were no long pauses, synthetic vocalization, or flawed responses—instead, the AI agent used phrases and conversational segues akin to talking with a real patient. As the AI agent is readily available for any length of time, the student can practice as often as necessary to sharpen their facility with dialogue, securing enhanced skills and self-assurance in patient communication. Since the AI agent speaks in such a natural manner, the two-sided conversation between the “patient” and the provider may also assist with developing social skills not previously inherent to the dental student. An instructor can create an unlimited number of patient cases with the desired characteristics, context, and prompts to approximate the variability students may encounter in the future with a diverse patient population. Repeated practice with these custom AI agents may help build competence and confidence in the preclinical dental student. The authors have nothing to report. The authors disclose no conflict of interest.
