OT  - Neurology
OT  - Biology
OT  - Medicine
OT  - Neuroscience

PMID- 40568575
TI  - Editorial: Artificial intelligence for cancer immunotherapy
FAU - El Naqa I
FAU - Pilon-Thomas S
FAU - Deutsch E.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.3389/fimmu.2025.1633422 [doi]
AB  - Zhan and colleague developed a prediction model for the classification of pulmonary nodules based using radiomics from preoperative CT imaging and clinical features (2). Several machine learning methods were evaluated with an explainable boosting model having the best performance. The pulmonary node imaging-grading reporting system (PNI-GARS) was the best predictive feature in their study.Two articles studied the prediction of axillary lymph node metastasis in breast cancer. The article by Guo and colleagues (3) developed a multimodal imaging model from mammograms and MRI. The best model combined traditional radiomics features with extracted deep learning ones using multilayer perceptron (MLP). In contrast, Ma and colleagues used ultrasound images to predict axillary lymph node metastasis (4). The best model combined sonogram features (microcalcifications) with radiomics using logistic regression.Duan and colleagues developed and validated a nomogram for diOerentiating immune checkpoint inhibitor-related pneumonitis from pneumonia in patients undergoing immunochemotherapy in a multicenter retrospective study (5). The nomogram was based on the random forest algorithm. The variables used in the nomogram included: smoking status, prior chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), ground glass opacities, nonspecific interstitial pneumonitis, Neutrophil to Lymphocyte Ratio (NLR), pleural eOusions, and Oxygen Partial Pressure (PaO2).A review articles was on the role of machine learning in predicting melanoma response to immunotherapy was presented by Li and colleagues (6). The authors conducted bias assessment the prediction model risk of bias assessment tool (PROBAST) and metaanalysis. They studied a total of 36 studies consisting of 30 cohort studies and 6 casecontrol. They analyzed outcome measures of progression-free survival, overall survival, and treatment response. Their findings indicted that there is considerable predictive accuracy in melanoma immunotherapy response and prognosis, however, there was a systematic lack of external validations in many cases.Lastly, a review of deep Learning applications for malignant lymph node segmentation and detection was presented by Wu and colleagues (7). This is valuable for treatment planning purposes. The review covered five clinical sites: head and neck, upper extremity, chest, abdomen, and pelvis highlighting current challenges and future trends that would impact clinical application.These articles despite their value in highlighting AI role in immunotherapy they covered only a fraction of the potential applications of AI/ML in immunotherapy particularly in areas related to understanding primary or secondary tumor resistance to immunotherapy, identifying improved biomarkers, and developing new treatment strategies for personalizing immunotherapy. For instance, AI can identify candidate biomarkers that can be applied clinically to select patients for the appropriate immunotherapy regimens and save others the unwanted toxicities and costs (8). This Research Topic highlights the significant impact of AI and ML in transforming cancer immunotherapy. It provides new insights and tools to address the complex challenges in this area. By utilizing advanced computational methods, researchers are improving the accuracy of diagnostic and therapeutic approaches and creating innovative solutions that could change patient care and outcomes. The use of AI and ML in immunotherapy research represents a major shift, promising to speed up the development of more eOective and personalized treatment strategies. The pace of these changes is likely to be tremendous in the years to come.
