PMID- 36342201
TI  - Artificial intelligence techniques may innovate patient selection for thoracic endovascular aortic repair
FAU - Domanin M
FAU - Mandigers TJ
FAU - Quarteroni AM
FAU - Trimarchi S.
DP  - 2022
AID - 10.1093/ejcts/ezac526 [doi]
AB  - The current definition of sarcopenia is a progressive and generalised skeletal muscle disorder that involves the accelerated loss of muscle mass and function, thereby leading to physical frailty and adverse outcomes [1]. Previous studies have investigated a link between sarcopenia and the survival of patients undergoing elective endovascular aortic repairs, and the radiologic assessment of sarcopenia has been highlighted as a potentially useful adjunctive preoperative tool for risk stratification in these patients [2]. For thoracic endovascular aortic repair (TEVAR) specifically, evidence remains limited. A recent study has linked lumbar sarcopenia to increased 5-year mortality in patients undergoing TEVAR for descending thoracic aortic aneurysms [3]. Another study stratified mortality after TEVAR by thoracic and lumbar sarcopenia and associated thoracic sarcopenia—but not lumbar sarcopenia—with an increased 5-year mortality [4]. The recently published study by Liu et al. [5] has narrowed this gap by exploring the association between lumbar sarcopenia and outcomes of 428 patients with complicated type B aortic dissection who underwent TEVAR. First, lumbar sarcopenia was manually quantified by software-guided radiological assessment [i.e. using computed tomography (CT)] of the lean psoas muscle area [LPMA; psoas muscle area (PMA) × psoas muscle density (PMD)]. However, the uniqueness of this study lies in the machine-learning-derived algorithm for automatic segmentation and measurement of the PMA and PMD that the authors developed. This algorithm was able to obtain values that were significantly and strongly correlated to the manual measurements. The authors stratified their patient cohort into a low and high LPMA cohort and found significantly higher rates of 30-day mortality and all-cause mortality at follow-up (not for stroke and reinterventions) in the low LPMA group. Multivariate regression also identified low LPMA as an independent predictor of follow-up mortality. An important aspect to highlight and discuss is the heterogeneity in the preoperative radiological assessment of sarcopenia among the literature [2]. As Liu et al. [5] pointed out, skeletal muscle mass can be evaluated using PMA, while PMD may be considered a surrogate for muscle function. Keeping the definition of sarcopenia in mind, which includes both skeletal muscle mass and function, the approach to combine PMA and PMD into LPMA seems logical. Nevertheless, the 2 previously addressed studies evaluating a link between sarcopenia and survival after TEVAR did both only consider PMA [3, 4]. Moreover, the second study differentiated between thoracic sarcopenia (i.e. rectus abdominis, latissimus dorsi, intercostal, erector spinae and external and internal oblique muscles at the T12 level) and lumbar sarcopenia (i.e. psoas muscle at the L3 level). These differences in methodological study designs are important when interpreting and comparing the findings of these studies, and future studies might aim to homogenize this. Future studies that aim to clarify the utility of preoperative sarcopenia assessment for TEVAR might also incorporate clinical measurements of muscle function instead of using radiological surrogates, such as measuring preoperative muscle grip strength, gait speed, and/or timed up and go testing [1]. Moreover, skeletal muscle mass could be quantified using dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry [1]. However, this is not feasible in an acute setting or in the routine preoperative assessment of patients undergoing elective TEVAR, while CT scans are widely available in these patients. Therefore, CTs may provide the right platform to include sarcopenia assessment into clinical practice as an adjunct to aid patient selection for TEVAR. In particular, the automated CT segmentation proposed by Liu et al. [5] may overcome the challenges associated with manual measurements (e.g. observer training, software availability, time consumption) and challenges associated with the realization of a widespread clinical application. Thanks to the U-Net algorithm proposed in the seminal work by Ronneberger et al. [6], Liu et al. [5] were able to enhance such adjunctive artificial intelligence techniques. On the one hand, more recent contributions along this line could be considered. Notable examples are the numerous developments and breakthroughs in the U-Net architecture, together with many innovations that have advanced in deep learning to empower U-Net [7]. Moreover, several clinical needs and technical challenges in medical imaging could benefit from deep convolutional neural networks [8]. On the other hand, algorithms based on physics-inspired biomechanics and fluid dynamics models could help explaining why sarcopenia impacts the outcomes of surgical interventions and more in general to better explain the pathogenesis of vascular diseases [9]. This analysis would involve a more in-depth description of the fluid–structure interaction of the vessel and surrounding tissue, with the aim of in silico reproducing physical biomarkers (e.g. pressure, wall shear stress, von Mises stresses) that eventually concur in the degeneration of the aortic mechanical status (Figs 1 and 2). Computational fluid dynamics of 3D models with velocity vectors identifying the different behaviour of jet flow and the impingement area on the AAA lumen boundary (left) with the original lumen boundary displacement maps (right) at systolic peak (from Piccinelli M, Vergara C, Antiga L, Forzenigo L, Biondetti P, Domanin M. Impact of haemodynamics on lumen boundary displacements in abdominal aortic aneurysms by means of dynamic computed tomography and computational fluid dynamics. Biomech Model Mechanobiol. 2013;12:1263–76. doi:10.1007/s10237-013-0480-5). Velocity magnitude (in metres per second) of blood flow in a whole human heart at the end of the ejection phase. Results obtained by a cardiac electromechanical-driven fluid dynamics computational model (from Zingaro A, Fumagalli I, Dede’ L, Fedele M, Africa PC, Corno AF et al. DCDS 2022;15(8):2391–2427. doi:10.3934/dcdss.2022052 and Fedele M, Piersanti R, Regazzoni F, Salvador M, Africa PC, Bucelli M et al. A comprehensive and biophysically detailed computational model of the whole human heart electromechanics, arXiv:2207.12460v1). Another study analysed the impact of preoperative total psoas area index (TPAI), a different method to assess sarcopenia, on patients with descending thoracic aortic aneurysms who underwent either open surgical repair or TEVAR [10]. Tanaka et al. [10] concluded that sarcopenic patients should preferably receive TEVAR if anatomically feasible, and because open surgical repair is more invasive, the effect of sarcopenia on outcomes may be more profound in open surgical repair as compared to endovascular interventions. Nevertheless, the association of sarcopenia with increased mortality by Liu et al. [5] and previous authors [3, 4] in patients undergoing TEVAR specifically, highlights that minimally invasive endovascular alternatives still request a minimal physical reserve to achieve favourable clinical outcomes. In this regard, a better identification of patients unfit for TEVAR may also help to maintain—the rather expensive—endovascular aortic repair sustainable, thereby avoiding the waste of limited resources for different healthcare systems. Given the limited but rising evidence regarding this topic, further research to determine the most suitable methodological approach to assess sarcopenia in patients undergoing endovascular aortic procedures seems needed, while AI techniques may be further improved and applied. Moreover, a better identification of patients who would benefit from pre- or postoperative muscle mass and function improvement using physical therapy and nutritional adjuncts may help to improve the longer-term survival after endovascular aortic repair as well and may theoretically present an important field of study.
JT  - Eur J Cardiothorac Surg
