PMID- 34679160
TI  - Additional Thoughts on Artificial Intelligence Evaluation of Facelift Surgery
FAU - Zhang C
FAU - Wang J.
DP  - 2022
AID - 10.1093/asj/sjab374 [doi]
AB  - We read with interest the article in Aesthetic Surgery Journal1 in which a new assessment tool for facelift surgery was introduced. Based on convolutional neural network algorithms, the authors successfully employed the artificial intelligence technique of face recognition to evaluate the perceived age changes before and after facelift surgery. Outcomes of different facelift procedures and specific techniques were also compared by the independent method. Facelifting is a main focus we have intensively worked on. A truly objective evaluation of postoperative outcomes has always been one of our challenges. Currently, the usual evaluation methods are patient-reported satisfaction and independent observer–reported estimation. However, subjective feelings are not necessarily able to accurately reflect the degree of age reduction. As Nahai said,2 it could be argued that dispassionate unbiased analysis is impossible to achieve with human observers. All observers, whether trained or untrained, carry bias.2,3 Other factors such as initial expectations or mutual compliments may even worsen it. Some patients may have the minimum ability to identify age by facial condition. Most notably, the subjective feeling of patients can be separated from actual surgical results or evaluation of others, which reminds us of unrealistically high expectations. Thus, preoperative expectation evaluation may help us avoid the dilemma of good surgical results but poor satisfaction. Objective testing methods, such as visioscan, cutometer, and 3-dimensional (3D) imaging analysis, can only evaluate skin texture or facial soft tissue capacity in 1 dimension rather than an overall assessment.4 An independent evaluation system can decrease bias and provide accurate age judgment. It is expected that artificial intelligence recognition can solve these problems effectively. No matter what kind of evaluation methods are adopted, satisfaction is always the most valuable for patients. After all, the ultimate purpose of facial aesthetic surgery is to please the patients themselves. Artificial intelligence recognition has greater potential to serve doctors rather than patients. It provides an objective method for doctors and helps compare the results of different surgical procedures. Different methods of facelift and whether fat grafting was compared in the current study and the results were also in line with the actual clinical observation. Four different convolutional neural networks were utilized to estimate preoperative perceived age. Although the mean age accuracy score was almost perfect at 100.4, the data of estimated preoperative age showed great differences among each network model. Microsoft (Redmond, WA) showed a maximum of 106.3, whereas Face Plus Plus (Beijing Megvii Technology Co., Ltd, Beijing, China) showed a minimum of 91.5. Taking a 50-year-old man as an example, his estimated age may range from 45.75 to 53.15 years. This result is consistent with another study by the authors’ team in which Microsoft and Face Plus Plus also showed the highest and lowest accuracy scores.5 It indicates that different algorithms yield different results. Thus, as the key to this methodology, an accurate algorithm needs to be unified and improved. A huge number of samples are always the cornerstone for machine learning to improve automatically. However, according to our observation, the number of multiple-site facelift surgeries is gradually decreasing due to the prevalence of less invasive surgeries, which may delay the progress of training data collection. We suggested that, as long as the apparent age changes significantly after operation, many facial rejuvenation surgeries can be included in analysis. Two-dimensional standard photographs are limited in accurately reflecting the actual facial characteristics. Photos taken under different parameters, angles, or distances may affect the ability of artificial intelligence to judge age. To improve accuracy, we believe that 3D imaging will be a better option because 3D imaging analysis can measure both the distance between facial landmarks and the changes of soft tissue volume. If artificial intelligence recognition is advanced enough to be applied to the local facial regions in the future, many other cosmetic surgeries can also be quantified. For example, the periocular aging can be divided into various items, including periocular wrinkles, upper eyelid skin relaxation, eyebrow ptosis, lower eyelid bags, and skin pigmentation. Each item can be scored by deep learning and recalculated by algorithms. The emergence of every new technique is inevitably accompanied by limitations and deficiencies. Although the application of artificial intelligence recognition in facial aesthetic surgical evaluation is still in a preliminary research stage, we believe in its unlimited potential in the future. With the accumulation of sample data, artificial intelligence algorithms will be continuously improved, followed by more accurate results. The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and publication of this article. The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and publication of this article.
