PMID- 32964230
TI  - Artificial Intelligence to measure coronary artery flow
FAU - Nicholls M.
DP  - 2020
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehaa668 [doi]
AB  - A multicentre research team has for the first time harnessed the power of Artificial Intelligence (AI) to instantly and accurately measure blood flow to the heart, helping predict which patients may suffer myocardial infarction (MI) or stroke. Professor James Moon, who led the study, sees the advance as significant and is optimistic that AI analysis of perfusion mapping will be a reliable, convenient, and detailed new biomarker in cardiac patient care. With myocardial perfusion reflecting the macro- and microvascular coronary circulation, researchers from University College London (UCL) and Barts Health NHS Trust collaborated with colleagues at the National Institutes for Health in the USA to explore the prognostic significance of stress myocardial blood flow (MBF) and myocardial perfusion reserve (MPR − the ratio of stress to rest MBF) in patients with suspected and known coronary artery disease referred clinically for perfusion assessment. The team conducted image analysis automatically, using a novel AI approach, to seek the associations of stress MBF and MPR with death and major adverse cardiovascular events, including MI, stroke, heart failure, late (>90 days) revascularization, and death. The study concluded: ‘In patients with known or suspected coronary artery disease, reduced MBF and MPR measured automatically inline using AI quantification of cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) perfusion mapping provides a strong, independent predictor of adverse cardiovascular outcomes’. Though measuring blood flow to the heart has been possible for decades, it remains an onerous task and non-invasive blood flow assessments, including CMR imaging, are difficult to analyse in a manner precise enough to deliver a prognosis or recommend treatment. Over the past 5 years, the UCL/Barts/NIH team has worked to make CMR ‘fully quantitative’ in measuring the actual amount of blood flow going to heart muscle, using AI to make the ‘slightly tedious’ human elements of drawing contours around the heart to identify where the muscle is and working out where the blood pool is, easier and quicker. In the study,1 researchers took routine CMR scans from more than 1000 patients attending St Bartholomew’s Hospital and the Royal Free Hospital and used the new automated AI technique devised by Dr Peter Kellman and Dr Hui Xue at NIH to analyse the images, enabling them to precisely and instantly quantify the blood flow to the heart muscle. By comparing the AI-generated blood flow results with the health outcomes of each patient, the team found that those with reduced blood flow were more likely to have adverse health outcomes. Professor Moon, Professor of Cardiology at UCL and the Clinical Director of Imaging at Barts Heart Centre (BHC), said this approach has now become an add-on to their clinical work and made interpretation of scans easier. CMR scans are still being read by eye for regional differences, but AI now measures the global changes and offer findings the eye may not have previously seen. With AI, time-consuming tasks are conducted rapidly and the team can operate at a scale it had not been able to do before. Additionally, AI enables that to be translated into clinical care, accelerate the process of discovery, and raise new questions. In addition, as well as being used to measure blood flow to the heart muscle to highlight abnormalities and predict adverse outcome for patients, Professor Moon said AI also told a story of what was happening in a wider context with the patient. ‘Blood flow to heart muscle is about the microvasculature of the circulation and the coronary arteries but both of those may be commonly diseased by processes going on elsewhere, such as atherosclerosis and ageing-related stiffening of arteries’, he said. Conventional CMR shows regional difference in perfusion to the expert eye. The AI would show this as an immediately obvious uniform orange colour during stress. But conventional imaging can miss balanced ischaemia, when all the blood vessels were equally diseased. The quantitative AI approach would show this as the myocardium remaining uniformly blue (as if unstressed). ‘By just measuring the large arteries, we are missing a whole chunk of the story about how our hearts are over our lifetimes, and that the microvasculature is clearly changing with age and diabetes and other parameters probably associated with chest pain and outcome. We think those are tractable with a new generation of treatments for the microvasculature disease function’, added Professor Moon, who sees an opportunity to develop new therapies for patients with chest pain where the coronary arteries do not appear abnormal. As a new marker of disease, he acknowledges there is still some way to go to fully understand and realize the potential of this advance with AI. ‘It is a significant step, not a silver bullet’, he said. ‘It makes what we do now easier and more reliable and opens the door to a future where we do new things’. As a next step, the team is seeing applications in other heart muscle disease, such as cardiomyopathies. ‘It is like turning the lights on to an understanding of how heart muscle works and seeing, especially in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, that there are really major perfusion defects that we never saw before’, he continued. The latest paper1 is part of a body of work, though an important milestone, which he acknowledges as another aspect to the clinical practice armoury, and it is being applied more widely in healthcare and is now available to about 30 hospitals. As well as arousing commercial interest, it has seen collaborators partner with engineers and physicists to translate AI to the coalface of clinical care. Funded by the British Heart Foundation, National Institute for Health Research, European Regional Development Fund, and Barts Charity, the study involved additional researchers from the Royal Free Hospital, Queen Mary University of London, and the University of Leeds. Dr Kristopher Knott (UCL Institute of Cardiovascular Science and Barts Health NHS Trust) said: ‘As poor blood flow is treatable, these better predictions ultimately lead to better patient care, as well as giving us new insights into how the heart works’. Dr Peter Kellman, director of the Medical Signal and Image Processing Program at the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute, part of the NIH, said the study demonstrated the growing potential of AI-assisted imaging technology to improve the detection of heart disease and ‘may move clinicians closer to a precision medicine approach to optimize patient care’. Professor Moon believes the AI approach will have more diverse applications and could change diagnostic imaging. ‘It is an indication that we are entering a new era of AI’, he said. ‘From a perfusion point of view, it will allow us to detect normality better; it may well define new disease groups; specific categories of microvasculature disease with reduced stress blood flow; and that may lay the foundations for new therapies’. Conflict of interest: none declared.
