PMID- 41474366
TI  - Abstract writing in the modern era of artificial intelligence
FAU - Chan K
FAU - Tang PT.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1093/cvr/cvaf253 [doi]
AB  - Abstracts are ubiquitous, and a key component of modern scientific research. In the 18th century, papers presented at meetings of the Royal Society would be summarized, initially for record-keeping (published from 1831 as the Proceedings of the Royal Society).1 Later, in the 19th century, scientific societies began publishing collections of summaries of newly published research.1 Early scientific abstracts were cheaper to print than full manuscripts, enabling wider publication, translation, and access to the contents of scientific meetings. Often written by professional staff such as society secretaries or abstractors, they sometimes replaced full manuscripts due to limited library services. In 1892, the Royal Society began requiring author-composed abstracts to reduce the burden on their secretarial staff—a practice not widely adopted until the mid-20th century.1 Nowadays, abstracts help researchers to rapidly communicate the rationale, findings, and potential impact of their work. An unclear abstract could potentially sink even the strongest work. This article explores how the modern scientific abstract has evolved into different formats, elements of a high-quality abstract, and the impact of the dawn of artificial intelligence (AI).
