TI  - Editorial: Spotlight on artificial intelligence in experimental pharmacology and drug discovery
FAU - Schöning V
FAU - Khurana A
FAU - Karolak A.
DP  - 2023
AID - 10.3389/fphar.2023.1261141 [doi]
AB  - The rapid advancement of ar1ficial intelligence (AI) has revolu1onized the field of experimental pharmacology and drug discovery. Many recent works have shed light on groundbreaking research and technological advancements in biomedical sciences and drug development. In this special issue, we bring to our readers' aMen1on six innova1ve ar1cles that demonstrate the poten1al of AI in addressing cri1cal challenges in biomedical fields. From iden1fying crucial kinases to genera1ng medica1on guides, characterizing small molecule effects, enabling early-stage drug development, and repurposing drugs for SARS-CoV-2, these ar1cles highlight the transforma1ve role and illuminate the poten1al of AI in reshaping the future of pharmaceu1cal research. They demonstrate how AI-driven approaches can address key challenges in the field and contribute to advancements in therapeu1c development and drug discovery.Gruver et al highlights the evolving understanding of pro-angiogenic growth factors and their significance in tumor angiogenesis. By presen1ng a novel approach to iden1fy cri1cal kinases involved in endothelial cell prolifera1on, the authors provide new insights for the development of comprehensive an1-angiogenic therapies. This research contributes to the ongoing baMle against cancer by expanding our knowledge of pro-angiogenic signaling pathways. In the work by Meyer et al, the focus shiUs to the regulatory medical wri1ng, which has faced increased importance due to the rising number of new drug applica1ons. The applica1on of natural language processing techniques to generate medica1on guides from exis1ng drug label informa1on offers a promising solu1on. This approach brings poten1al to modernize medical wri1ng processes, reduce manual labor, and enhance efficiency and accuracy in carrying cri1cal informa1on to healthcare professionals and pa1ents. By integra1ng heterogeneous data into a human interactome network, Mangione et al aims to understand drug behavior in biological systems via enhanced Computa1onal Analysis of Novel Drug Opportuni1es (CANDO) plaZorm applied to mental disorders and cancer metastasis. CANDO allows for integra1on of drug side effects, protein pathways, protein-protein interac1ons, protein-disease associa1ons, and the Gene Ontology, complemented with the exis1ng drug/compound, protein, and indica1on libraries. This broad characteriza1on contributes to improved therapeu1c candidate selec1on and enhances drug discovery while addressing two major challenges in therapeu1c clinical trials: efficacy and safety. Barre1 et al explores the integra1on of AI in early-stage drug development. Recognizing the significance of target evalua1on and understanding disease progression, the authors inves1gate how AI algorithms, combined with digital research environments, can effec1vely manage diversity and volume of data, and predict outcomes. This integra1on offers promising opportuni1es to accelerate drug development while ensuring data security and intellectual property protec1on. The need to fight SARS-CoV-2 is addressed by Elkashlan et al, who discusses the frequently used databases and machine learning (ML) models u1lized to target the virus. Authors discus the features and limita1ons of the databases, while the ML models, including deep learning and conven1onal ML models, are discussed from the perspec1ve of the underlying methodology, and its recent applica1ons in the prospec1ve predic1ons of SARS-CoV-2 inhibitors. This approach has the poten1al to expedite drug discovery processes, providing effec1ve solu1ons in the fight against emerging infec1ous diseases. Lastly, Rua1a et al emphasizes the cri1cal challenge of iden1fica1on of SARS-CoV-2 inhibi1on and the importance of computa1onal tools having power to integrate, process and analyze mul1ple data in a short 1me. However, the authors highlight serious risks of obtaining unrealis1c results when the u1lized models are not derived from trustworthy data and the resultant predic1ons lack valida1on through experimental evidence. This study reaffirms the fundamental principle of "garbage in, garbage out" and highlights the importance of data quality and experimental valida1on in in silico drug discovery.Jointly, these ar1cles demonstrate the poten1al of AI for addressing various aspects of pharmacology and drug discovery from iden1fying crucial kinases to restructuring medical wri1ng processes, characterizing small molecule effects, facilita1ng early-stage drug development, repurposing drugs for emerging diseases, and improving virtual screening approaches. With the emergence of AI-aided discoveries offering significant prospects in development therapeu1cs that are both safe and effec1ve, AI-driven approaches are beginning to transform pharmaceu1cal industry.
