PMID- 38887862
TI  - RETRACTED: Artificial dermatitis versus artificial intelligence: Detecting etiology with Google Lens
FAU - Ayhan E
FAU - Alagöz Y.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1111/srt.13725 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editor, Artifact dermatitis is primarily a psychiatric disorder. The diagnosis can only be made after long and detailed investigations, as they do not recognize that they are the cause of the disease presentation.1 It is often difficult to find the action or the method. Sometimes it is possible to detect it by using a camera or a mirror.2 Today, smartphones are a powerful scientific tool. However, there are studies using artificial intelligence-based smartphone applications that evaluate facial wrinkles, pores, pigmentation, acne profile.3-5 But its potential is still not fully understood. One of the companies creating new digital software that can be used in education is Google, which has created tools such as Google Lens. Google Lens is an image recognition technology based on neural networks and developed by Google. It is currently most often used to identify animal or plant species.6 Here we present a patient with artifact dermatitis whose etiology was identified with Google Lens. A 48-year-old male patient presented with a geometric shaped rash in the abdominal region. He stated that the complaint occurred 2 days ago and he did not know how it occurred. He also stated that he had a similar pattern on his left arm before. On dermatologic examination, four complete and two large incomplete linear lesions were observed in the abdominal region on the left upper side of the abdomen, which were erythematous, surrounded by a half ring, emerging from the erythematous circle in the center and reaching the circle. In addition, a brown geometric shaped patch was seen on the left shoulder which began to desquamate. Although the active lesion had a star-like structure, its etiology was not understood. The patient insisted that he did not know how the lesion occurred. However, the patient requested a rest report, suggesting that he wanted to obtain secondary gain. The patient was offered topical treatment. Then, while thinking about how the lesion was formed, the idea of scanning with Google Lens application emerged. The patient's image of the lesion was scanned with the Google Lens application on the mobile phone. No scientific data was found, but the image of our patient's lesion matched the images in the forum where some internet users discussed their lesions. Although these images had different shapes and patterns, they were very similar to our patient's lesion (Figure 1). In the forum, it was discussed that the lesions were created with a hair dryer. The entire forum was reviewed and some pictures were found showing that some lesions matched the shape of the patient's hair dryer. Our patient was then contacted by phone and told that the lesion might have been caused by a hair dryer, but the patient refused. Artifact dermatitis is a psychocutaneous syndrome. The lesions are sometimes quite masterfully formed.2 The morphologic structure is often in the form of bizarrely shaped, sharp-edged, angular or linear geometric patterns and is observed in areas easily reachable by the dominant hand.7 Diagnosis of artifact dermatitis is a difficult process. In the first step, there is uncertainty in the history taken from the patient. The only information that can be obtained is the sudden appearance of lesions without any antecedent signs and symptoms.7 Although there is no information in the literature about the etiology of the lesion in our case, a large number of patients with similar lesions discuss this issue in some forums on the internet. This condition is often associated with non-scientific paranormal phenomena. However, when the entire forum is reviewed, it is understood from the pictures shared that the image of the lesions of some patients matches the image of the hair dryer. At first glance, the geometric shape of our patient's lesion suggested artifact dermatitis, but we had no idea how it was formed. Then came the idea of scanning the patient's lesion image with Google Lens. Google Company developed Google Lens as an innovative technology that can recognize images using artificial neural networks to retrieve information about each object. Google Lens is an AI-driven technology that uses the camera on a smartphone and deep machine learning techniques to detect the object held in front of the camera lens, as well as understand it and offer actions such as translation, scanning and shopping.8 Google Lens is a unique mobile software that currently allows us to identify the species and genus of animals and plants. But it is also used in mineralogy, marketing, architecture and history.6 As far as we know, this is the first case of Google Lens being used in the medicine field. In summary, the etiology of geometric patterned lesions can be detected with Google Lens, but this application needs to be developed specifically for the medicine field. In addition, more studies are needed so that the platforms where patients with such lesions can find answers to their questions can be scientific platforms instead of forums. The authors have nothing to report. The authors declared no conflict of interest.
