TI  - Comprehensive Consideration of Ethics in AI-assisted Scientific Writing and Peer Review
FAU - Yoo JH.
DP  - 2026
AID - 10.3346/jkms.2026.41.e281 [doi]
AB  - The rise of large language models and generative artificial intelligence (AI) is driving fundamental shifts in the scholarly publishing and peer review ecosystem.As manuscript production enters an era of sophisticated machine assistance, there is an urgent need to transition from a traditional focus on preventing misconduct toward a more proactive ethical framework.We would like to propose new standards centered on transparency, accountability, and confidentiality, providing a definitive response to the challenges posed by the integration of AI in academic discourse.Regarding authorship, AI is precluded from being credited as an author; ultimate responsibility for scholarly integrity remains solely with human authors.Transparency is upheld through a tiered disclosure framework that mandates reporting based on the extent of AI involvement.Concerning peer review, while AI's potential to optimize efficiency is acknowledged, its application must be restricted to closed-loop, secure systems to prevent data breaches.Furthermore, this review highlights emerging risks such as algorithmic sycophancy and prompt injection attacks, emphasizing that final validation through human professional insight remains indispensable to the integrity of the review process.In conclusion, we present a comprehensive roadmap for revising regulations that integrates the author's duty of transparent disclosure, the reviewer's compliance with confidentiality and security, and the editor's ethical oversight.This framework seeks to position AI not as a subject of categorical prohibition, but as a progressive academic assistant anchored in human intellectual responsibility.The opinions in this Special Issue will provide a practical framework for upholding academic rigor and enhancing institutional trust within the era of AI.
