PMID- 37392428
TI  - Applications of Artificial Intelligence and Large Language Models to Plastic Surgery Research
FAU - Seth I
FAU - Bulloch G
FAU - Rozen WM.
DP  - 2023
AID - 10.1093/asj/sjad210 [doi]
AB  - This letter is a response to the commentary by Najafali et al1 examining our original work, “Evaluating Chatbot Efficacy for Answering Frequently Asked Questions in Plastic Surgery: A ChatGPT Case Study Focused on Breast Augmentation.”2 We applaud the authors for their notable statements and concur wholeheartedly with their assertions. Recent strides in artificial intelligence (AI) have showcased the impressive potential of large language models (LLMs) within surgical disciplines, including plastic surgery.3 AI has been received as a disruptive force by the medical community, although it has generated remarkable leaps and bounds in assisting with the diagnosis of medical conditions and their treatment strategies. With the inception of AI-driven LLMs, such as ChatGPT-4 (OpenAI, San Francisco, CA), BARD (Google, Mountain View, CA), and BingAI (Microsoft, Redmond, WA), we located a potential space of entry to patient care in our recent case report on ChatGPT for responses to common questions for breast reconstruction. We evaluated the response of 3 prominent LLMs on 2 questions about breast augmentation (Supplemental Figures 1 and 2, available online at www.aestheticsurgeryjournal.com). When prompted to exhibit how LLMs could inform patients on breast augmentation, ChatGPT-4 explained scenarios in which it might solve general inquiries and facilitate questions for surgeons from perioperative to postoperative stages, as did BARD at a similar but less comprehensive level, while BingAI suggested links to less reliable sources, such as phillips.com. To the second query on future advancements in breast augmentation, ChatGPT-4 responded with insights that, although already known, addressed the topic effectively. Both ChatGPT-4 and BingAI touched upon the crucial aspect of ongoing research into monitoring the safety of breast implants. In addition, a quantitative analysis based on the Flesch Reading Ease Score, the Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level, and Coleman-Liau Index scores measured the reliability of each LLM, the results of which favored ChatGPT-4 (see Supplemental Table 1, available online at www.aestheticsurgeryjournal.com). To assess the appropriateness, quality, and relevance of the information provided to patients, we employed a modified DISCERN scoring system, with ChatGPT-4 once again proving superior. Despite this, all LLMs provided information that was non-technical and superficial, which is unlikely to add to a doctor's knowledge, hence this information could be leveled more at patients to improve the delivery of healthcare information in a cost- and time-efficient manner. As Najafali et al pointed out, LLMs in plastic surgery practices have the potential to revolutionize the life of the modern surgeon by making it possible to provide around-the-clock reassurance to patients about their aesthetic and physical health concerns, a service that is currently near impossible for surgeons themselves to deliver. This would improve patient attitudes and relieve anxiety about questions that may otherwise go unexpressed and will offer an invaluable opportunity to provide better informed consent to patients at multiple time points. We believe these features may reduce the rates of legal action against surgeons or at least provide surgeons with the assurance of due diligence by offering patients information outside of the office. However, as we stand on the cusp of this transformative wave, it is critical to address the hurdles and constraints of deploying AI-driven language models in a medical context. We agree with Najafali et al that prospective studies comparing LLMs such as BARD and BingAI against healthcare specialists are crucial to evaluating the quality of LLMs, and ensuring their expert review before their integration into healthcare. To ensure their regulation, a specialized version of an LLM for healthcare may need to be developed and validated according to guidelines of a country's medical, ethical, and legal systems to ensure their widespread agreement and suitability. Our previous research confirms that ChatGPT-4 holds promise for diagnosing plastic surgical conditions and offering management guidance to healthcare professionals and patients,2,4–7 but it also consistently struggles to accurately cite sources—a limitation that undermines the reliability of the information it provides.6 In our original studies, ChatGPT-4 stressed the importance of consulting a board-certified plastic surgeon and emphasized that these tools should supplement, not replace, surgeons. It also candidly acknowledged its limitations and advised seeking professional medical advice, thereby affirming that technologies such as GPT-4 can be valuable supplements to patient education. It is unclear if this disclosure statement is a protective mechanism to cover for the inaccuracies that arise in some circumstances of its use, but the fact it is confidently inaccurate at times is particularly worrisome when nonmedical individuals seek medical advice. Now and in the future, regardless of LLM capacity, it is therefore essential to preserve doctor-patient interaction and to use GPT-4 as a closely supervised supplement rather than as a replacement for the delivery of surgical information and consent. In summary, it is necessary to conduct more in-depth examinations of LLMs such as ChatGPT, BARD, and BingAI. Acquiring detailed information on specialized surgical procedures such as breast augmentation from these models has its limitations; the results can, at times, be inconsistent or lack depth, which are current dangers for use as a source of perioperative information. Hence, it becomes vital for both researchers and clinicians to cross-verify responses with evidence-based resources and expert opinions to guarantee precision and dependability. If GPT-4 is to be expanded to serve as an adjunct for presurgical planning, decision-making, and patient education for procedures such as breast augmentation, we strongly advise the development of an independent LLM that is trained for this purpose. This article contains supplemental material located online at www.aestheticsurgeryjournal.com. The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and publication of this article. The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and publication of this article.
