PMID- 42213515
TI  - Call for papers for the themed collection on transforming pharmacy practice with artificial intelligence-enabled solutions
FAU - Hoti K
FAU - Munir F
FAU - Weidmann AE.
DP  - 2026
AID - 10.1093/ijpp/riag046 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) relates to the ability of machines to demonstrate intelligence, which essentially implies the mimicking of human cognitive functions. AI is not a new concept, but it is only in recent years that its presence and integration within health care-related digital solutions are expanding rapidly, especially as larger datasets and more powerful computing capabilities become available [1, 2]. AI-enabled solutions in health care are creating a potential to improve efficiency, personalize care, and support a more patient-centred approach, from predictive analytics in medication management to automation of routine processes. Yet, despite growing scholarly attention and AI-related prior themed collection in pharmacy practice journals, significant gaps remain in understanding how these technologies are adopted and implemented in real-world pharmacy settings. Globally, organizations such as the World Health Organization and the International Pharmaceutical Federation (FIP) have emphasized the importance of digital health and AI as tools to enhance efficiency, personalization, and patient-centred care [3, 4]. While there is a growing body of literature on AI innovations, there is limited exploration of how these solutions are being adopted in real-world pharmacy settings and how they are reshaping workforce roles, professional responsibilities, patient engagement, and impacting patient outcomes. Considering the recent FIP statements of policy regarding digital technologies which also include AI and its vision for a digitally enabled pharmaceutical workforce, and the urgent need to understand how AI can be responsibly and effectively integrated into practice, the ‘International Journal of Pharmacy Practice (IJPP)’ is launching a themed issue titled “Transforming Pharmacy Practice with Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Solutions” [3]. The goal of this collection is to bring together diverse perspectives that highlight current applications, identify barriers and facilitators of adoption, and articulate opportunities for innovation in pharmacy practice worldwide. Together, these contributions will illustrate how pharmacy practice can navigate this transformation responsibly while maximizing the potential of AI to enhance health outcomes. AI, integrated in various technologies, is enabled through a number of key techniques such as machine learning, deep learning, and natural language processing. The integration of these diverse AI-enabled technologies is promising to drive a major progress in health care, whereas various AI-based solutions are increasingly being investigated in pharmacy practice as well, with applications spanning clinical decision support, automated dispensing, medication safety, and patient engagement [5–11]. However, much of this work remains at the research or pilot stage rather than being implemented in routine clinical pharmacy practice. Demonstrating how these innovations translate into real-world settings is critical, and this call aims to highlight and advance the reporting of AI-enabled solutions that are being applied and evaluated within actual pharmacy practice environments. As these technologies mature, AI promises to transform the pharmacist’s role into one more centred on personalized medicine and proactive patient care. Beyond clinical review, AI-powered tools are supporting automated dispensing workflows and supply chain processes, minimizing errors and improving efficiency [12]. Equally important are patient-facing innovations such as mobile health applications and digital therapeutics, which leverage predictive analytics to forecast exacerbations and encourage adherence [6]. Globally, pharmacy literature has begun to reflect this momentum, documenting the rapid growth in research related to AI-powered clinical pharmacy tools and technologies, particularly since 2020 [5, 6, 12–15]. These studies emphasize that while adoption is still emerging, there is clear evidence of AI’s potential to optimize medication order review, therapeutic education, and community screening services. To meet this opportunity, pharmacy education must incorporate AI fundamentals, equipping future pharmacists with competencies in digital health, data science, and informatics. Recent years have also evidenced a rise of large language models (LLMs), such as various models of ChatGPT, Gemini, Grok, and other LLMs. These AI-based systems are neural network architectures that employ deep learning to train on billions of words derived mainly from various internet-based sources [16]. The published impact of LLMs suggests that they can serve as valuable educational supplements when implemented with appropriate guardrails, verification processes, and explicit instruction in critical evaluation [14–19]. However, significant gaps as yet remain in our understanding of its long-term impact on students’ professional development and clinical reasoning skills [14–19]. This is very important considering the growing potential for the utilization of AI in medication optimization, such as AI-based clinical decision support systems, pharmacogenomics, adherence, and adverse drug reaction prediction [20–23]. This requires the pharmacists to have the skills to correctly contextualize the AI output, analyse its accuracy in the relevant context, and use the information to improve a patient’s therapy, health, and safety. While the impact of AI in practice may span several applications, including patient-facing ones, e.g. AI-powered chatbots for self-care, adherence monitoring, and patient engagement, there are still several concerns around the ethical application and use of AI in pharmacy practice and research [24, 25]. The recently published MedinAI guidelines aim to enhance transparency and reproducibility in the reporting of AI-based studies [26, 27]. High-quality research studies are needed to show how we can use AI effectively to improve patient therapy safety across the broad spectrum of real-world pharmacist services to augment, not replace, our expertise [28]. The adoption of tailored AI solutions into our daily pharmacy and research practices is governed by several barriers and facilitators. Sufficient understanding, digital literacy, and correct systems integration, to name but a few. Furthermore, at this stage, it is also difficult to evaluate implications of AI-enabled solutions in pharmacy practice considering limited adoption and limited availability of results from their application in real-world pharmacy practice [28]. This modern technology presents a steep learning curve for all healthcare professions, but correct and effective implementation in pharmacy practice is difficult without sufficient training opportunities, irrespective of the pharmacist’s positive intentions and perception of improved efficacy. There is a growing need for structural professional development opportunities for pharmacists, guidance on systems integration in daily pharmacy practice, and regulatory clarity on legal and ethical use. This makes the reporting of good quality research on this topic so important to help us all identify effective ways to optimize the use of this technology to further our daily practice and patient care. Pharmacy practice, a term that encapsulates the research, development, formulation, distribution, access, and clinical use of medicines, presents a unique setting, but AI applications in this field must also account for challenges that extend across health care more broadly [29]. These include the potential for bias, equity issues, workforce implications, and evolving ethical framework to name a few [30, 31]. Furthermore, recent developments related to medical applications which are based on LLMs have also raised some ethical concerns. These include privacy concerns, potential leak of personal information from generated outputs of LLM-based solutions, incorrect or misleading information (i.e. so called “hallucinations”), and issues around prompting [32]. These potential implications necessitate a cautious approach, especially until more evidence is available that clarifies the different reported ethical issues [30, 31]. Nonetheless, notable work has been conducted recently in this area, such as the development of guidelines for studies in medicine and pharmacy [27]. These guidelines aim to promote transparency as well as ethical development of AI in these fields. Additionally, a checklist for assessment of ethical issues surrounding GenAI-related research has also been developed [32]. These advancements should be considered by pharmacy researchers as well as practitioners aiming to implement AI-related solutions in pharmacy practice. This will assist in mitigating against ethical concerns and therefore contribute toward ensuring a transparent and responsible development and deployment of AI-enabled solutions in pharmacy. Workforce implications also require careful consideration. The pharmacy profession has already seen a number of traditional roles replaced or augmented by technology solutions. One such familiar example is the impact of automation on dispensing activities. AI-enabled solutions have the potential to reshape the delivery of pharmacy services, enable more remote care by improving pharmacists’ reach, as well as assist pharmacists in the way they currently make clinical decisions [33]. This may allow more time and resources for pharmacists to focus more on their core pharmaceutical expertise and hence reduce their current workload around more replaceable tasks including documentation and cumbersome administrative processes. In this regard, more evidence is needed from the application of AI-related pharmacy research to ascertain the full impact of AI-enabled solutions. It is encouraging to note, however, that AI-related pharmacy research thus far does not point toward replacement of their expertise and skills [28]. Nonetheless, augmentation of their expertise and skills may still be required as AI-enabled solutions are incorporated into real-world pharmacy practice. Given that pharmacists are generally receptive to integrating digital technologies into their daily practice, adapting pharmacy curricula and expanding continuing education programs will be essential to support the anticipated enhancement of pharmacists’ skills and expertise [34, 35]. This will be necessary to better prepare pharmacists for the era of digital pharmacy and AI and therefore establish pharmacists as leaders and innovators among health professionals, especially when it comes to adoption of AI-enabled solutions in their professional practice. Advancing the integration of AI into pharmacy practice offers the potential to transform patient care, optimize workflows, and expand the pharmacist’s role in the healthcare system. Much research has been published so far around the potential of AI in addressing a number of important and relevant pharmacy practice-related areas. However, there is still a lack of evidence around their implementation and real-world adoption in pharmacy practice. This prompts a number of important questions such as: How can pharmacy education and curricula evolve to ensure future practitioners are equipped with necessary AI literacy, clinical judgment, and digital fluency? What models of implementation can sustainably integrate AI into everyday practice across diverse healthcare systems? How can the results of AI-related research be translated into pharmacy practice? What are the key outcomes that AI-enabled solutions are delivering when implemented in real-world pharmacy practice and what are the challenges that are being encountered by pharmacists? Addressing these questions will be critical to the further development and implementation of AI solutions in pharmacy practice and ensuring that AI adoption remains patient-centred, ethically grounded, and globally relevant. Therefore, researchers, educators, and practitioners worldwide are invited to submit manuscripts for this themed collection. A key emphasis will be placed on submissions reporting the experiences and outcomes from the implementation of AI-enabled solutions in pharmacy practice. By contributing empirical studies, innovative practices, and educational approaches, authors will help build a global repository of evidence on AI in pharmacy, creating a shared foundation to guide the sustainable innovation and shape the future of the profession. Kreshnik Hoti (Conceptualization, Writing—original draft, Writing—review & editing), Faria Munir (Conceptualization, Writing—original draft, Writing—review & editing), and Anita Elaine Weidmann (Conceptualization, Writing—original draft, Writing—review & editing) None declared. None declared.
