PMID- 40851220
TI  - Empowering the next generation: integrating artificial intelligence education into medical training
FAU - Tan JA
FAU - Ng IKS
FAU - Hong A
FAU - Teo DB
FAU - Tan LF.
DP  - 2026
AID - 10.1093/postmj/qgaf144 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly transforming the practice of medicine [1, 2]. From diagnostic imaging [3], predictive analytics [4] to patient triage [5] and clinical documentation, AI has become an indispensable part of modern healthcare by showing promise in delivering high-quality clinical care with greater efficiency and lower costs. As this technological shift accelerates, medical education must surely evolve accordingly [2, 6], so that we produce future-ready graduates who are able to work alongside AI tools—not only as end-users but also as collaborators in developing, integrating, and refining these technologies within clinical workflows. Broadly, this includes leveraging AI for diagnostic support, personalized decision-making, patient communication, and therapeutic interventions [7]. On the contrary, having a limited and rudimentary understanding of AI’s capabilities, limitations, and ethical considerations may lead to inappropriate reliance, misuse, or missed opportunities for enhanced care. Such gaps in knowledge could ultimately compromise patient safety, undermine clinical judgment, and impede healthcare transformation. In 2024, a global, multicenter cross-sectional study was performed to assess the attitudes and readiness of healthcare students (predominantly, medical students) in navigating AI-centric clinical practice [8]. While the authors found that most students reported positive attitudes towards the use of AI technologies in healthcare, most students felt that they lacked AI-related knowledge, were unprepared to use AI in their future practice, and had inadequate AI-related training in their medical curriculum. Thus, there is clearly an urgent need to equip healthcare staff and trainees with the necessary foundational knowledge in AI and its ethical, practical, and clinical implications through carefully curated training pedagogies in an AI-ready medical educational landscape [9].
