PMID- 32187842
TI  - Advances in Quantitative Bioimage Analysis for Proteins and Cells
FAU - Figge MT.
DP  - 2020
AID - 10.1002/cyto.a.24000 [doi]
AB  - The current issue of Cytometry Part A includes a collection of six articles of mixed types—that is, one review, three original articles, one brief report, and one technical note—that are thematically focused on advanced methods for quantitative bioimage analysis. These methods are applied to a highly diverse spectrum of biological systems ranging from studying protein stability and assessing cell cycle staging at the molecular level to the characterization and classification of cells in the context of leukemia, migratory behavior of single stem cells, and their formation into cellular spheroids. The wide range of applications gives a clear impression of how indispensable quantitative bioimage analysis has become in recent years for the automated, objective, and precise evaluation of biological processes in microscopic image data. It is therefore highly desirable that information on the application of new methods, as well as of already known methods, in new fields of work is made available to a broad audience of scientists. The present collection of articles is intended to contribute to this goal and represents a timely update of earlier Special Issues on this topic in Cytometry Part A 1, 2. The review article by Shifat-E-Rabbi et al. 3 provides a comparative overview on methods for cell image classification. In particular, three main classification approaches are discussed: 1. feature extraction and machine learning, 2. neural networks, and 3. transport-based morphometry. These different applications of cell image classification are explored and compared for accuracy of results and applicability in practice using publicly available datasets from various areas, for example, drug discovery, digital pathology, and genetic screening. The empirical overview closes with some helpful suggestions on how to solve the problem of choosing the most appropriate method for a given dataset. In addition, the authors provide an open-source cell image classification toolbox of the various methods to enable quantitative comparison for specific data of users. Deep learning performed by neural networks is also at the heart of the brief report by Doan et al.4, which is devoted to the aim of improving precision medicine. Using the example of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) as the most common childhood cancer, the authors perform a rigorous analysis on bone marrow samples labeled with an ALL-discriminating antibody combination. Applying imaging flow cytometry on these samples, it is convincingly demonstrated that, by incrementally ignoring fluorescent markers and ultimately using only features extracted from bright-field and dark-field cell images, a neural network with architecture of type ResNet50 5 is still able to identify ALL cells at a high accuracy rate of >88%. These results provide a concrete example of a deep learning application at the single cell level, enabling personalized medicine. The authors stress that, while the focus in their study is on the cancer type ALL, residual disease monitoring is a key response biomarker in all chronic and acute leukemias and multiple myeloma, as well as in monitoring circulating tumor cells 6. It can be expected that the proposed antibody-free, single-cell method is not only cheap and quick but could be performed by a simple, laser-free cytometer, allowing automated point-of-care testing. Monitoring cells and their dynamic behavior is of broad interest and is also the topic of the original article by Jiang et al. 7. The authors evaluate the feasibility of quantitative bioimage analysis for monitoring stem cell quality during in vitro culture and explore the passaging effects on stem cell migration. An image-based analytical tool using cell tracking, cytometric analyses, and gating with time-lapse microscopy was developed to characterize and quantify the migratory behavior of human mesenchymal stem cells. The customized software tool is based on the commercial framework MATLAB making use of a previously developed segmentation and tracking framework for nonrigid cells in phase-contrast microscopy 8. Here, the software tool is extended to detect and track different cell patterns in video data, as well as to quantify the dynamic behavior during cell clustering. Agreement of the authors' findings with biochemical reports regarding the passaging effects on stem cell migratory behavior suggests that the developed imaging method is capable of monitoring quality of the cell culture effectively. The quantitative results show that the human mesenchymal stem cells at an early passage are more likely to move individually rather than as aggregates. With subsequent passages, they tend to aggregate and exhibit 3D spheroid formations that can also be tracked as a whole. The authors suggest that the focus of future studies will be on analyzing the dynamic behavior of individual cells within cell aggregates. The quantification of cell properties is usually carried out by flow cytometry. The original article by Frölich et al. 9 addresses the issue of flow cytometry being relatively low in resolution and unable to measure phenotypic changes or processes occurring in subcellular compartments. The authors integrate automated microscopy with a bioimage analysis pipeline to quantify multiple fluorescent markers from specific subnuclear regions throughout a population of cells. The pipeline is based on the commercial framework HCS Studio™ Cell Analysis Software 10, which applies customized machine learning algorithms to automatically identify and segment cells into defined regions of interest based on fluorescent markers, to measure marker intensities, and to compute marker colocalizations in specific segmented regions. While it is shown that the analysis pipeline can be used to assess the formation of a variety of different molecular complexes with functional relevance in the cell cycle or in apoptotic processes, it needs to be added that, in the current setup, this is realized at the costs of purchasing commercial software. Going beyond standard flow cytometry approaches, the technical note by Adachi et al. 11 addresses technological challenges of imaging flow cytometry, such as the ability to acquire and analyze high-content image data from many single cells at high speed in real time. A new approach for morphological cell analysis builds on a machine learning-integrated method that directly analyzes compressed imaging signals of cells without image production, under the exciting name of Ghost cytometry 12. The authors extend their previously performed classification of two cell line samples exhibiting subtle differences in their two-dimensional fluorescence images that were not easily distinguishable by the human eye. In particular, they address the question of whether the direct analysis of the compressed signals could be based on nonmorphological information, such as the speed of cells in flow, which may be coupled with morphological information such as the cell size. However, they demonstrate that cell classification by Ghost cytometry is 1. superior to using the height of forward-scattering and side-scattering signals of a conventional flow cytometer and 2. based on the difference in cellular structures. It can therefore be concluded that classification using the Ghost cytometry is indeed based on the image information of cells. Quantitative bioimage analysis can be performed at all kinds of time and length scales. Going from the micrometer to the nanometer range, the original article by Hickman et al. 13 reports about a high-content screening pipeline that combines automated confocal microscopy and image analysis to score half-lives of nanometer-sized proteins as a function of fluorescence intensity and distribution. The typical protein half-life in cells is estimated to be several tens of hours; however, some proteins possess much shorter half-lives in the order of 1 h or less. Fast-turnover proteins are key regulators that control several essential cellular processes and also often contribute to disease conditions. To advance the current understanding of protein-turnover mechanisms in mammalian cells, the authors address the need for a highly sensitive, specific, rapid, and informative assay. Here, they design and implement a high-content screening pipeline—based on the commercial image analysis software Acapella 14—to demonstrate, in a proof-of-concept analysis, the successful identification of six known and two novel regulators of short half-life proteins from a collection of several hundreds of kinase inhibitors. These achievements are promising for large-scale screening for regulators of short half-life proteins. Being aware that the field of quantitative bioimage analysis is advancing fast, we hope that the present compilation of articles gives the reader some insight into ongoing developments in quantitative bioimage analysis. New challenges and advancements can be expected in the near future, for example, with regard to the quantification of biological processes that are monitored by multimodal imaging techniques. Moreover, it should be mentioned that bioimage analysis is part of an even broader field, the field of Image-based Systems Biology 2, 15. The latter research field combines automated quantification of image data with mathematical modeling by providing a highly valuable source of quantitative values for model parameters. Simulations of mathematical models on the computer can generate predictions that may be further investigated in subsequent experiments and, through that, keep the systems biology cycle between experiment and theory going. In fact, we ourselves look forward to reporting on the next exciting developments in the future!
