AB  - Transthyretin (ATTR) cardiac amyloidosis is an increasingly recognised disease, especially among older patients with aortic stenosis and heart failure.1 Although the prognosis of patients with ATTR-cardiac amyloidosis is poor without treatment,1 early initiation of targeted therapy reduces all-cause mortality and cardiovascular-related hospitalisations.2 Bone scintigraphy imaging has emerged as a highly accurate method for identifying patients with ATTR-cardiac amyloidosis. However, early identification of patients with ATTR-cardiac amyloidosis remains a clinical challenge, with a median time from symptom onset to diagnosis of 2 years.
