PMID- 36727333
TI  - Commentary: Artificial intelligence and machine learning in ocular oncology: Retinoblastoma
FAU - Gopal L.
DP  - 2023
AID - 10.4103/ijo.IJO_2269_22 [doi]
AB  - This issue of Indian journal of ophthalmology has an article entitled ‘Artificial intelligence and machine learning in ocular oncology: Retinoblastoma’.[1] Artificial intelligence (AI) has become the new buzz word with its applications spreading into diverse specialties – medical and non-medical. In ophthalmology, AI has been explored in diabetic retinopathy, age-related macular degeneration, glaucoma, pathological myopia, and retinopathy of pre-maturity (ROP). For diabetic retinopathy, a commercially available program by a startup company ‘AEYE Health’ has been approved recently by Food and Drug Administration in USA. Contrary to lay person’s understanding, AI is not a tool meant to replace human intelligence. Specifically, AI is used to enhance the human competence, reduce the human errors, reduce the burden of mundane work, and increase the reach of medical care to even remote places. AI can be used at its basic level to perform simple tasks such as feature extraction and identifying pathology from photographs. At a more advanced level, ‘machine learning’ describes computer programs that learn without being explicitly programmed, whereas ‘deep learning’ is a step further in the sense that the programmer does not even describe the features to be looked for in the photographs. The program effectively learns on its own and correctly identifies the diagnosis of interest, although the path it took in this process may remain unknown to us (like a black box). In the current article, the authors share their explorative experience of use of AI in the field of ocular oncology, specifically retinoblastoma. The authors used AI to identify optic disc, individual tumors (including dimensions and distance from the optic disc), and vitreous seeds. It is obvious that ‘retinoblastoma’ is a different ball game compared to diabetic retinopathy, glaucoma, or ROP with reference to applicability of AI. In this context, one must place the role of AI in the correct perspective in the overall management of ‘Retinoblastoma’ in both the clinic and the society. The following objectives/issues come up for discussion: Can AI screen fundus photos and detect those with retinoblastoma:The work by the authors of the present article proves that this is possible with great sensitivity and specificity, especially for the earlier stages of the disease (Group A had 100% sensitivity, specificity, and positive and negative predictive values).Where can this be potentially utilized:If screening programs are in place like diabetic retinopathy wherein non-medical personnel can capture images of fundus, the program can quickly identify those with suspect RB and ensure their prompt examination by trained ophthalmologists. However, for this to succeed, there needs to be a system in place to capture fundus images of children at risk. Although universal eye screening of newborn permits collection of these images,[2] a one-time evaluation immediately after birth is obviously not enough.Considering the low community level incidence of retinoblastoma, programs to collect routine fundus images of the population of 0–14 years is neither practicable nor cost-effective (Indian studies have shown a pooled age standardized incidence of 4.4 per million).[3]At this point of time, the only place where this can probably be utilized is in screening siblings of patients with RB (a higher-risk group than general population).Is a fundus photograph enough to exclude RB:Compared to diabetic retinopathy and ROP where a wide-angle fundus photograph can effectively diagnose or rule out sight-threatening disease, even a wide-angle fundus photograph cannot rule out the possibility of a small retinoblastoma tumor hidden near ora serrata unless fundus photographs are possible to be taken with 360-degree indentation of the peripheral fundus (not a possibility outside the clinic setting and without general anesthesia).Again, considering the small number of children in this category that need periodic evaluation, a direct examination by a trained specialist under anesthesia is feasible and with no possibility of missing a life-threatening condition because the periphery can be examined adequately.Can AI be used to refine the diagnosis of RB:It would be exciting to see if AI can help in differentiating RB from mimicking conditions – a not uncommon clinical dilemma one faces. However, the present study does not address this question. From the practical utility of AI, this would be an area worth exploring if one can feed information from fundus photos, ultrasound, magnetic resonance imaging, and clinical numerical data.Can AI be used to identify efficacy of treatment:This would perhaps be an immediately useful application of AI in the management of RB. Often the improvement with treatment (such as chemotherapy/laser) and so on is dramatic, and response can be seen and concluded upon even without referring to pre-treatment photographs. However, on occasions, the tumor may show slow response and clinical evaluation alone is not enough. One relies on careful comparison with photos taken in previous visits to gauge the efficacy of treatment. In this respect, AI can be a valuable tool.It can potentially judgea) the reduction in size of tumor with greater precisionb) the reduction in number and size of vitreous seeds. In summary, the value of AI in the management of retinoblastoma appears to be not so much in detection of tumor in the society (a la diabetic retinopathy, ROP) but perhaps in the management of diagnosed cases of retinoblastoma to evaluate efficacy of treatment. The role in diagnosing RB from mimicking conditions needs to be explored.
