PMID- 37967157
TI  - Addressing academic dishonesty caused by artificial intelligence tools
FAU - Cordero DA Jr.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/pubmed/fdad233 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editor, I read with interest the recent article published in this journal when Padillah highlighted the practice of ‘ghostwriting’, an emerging form of academic dishonesty facilitated by ChatGPT. Ghostwriting is basically the act of hiring someone else, or in the current case, using artificial intelligence (AI) like ChatGPT to produce written academic work on behalf of a student.1 From my standpoint as an educator, facing the reality that various AI-related issues can incapacitate students from using their intellectual skills is alarming. Ghostwriting is only one of the future potential dangers of AI, and there will surely be more to come that will challenge students' academic integrity and moral values. The big question is, how can the academe address the inappropriate use and abuse of AI-powered tools to not totally ‘replace’ or ‘destroy’ natural human intelligence? The easiest way to battle AI tools in schools is simply to ban the use of them, which requires strict monitoring. However, this may not be effective since some students will still find their way to disobey this policy, especially if unsupervised. It is important to note that AI still originated from human intelligence. Without humans, there will be no AI. Thus, humans can create an antidote for his/her own ‘abused creation.’ Since AI is a technology-powered tool, humans can also create technology-based counter tools that will determine the originality or extent of what is copied by the students by putting indelible marks. It is good that just recently, the makers of ChatGPT are themselves exploring ‘watermarking' technology to indicate when a document has been generated by the software.2 Also, GPTZero was invented by Edward Tian, a technology that determines if a text was written by a human or a bot with high accuracy.3 These sample AI-detection tools can be used by educators with optimum training. On the other hand, another way to address the issue is a well-planned integration of AI tools into the teaching pedagogy. Since we aim to protect the general well-being of students, especially their mental and moral aspects, limiting their academic freedom may not be the best solution. Therefore, educators need to integrate AI tools into the curriculum and prepare learning activities that will use the technology in appropriate topics, methods and timelines. This means that as early as the start of the course, the syllabus will already reflect the times at which topics and when these AI tools are allowed to be used. The educator should ensure that a balanced division between traditional and AI-moderated research is incorporated. All these policies should be explained to the students during the course orientation. Lastly, it may sound cliché, but the educator’s habitual reminder to motivate students to uphold the moral values of honesty and integrity is still a must.
