PMID- 38271410
TI  - Role of artificial intelligence in cornea practice
FAU - Agarwal S.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.4103/IJO.IJO_61_24 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is a rapidly evolving field that employs computer algorithms to mimic human intelligence. It can analyze large amounts of data from various sources, such as clinical records, imaging modalities, genetic tests, and patient-reported outcomes, and generate meaningful insights that can help clinicians make better decisions. With the extensive use of digital imaging and objective metrics in ophthalmology, AI integrates seamlessly.[1] AI can be further classified into subfields, such as machine learning (ML), which is the process of learning from data without explicit programming, and deep learning (DL), which is a subset of ML that uses multiple layers of artificial neural networks to learn from complex and high-dimensional data.[2] In this editorial, I will highlight some of the current and future applications of AI in cornea along with the challenges and opportunities for its clinical implementation. Microbial Keratitis Predicting the causative organism in infective keratitis clinically has always been a challenge with it being accurate in only 60–65%. Low culture yields and need for well-trained microbiologists have been a concern too, making accurate diagnosis difficult.[3] Slit lamp photographs are routinely used to monitor in addition to documenting the clinical findings. DL-based algorithms using slit lamp photographs have been developed to differentiate infective from non-infective keratitis and also fungal from bacterial with a specificity of 76.5% for bacterial and 100% for fungal, and accuracy of 90.7%, which is much higher than the clinical predictability.[4,5] Neural networks using in vivo confocal microscopy (IVCM) specially for fungal keratitis are also being explored.[5] Dry Eye Disease (DED) DED is one of the most common multifactorial ocular surface diseases, which significantly impairs the quality of life. Further, the sign-symptom discord makes the diagnosis and management challenging. With the availability of new imaging modalities, such as infrared meibography, tear interferometry, non-invasive tear film measurements, IVCM, and anterior segment optical coherence tomography (AS-OCT), visualization and objective assessment of tear film and various other parameters have become possible, albeit their interpretation could be biased, based on the examiner’s judgment and experience. To overcome these challenges, AI has been integrated to aid in the diagnosis and management of DED. Nam et al. developed a model based on ML and neural network which revealed females, refractive or cataract surgery, psychological stress, and age 54–66 years as important risk factors for DED.[6] To aid in quantitative assessment of dry eyes, algorithms based on videos recording the breakup time or blinks have been developed.[7-9] Analysis of meibography images using AI has enabled a more rapid and accurate quantitative analysis of meibomian gland changes.[10,11] Changes in corneal nerves, dendritic cells, and even meibomian glands imaged using IVCM have also been reported by AI-based algorithms in addition to DL-based segmentation of tear meniscus using AS-OCT images, indicating that AI can be a powerful tool for diagnosis of DED. Utilizing AI for the analysis of data obtained from the aforementioned equipment can enhance the precision and accuracy of test assessments, thereby contributing to improved management.[12] Keratoconus Imaging modalities, such as corneal tomography, topography, and AS-OCT, have been in use for some time now in diagnosing corneal ectasia. Diagnosing forme fruste or early keratoconus is still a challenge specially in preoperative refractive surgeries, and AI-based algorithms, such as KeratoDetect and Ectasia Status Index, are now proving useful, especially in such cases.[12,13] Most algorithms were earlier based on the color-coded maps; however, recently Cao et al.[14] added clinical parameters and reported random forest method to have the highest area under the curve (AUC) (0.97) for detecting subclinical keratoconus. In addition, it is now being explored to identify susceptibility genes in keratoconus.[12] Corneal Dystrophies AI algorithm based on high-definition AS-OCT images has been 99% specific and 98% sensitive in differentiating normal cornea from early or late stages of Fuchs endothelial corneal dystrophy [FECD]. It can detect, segment, and measure features of FECD, such as corneal thickness, endothelial cell density, guttae count, and corneal haze, from specular microscopy or AS-OCT images as well predict the need and timing of endothelial keratoplasty, by assessing the progression and risk factors of the disease.[15] Corneal Grafts Analysis of specular microscopy images has helped develop AI-based algorithms to help differentiate between normal and pathological endothelium, detect graft detachments, and predict need for rebubbling in Descemet’s membrane endothelial keratoplasty (DMEK).[16,17] Challenges and Limitations AI models require large and diverse datasets to train and validate their performance. However, there is a lack of standardized and harmonized data collection, annotation, and sharing protocols in ophthalmology, especially for corneal diseases. There is also a need to ensure the quality, validity, and representativeness of the data used for AI models, as well as the ethical and legal aspects of data privacy and security.[1,12] Standardization of imaging technique is difficult in corneal conditions because of variability in contrast, magnification, angle, and width of the slit beam. Unless the raw data fed into the system is of a high quality, the output can never be reliable. The cost of the machines, technical challenge of image acquisition, and variability of images acquired between two machines based on the same technology further make it difficult. AI research also requires standardization of reporting and conducting studies, and recently, AI guidelines, such as CONSORT-AI, SPIRIT-AI, and STARD-AI, have been developed.[18] There is a need for more collaboration and communication among the AI developers, clinicians, patients, and regulators, to ensure that the AI algorithms are aligned with the clinical needs, expectations, and values of the stakeholders. In conclusion, AI is a promising and exciting field that has the potential to revolutionize various aspects of diagnosing and managing corneal disorders. However, there are also some challenges and opportunities that need to be addressed before AI can be fully realized and implemented in clinical practice. As we march toward the era of digital health, AI can be a powerful tool to help improve the quality and efficiency of eye care, as well as the satisfaction and well-being of the patients.
