PMID- 38997219
TI  - Generative artificial intelligence in dermatology: Recommendations for future studies evaluating the clinical knowledge of models
FAU - Pillai J
FAU - Li B.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1111/srt.13854 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editor, Huang et al. recently published two reports evaluating the clinical knowledge of OpenAI's ChatGPT, a popular large language model (LLM), in identifying systemic lupus erythematosus and psoriasis.1, 2 The authors queried 14 common questions to the model and assessed results through expert evaluations and readability scores. Although the motivation of the authors for evaluating these emerging technologies was novel, there are major limitations of the studies that should be noted. The purpose of this letter is to define these susceptibilities and provide recommendations on methods for future studies evaluating the aptitude of LLMs in clinical knowledge of dermatological conditions. Firstly, authors should make every effort to maximize interpretability of model outputs. Without any follow-up inquiries, it is unclear if a LLM misunderstood a prompt given by the authors, a phenomenon known as goal misgeneralization,3 and can therefore yield moderate results, as explained in the studies. Alternatively, a LLM may experience hallucinations, providing plausible but factually incorrect information.4 Ultimately, these two explanations must be noted within future studies evaluating the clinical knowledge of LLMs in dermatology, as they may help clinicians make informed decisions about which outputs to trust. In Table 1, a three-question template is provided, where the initial query assesses for the accuracy of the response, the second asks for misgeneralization, and the third highlights potential LLM hallucination. By having a stringent experimental design for questions, researchers can thoroughly understand the mechanisms of these models and their outputs. Secondly, authors should clearly define the angle from which the model is being evaluated to ensure straightforward experimental replication and validation. For instance, context windows of LLMs allow them to recall aspects of prior user inputs within the same conversation, influencing future outputs. This potential source of variability can be minimized by prompting the model with questions in separate conversations, limiting the context window to a single question. In addition, the ability of a model to perform well varies drastically given different prompts and must be noted. For instance, informing the model of its role could potentially change its performance and should be clearly articulated by future studies. An example of this includes inserting text preceding the prompt such as “You are an experienced medical practitioner in a dermatology clinic”. Lastly, we agree with Daungsupawong et al. on the benefit of using a wider range of questions for comprehensive model evaluations.5 We also encourage further analysis of responses, such as including a control group of physicians for direct comparison with the model. Contrary to Daungsupawong et al., we believe that the biases of expert evaluation are likely minimal because of the moderate number of experts (n = 5) in these prior studies. Furthermore, more models should be evaluated (e.g., GPT-4, GPT-4o, Claude, Llama) to ensure experiments more broadly reflect the capabilities of LLMs. Overall, we encourage future studies exploring the clinical knowledge of LLMs in dermatology to (1) scrutinize model reasoning, (2) clearly articulate the angle of evaluation, and (3) ensure thorough and rigorous experimental design. The authors have nothing to report. Data sharing is not applicable to this article as no new data were created or analyzed in this study.
