PMID- 38723587
TI  - Widening the scope of artificial intelligence applications in dermatology
FAU - Matin RN.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/ced/llae174 [doi]
AB  - I am delighted to present this special issue that is focused on artificial intelligence (AI) applied to dermatology. With the UK government predicted to imminently confirm a further £100 million investment to accelerate AI use in healthcare1 this special issue is timely. It brings together invited submissions on a range of topics under the broad umbrella of AI ranging from diagnostic accuracy studies using clinical and histopathology images to prognostic models for skin cancer to potential use cases for large language models (LLMs). We also have a number of Letters emphasizing this is an exciting topic that is of great interest to Clinical and Experimental Dermatology readers. To date, there has been significant interest in developing AI diagnostic tools in the skin cancer pathway but numerous challenges have prevented large-scale deployment. Innovating the skin cancer pathway will likely involve incorporating different AI technologies at various stages throughout the pathway with the aims to improve cancer prevention, to optimize early cancer diagnosis and to personalize prognostication and management. In this issue, Carse et al.2 report classification of real-world macroscopic clinical images in the primary–secondary care interface using transfer learning. They demonstrate the significant value of using real-world clinical data and show how to obtain satisfactory performance using challenging local data with limited diagnostic labelling. These findings provide further insight into how the deployment of diagnostic systems at scale could be achieved. Wen et al.3 discuss the challenges of creating clinically valuable skin cancer datasets used to test, train and validate AI technologies and propose strategies that could improve the quality and utility of these datasets. Developing skin disease datasets remains a core barrier to exploiting the advances we have observed with AI technologies and understanding the limitations of current datasets can inform future work. Increasingly digitization of pathology slides is becoming standard practice and combining AI with pathology is an emerging field.4 In this issue, Murphree et al.5 present a proof-of-concept study demonstrating automated detection of basal cell carcinoma from frozen tissue shave biopsies that has potential utility for the Mohs surgeon. They used 287 whole-slide images of frozen sections that were annotated to train a machine learning pipeline demonstrating sensitivity and specificity of 0.72 and 0.88, respectively, showing promise for an intended use that could address a clear unmet need. Pérez-Baena et al.6 propose a use of AI in histopathology outside the usual goal of clinical classification, demonstrating how AI can potentially enhance risk stratification of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma beyond the current staging systems using cellular morphometric biomarkers. We anticipate that more applications of AI beyond the intention of diagnostics are likely to emerge in the future as there is a growing unmet need to personalize patient care. Given the dynamic and rapidly advancing field of LLMs, however, it is unsurprising that a significant proportion of articles submitted for this special issue are dedicated to exploring generative AI use cases. LLMs are generative pretrained transformer (GPT) models that produce text resembling human writing (hence also referred to as AI chatbots) allowing users to interact with the model as if they are communicating with another human. The third iteration of an LLM from OpenAI, ChatGPT, was considered to be the fastest growing app and was a game changer leading to widespread adoption of LLMs by the general public. ChatGPT-3.5 has since been superseded by ChatGPT-4.0, in terms of its ability to generate more complex human-like responses, having been trained on larger datasets including images, but the latter is only available to paid subscribers. Other LLMs are also now publicly available for use, including Google’s Gemini (until recently known as Bard) and have been explored by contributing authors in this issue. Ko et al.7 surveyed Argentinian dermatologists about ChatGPT use and reported that 35% of responders (89/257; overall survey response rate 13%) had used ChatGPT with 31% (28/89) using it weekly/more frequently. This compares with 52% (45/87) of responders in a US survey that used LLMs daily/weekly.8 Anyone who has used ChatGPT will be aware that it requires entering prompts/text instructions in the form of questions/commands. Creating effective prompts can be challenging and single word substitutions can entirely change the response received. The potential use cases appear to be limitless and continue to grow as the technology advances. Using current general purpose LLMs such as ChatGPT and Google’s Gemini, however, cannot be assured to be safe when used in healthcare. They are prone to errors or ‘hallucinations’ and even lack the ability to do easy tasks such as basic arithmetic. They are skilled at producing creative outputs rather than necessarily providing factual information and therefore they are able to produce very reasonable sounding misinformation to the non-expert. I highlight an example of this in Table 1 when I asked ChatGPT about patient-reported outcomes (PROMs) for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma and it provided a fictitious PROM supported by sources that did not exist.9 Despite being very polite and providing very plausible responses, to someone who does not have personal expertise in this area, the PROM that was suggested appeared to be a very credible tool for use. For many publicly available general LLMs such as ChatGPT, it is unclear what data were used to develop, train and validate the models (and if they are indeed accurate and up to date) and anyone who has used it will be familiar with my experience (Table 1) that when asked to provide references or source of data supporting its claims, responses are frequently fictional. Medical students and trainee dermatologists who rely solely on these tools risk learning inaccurate, and potentially unsafe, medical/dermatological information. Finally, LLMs have been shown to perpetuate harmful race-based biases leading to potential discrimination if they are used without adequate oversight.10 Example of ‘hallucinations’ and the need for expertise when using ChatGPT as a search tool9 cSCC, cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. Example of ‘hallucinations’ and the need for expertise when using ChatGPT as a search tool9 cSCC, cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma. In this special issue, three groups assessed the accuracy of LLMs answering dermatology speciality examination questions summarized in Table 2.11–13 All questions posed were closed, single answer multiple-choice questions and all three studies demonstrated that most LLMs can pass specialty examinations, accepting of course that these questions do not reflect real-life clinical practice situations and scoring highly in the specialty exam does not necessarily correlate with clinical competence as judged by the public. Summary of accuracy of large language models tested on dermatology specialty examination questions NR, not reported; NT, not tested; SCE, Specialty Certificate Exam. aAutumn 2016 questions; bSpring 2017 questions; cAutumn 2017 questions. Summary of accuracy of large language models tested on dermatology specialty examination questions NR, not reported; NT, not tested; SCE, Specialty Certificate Exam. aAutumn 2016 questions; bSpring 2017 questions; cAutumn 2017 questions. LLMs have been shown to perform similarly across medical school/board exams and in other specialties with a recent study demonstrating accuracy in answering pathology examination questions14 where ChatGPT-4 achieved a 100% (n = 86) accuracy rate in questions when they included clinical context, surpassing Bard’s (now known as Gemini) 87% (n = 75). Without any additional clinical context, the accuracy of both AI tools was significantly lower: ChatGPT-4 at 52% (n = 45) and Bard at 38% (n = 33). Ramachandran et al.15 highlight that current dermatology specialty exams may not accurately reflect the ability to make good clinical decisions, proposing that natural language processing (NLP) could, however, assist in developing more accurate, objective and personalized assessment methods. The scope of use of NLP, in particular natural language understanding to support dermatologists (for example through administrative support)16 has not been widely studied as there has been a major focus historically on developing machine learning techniques to assess skin lesion images. Exploring the wide range of applications for NLP technologies in dermatology are needed. To date, current LLMs used for diagnostic purposes as a medical device show low diagnostic accuracy as many have not been developed, trained, tested and validated using clinical (preferably prospectively collected real-life) data, and have not been designed with a specific intended clinical indication/use case. Using ChatGPT-4.0, Stoneham et al.17 entered clinical and examination findings retrospectively for 36 patients referred to a dermatology emergency clinic and demonstrated diagnostic accuracy of 39% or 56% dependent on the level of specialty expertise entering the clinical data compared with diagnostic accuracy of 36% among nonspecialists and 83% among dermatologists. Ahuja et al.18 also used ChatGPT-4.0 and demonstrated poor diagnostic accuracy for 45 publicly available skin condition images. To date, no clinical LLMs have received the appropriate risk level regulatory approval for the purposes of use as a diagnostic medical device by UK Notified Bodies and the MHRA (Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency) and therefore should not be used for this intended use. Indeed, there has been debate as to whether new regulation approaches are necessary to minimize safety and legal risks to patients and clinicians. In the short term, however, LLMs are not necessarily compliant with general data protection regulation or Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act data protection laws and pose considerable cybersecurity risks.19 Clinicians and the public should therefore avoid entering personal identifiable or protected health data. Clinicians increasingly are using LLMs to offload repetitive tasks and in the USA it has been incorporated into electronic medical record platforms like Epic to provide administrative support.20 Although it is plausible that LLMs could augment or replace commonly used medical resources such as UpToDate or DermNet, using them for general medical searches and queries, as aptly demonstrated in the vulval dermatosis use case reported by Tummon et al.21 in this issue, could harm patients and risk litigation for inexperienced doctors who may rely on plausible sounding misinformation to make clinical decisions.22 Even as a search engine, LLMs are prone to misinformation, and there is a legal risk if a clinician relies on information from an unregulated medical device to inform clinical decisions. General search engines such as Google are protected under Section 230 of the 1996 Communications Decency Act23 as the content provided is from third parties, who have their own individual liability. Many LLMs such as ChatGPT do not disclose their sources and therefore there is no way of checking if the summarized information provided is factually correct unless you are a subject matter expert. Relying on LLMs in medicine risks violating professional standards, physician codes of conduct and patient data protection laws.22 With ongoing and potentially worsening challenges accessing healthcare, it is not surprising that the public will turn to LLMs as a medical resource. Reynolds et al.24 compared the quality of responses from ChatGPT and physicians to patient queries and showed that ChatGPT was no match for human qualities such as readability and empathy and certainly not ready to replace a clinician. Clinicians have a duty of care to patients to advise them that LLMs are largely unregulated for healthcare uses, are potentially misleading, risking their safety, and should highlight that they lack the compassion, trust and confidentiality that clinicians can provide. Determining the optimal use cases for LLMs in dermatology is a continuing challenge. Building, testing, validating and then gaining regulatory approval for diagnostic medical devices is a protracted process and it is likely that LLM technology will continue to advance significantly before these are realized. Institutions in the USA, for example, are beginning to explore and evaluate deployment of LLMs for areas other than direct clinical care, such as basic/clinical research and operational processes,25 tackling these ethical, legal, technical and regulatory challenges posed. In dermatology, the use case beyond diagnostics is also being imagined with a recent publication showing an image–text foundation model called MONET (Medical cONcept rETriever) developed for medical concept annotation.26 These foundation models developed with a specific intention enables AI transparency, which in turn builds trust among patients and clinicians. As these technologies continue to evolve to provide immense potential to improve skin disease outcomes, the wide-ranging contribution of authors to this special issue prove that there is an ever-increasing enthusiasm from the dermatology community to work with the AI community and the public to co-develop, evaluate and adopt LLMs in dermatology. This editorial received no specific grant from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Not applicable. Not applicable.
