PMID- 37973395
TI  - The evolving nature of artificial intelligence: role in public health and health promotion
FAU - Ayenigbara IO.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/pubmed/fdad240 [doi]
AB  - Dear editor, In response to a recent article published in this journal on artificial intelligence (AI) and public health.1 I am fully in support of the writer's assertions, and consequently, I highlighted in this article the numerous beneficial roles of AI in public health and to ensure equitable health promotion globally, particularly in low- and middle-income countries. AI, and the recent development of ChatGPT (openAI, San Francisco, CA, USA), is rapidly evolving in different sectors around the world, including public health. In public health, the introduction of AI offers novel ways that will ensure the health promotion of the people through timely identification and prevention of diseases and other health problems, and improved delivery of medical care services, which in turn will help in the efficient, effective and equitable distribution of health care services to the people globally.2 AI can be adopted in the predictive analysis of disease and health problems through improved surveillance. For instance, AI machines or algorithms can help with the analysis of wide range of data and information from both human and nonhuman sources, which might help to predict the outbreak of health problem and diseases before they manifest, hence assisting public health officials in taking proactive and timely preventive actions, consequently diminishing disease morbidity and mortality.3 Likewise, AI could be used in the provision of automated and individualized-based well-being recommendations. For instance, AI applications or machines could help with the analysis of holistic health information of an individual thereby helping with the provision of appropriate specific-based behavioural and non-behavioural healthy recommendations for the promotion of individual’s well-being, especially the reduction of weight.4 Inequitable health care distribution is a major problem globally, especially in low- and middle-income countries and underserved communities. AI applications and algorithms could be used to cushion this effect by helping with the real-time analysis of health information of different groups or population of people which might help to identify the trend of health inequalities thereby helping public health researchers and personnels with the proper planning, execution and focusing of health intervention efforts to places and locations that are mostly in need.5 In addition, AI can be used to improve the delivery of medical care services. For instance, AI applications could help with the computerized scheduling of daily hospital tasks which includes the sorting, secondary assessment of patient’s complaints and scheduling of patient’s visitations, which will help to lessen the burden on human manpower and free up medical workers to focus more on urgent and important duties.6 AI could also be used in the diagnosis of disease and interpretation of results, thereby leading to timely detection of an health problem and early medical care, consequently helping in reducing disease burden and treatment expenses.7 It is important to note that some challenges might be encountered in the application of AI in public health. Some of which are ethical issues, low technical manpower especially in underdeveloped and developing countries, as well as information privacy and protection issues. In addition, issues of disparity might also occur in situations whereby the application of AI is not universally implemented.1 However, in respective of the possible problems highlighted, the beneficial effects of the application of AI in public health outweigh its non-application, and some of the implementation issues could be reduced and prevented through multifaceted efforts taking into consideration health personnel and medical service providers, lawmakers, AI experts and the general public.1 To conclude, AI offers a novel and unique dimension for promotion of health, and with proper planning and integration especially in low- and middle-income countries, AI would greatly help in public health and equitable health promotion of the people globally.
