PMID- 36440735
TI  - Artificial intelligence (AI) and human virtues: Towards a moral and quality health care system
FAU - Cordero DA.
DP  - 2022
AID - 10.1093/intqhc/mzac096 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editor, In a recent Editorial published in this journal, the authors rightfully presented the challenges and opportunities of artificial intelligence (AI) in health care. They highlighted that interest in AI in health care has grown substantially; however, challenges also emerged like the unavailability of large data sets for all researchers, the algorithms used to extract information from the data can quickly become obsolete as new techniques are invented and many others [1]. These claims are indeed valid since machine learning (ML) requires a continuous effort to master it. However, as AI undoubtedly contributes a lot to the health care system for both health providers and patients, another challenge that should not be set aside is its harmful impact when misused and abused. AI can be used to spread misinformation (MiF) and disinformation (DiF) in dealing with public health concerns. MiF is a broader classification of false or inaccurate claims shared largely unwittingly and without the intention to deceive, while DiF refers to a specific subset of MiF created with deliberate intentions to deceive [2]. Sometimes, ML algorithms do not perform better than traditional statistical methods, leading to the publication of papers that lack clinical or scientific value. Most ML and deep learning algorithms are often still regarded as a ‘black box’. During the start of the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic, scientists published an algorithm that could predict coronavirus infections from lung photos. Then, it turned out that the algorithm had drawn conclusions based on the imprint of the letter ‘R’ (for ‘right lung’) in the photos, which was always found in a slightly different spot on the scans. The researchers advise that ML algorithms should be evaluated against traditional statistical approaches (when applicable) before they are used in clinical research [3]. AI can also be used to create ‘deep fakes’, which are systems that adapt audio, pictures and videos to make people say and do things they never did [4]. In a recent finding by the World Health Organization, incorrect interpretations of health information often negatively impact people’s mental health and increase vaccine hesitancy and can delay the provision of health care. There were up to 51% of social media posts on vaccines, 28.8% on COVID-19 and 60% related to pandemics that contained health MiF, while 20–30% of videos were found to contain inaccurate or misleading information on YouTube [5]. The responsible use of AI should not be neglected, which means that human virtues must be enhanced to avoid abusing it. Virtues involve the good habits that help us discover and discern the truth. If one has the virtue of temperance, fake information will not be shared with anyone. If one has the virtue of trust, only information from experts and those in authority will be accepted as true. If one is compassionate, all actions will not cause any harm to anyone. And so, it all boils down to virtues. But how to cultivate one’s virtue? Since virtue is a habit, one must practice it regularly through social interaction until the person will become comfortable in responding to challenging situations appropriately and later on become an integral part of one’s moral system.
