AB  - Speeding up and improving the diagnosis process exactly where and when events occur is the goal of actual point of care (PoC) diagnostics. Besides progress in sensing technologies that pertain to multidisciplinary domains, including nanotechnologies, microfluidics, and advanced materials, it is envisaged that PoC diagnostics can significantly benefit from a tighter interplay with artificial intelligence (AI). The interdisciplinary impact of AI is closely related to the general area of digital signal processing, forming an integrating platform for different applications and unifying their background based on computational intelligence and machine learning (ML). This approach follows the ideas of Leibniz presented in history, trying to interconnect researchers of different narrow areas who lost their ability to communicate (1).
