PMID- 32310697
TI  - Global Developments in Stress Perfusion Cardiovascular Magnetic Resonance
FAU - Arai AE
FAU - Hsu LY.
DP  - 2020
AID - 10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.120.045967 [doi]
AB  - There have been several important developments in stress perfusion cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) imaging in the last few years. During the same time, there have been major new findings that are influencing the role of diagnostic imaging for detecting and managing coronary artery disease. On the technical side, artificial intelligence (AI), including subcategories of machine learning and deep learning, promises a wide range of benefits for diagnostic testing and cardiovascular imaging. The article by Knott et al1 in this edition of Circulation is 1 example of how AI methods can automatically perform complex analysis of imaging data and summarize it in ways that are clinically and prognostically important. Before discussing AI developments in CMR perfusion imaging, it is worth pausing to consider some of what has been learned about ischemia testing in general and some exciting developments in stress perfusion CMR. For decades, there has been a tension in diagnostic testing for coronary artery disease between proponents of anatomic assessment versus proponents of physiological detection. The United Kingdom is making a fundamental move from stress testing to using coronary computed tomography angiography (CCTA) as the first-line test in patients with stable angina. The NICE Guidelines2 (National Institute for Health and Care Excellence) now recommend CCTA as a first line test for diagnosing chronic chest pain. These recommendations were inspired, in part, by the SCOT-HEART trial (Scottish Computed Tomography of the HEART Trial), which showed that CCTA reclassified the diagnosis of coronary heart disease in 27% of patients, reduced the use of stress testing, but increased use of invasive coronary angiography, and changed patient treatment.3 At 5-year follow-up, the SCOT-HEART study resulted in a lower 5-year combined end point of nonfatal MI or death from coronary artery disease with similar long-term rate of utilizing coronary angiography and coronary revascularization. One should note that the revised NICE guidelines still list imaging stress tests as a second option for evaluating patients with chest pain but specifically say that exercise stress ECG and CMR coronary angiography should not be used. In terms of physiological assessment of the significance of coronary artery stenosis, invasive fractional flow reserve (FFR) has been accepted as the reference standard for functional significance of a coronary stenosis. In parallel, the NICE guidelines now also recognize a role for fractional flow reserve derived from CT coronary angiography (FFRCT) 4 as the noninvasive correlate to determine the functional significance of a coronary stenosis on a CCTA. In general, FFRCT appears to improve the specificity CCTA with minimal change in sensitivity.5 In the United States, stress myocardial perfusion imaging, mostly performed with single-photon emission computed tomography cameras, has remained the © 2020 American Heart Association, Inc.
