DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1093/af/vfae026 [doi]
AB  - • Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to save producers and researchers money and time in deploying camera trap arrays for animal monitoring. • Accessible tools for animal detection and classification, individual animal id, and behavior, movement estimation are available to a wide range of practitioners. • AI tools can support adaptive management strategies. • AI tools are currently adaptive to user input, allowing engaged users to influence development. The future implications of these technologies in assisting adaptive and precision management in complex agroecosystems will depend on continued collaboration among managers and scientists. Agroecosystems are often managed as multifunctional landscapes for food production, conservation, recreation, and social goals. Resource managers and livestock producers make crucial decisions dependent on spatiotemporal information of the presence or abundance of certain species, including predators, invasive species, or potential disease vectors, but this information is frequently lacking for complex ecosystems. Camera traps are a low-cost, non-invasive, and widespread approach to collecting animal monitoring data. The sheer volume of images generated by a camera trap array presents a resource challenge for converting these raw data into quantifiable, accessible decision aids, e.g., species distribution maps, or early warning systems to detect disease or invasive species. Artificial intelligence (AI) technologies can improve the capacity of scientists, managers, and ranchers to assess, monitor, and adapt management practices to these dynamic conditions and disparate objectives, increasing productivity and profitability while minimizing resource inputs. This paper explores the intersection of camera trap monitoring and AI-based image processing, known as computer vision, as an example application of AI to adaptive agroecological management. The chosen tools are accessible to a wide range of practitioners without necessitating computational expertise and have potential to expand to wider use. We propose a logic feedback model (Figure 1) that incorporates AI tools for camera traps to quantify and assist with decision making, using a scientific framework to inform contextual management strategies. Logic feedback model where computer vision tools for camera trap images form the core of adaptive management, research, and planning strategies. A good experimental design is essential to camera arrays regardless of size or project context, from meeting production objectives to answering research questions. A systematic approach engenders more comprehensive information-gathering to address motivating questions or information needs and allows one to answer additional questions that were not conceived at the initial planning stage. Design parameters vary depending on species of interest and planning goals; nascent computational tools such as R packages camtrapmonitoring (https://github.com/robitalec/camtrapmonitoring) and cameratrapgridR (https://github.com/CameraTrapDetectoR/cameratrapgridR/) can help create methodological camera trap deployment arrays according to user-specified criteria. While AI is not directly applied during planning, any insights gleaned from using AI on a camera array would inform future planning stages as part of iterative adaptive management or research strategies. Computer vision models are currently being deployed on-device by custom providers or in a cloud environment to reduce false triggers, classify animals by species, send notifications in real time, and even identify individual animals. The Conservation X Labs Sentinel camera (https://sentinel.conservationxlabs.com/) uses customized object detection models and adapted hardware to provide real-time notification capabilities in a variety of settings, including livestock monitoring, with full technological support. The NanoDetector (https://nanodetector.ai), developed by the University of Idaho with the Wildlife and Humans in Shared Landscapes Project (https://whisl.org) also deploys customized AI models on-device to detect and classify animals and notify users in real time using open-source software and standard commercial hardware. Recreational camera manufacturers including Bushnell, Moultrie, and Spypoint advertise species recognition for common hunting targets; we are unable to verify the methodology or accuracy of these offerings, but they indicate a growing interest in computer vision for camera trap images. These examples indicate a future widespread availability of real-time or on-device AI camera trap technologies. Due to the size and volume of image files generated and potential incompatibilities with remote processing and transfer, current practice usually necessitates processing camera trap data upon retrieval; many computer vision tools are specifically designed for this application. Select examples stand out for their accessibility, privacy, or application to animal production. Our tool CameraTrapDetectoR (Tabak et al., 2022; https://github.com/CameraTrapDetectoR) deploys object detection deep learning models at the taxonomic species, family, and class levels on multiple platforms to serve users with varying levels of technical experience. Models are trained on an agriculture-focused dataset by a USDA development team, with a mission of retaining data privacy while providing free, generalized, one-stop detection, and classification AI models. Pytorch Wildlife (https://github.com/microsoft/CameraTraps) is supported by millions of training images and is highly accurate at animal detection but necessitates subsequent computer vision tools to automate classification. Wildlife Insights (https://www.wildlifeinsights.org) has a comfortable user interface and species-level classification but requires users to share their data to a cloud source and charges a fee above an image threshold or team size. The automation of image processing via computer vision acts as a gateway to establishing larger camera trap arrays with more cameras that can be deployed over longer time periods. The ability to process more data across larger areas at faster speeds will lead to more comprehensive data and better-informed analyses and decisions. Computer vision tools are also innovating analysis of classified camera trap images. Two types of AI models have broad implications for both managers and researchers: individual animal id and pose estimation. AI-assisted estimates of density, group behavior, and presence/absence can already be used to track management outcomes and inform adaptive management in animal systems. We anticipate future development of more affordable, accessible, and accurate AI tools for individual animal identification to estimate population size and monitor ecological events such as wildlife-livestock interactions, foraging behavior, and the effect of environmental factors. Managers could more closely monitor individuals’ behavior, and researchers will be able to use these tools to replace mark-recapture methods and other expensive, intensive interventions to estimate populations and monitor the spread of invasive species. The concept has been proven in an open-source format by SealNet (https://github.com/zbirenbaum/SealFaceRecognition), a facial recognition software for harbor seals that can be run on an AWS-supported Graphical User Interface (GUI) and by BearID (https://github.com/bearid/bearid-dlib), an open-source workflow to recognize individual bears that can be reproduced on other datasets. Both projects operate on closed datasets where all individuals are known and previously identified; however, their success indicates progress towards open systems with updated methodologies where previously unknown individuals can be identified and labelled. AI-supported pose estimation and movement tracking tools are essential for quantifying animal behavior, e.g. early disease detection or quantifying desirable behaviors, and can be used to inform management decisions. The DeepLabCut (Mathis et al., 2018), LabGym (Hu et al., 2023), and SLEAP (Pereira et al., 2022) tools guide users to label a small number of training images, then fine-tune a custom deep-learning model to quantify and track behaviors across an entire dataset. Additional tools to track animal behavior include DeepEthogram (https://github.com/jbohnslav/deepethogram) and SimBA (https://github.com/sgoldenlab/simba). Species interactions are of particular interest in camera trap arrays, and SLEAP offers the unique capability to detect and track multiple species’ behaviors in the same frame. These tools can measure animal physiology or traits such as the standardization of Body Composition Score, quantify behaviors and features that optimize reproductive output, or detect and quantify interaction between individuals. Results from these tools can also feed back into image processing models to optimize their training data and improve performance; for example, the CameraTrapDetectoR project is using SLEAP and DeepLabCut to improve training data by annotating animal orientation, thus creating a more effective training sample with fewer images that is stratified across different poses and behaviors. AI-based tools are essential to expanding the information reach and processing capacity of large data for systems-based, trans-disciplinary learning that provide better decision frameworks for all stakeholders on a multi-use landscape. For example, a collaborative stakeholder group can combine resources to deploy a single robust, comprehensive camera array on a landscape that would enable all contributors to answer different questions, build trust and learning, improve efficiency and service-delivery outcomes, and close the gap among organizations with different missions using the same landscape. A collaborative approach would further reinforce ethical and responsible use of AI-based tools. The fast-paced, flexible nature of computer vision provides a current opportunity to become an engaged user in these open-source tools and directly influence development through participant feedback. These tools can support a system of precision animal production that bolsters existing collaborative, adaptive management practices and enhances our ability to respond to dynamic biological and social conditions. Amira Burns is a fellow with the USDA Agricultural Research Service (ARS), Range Sheep Production Efficiency Research Unit at the US Sheep Experiment Station, and the ARS SCINet Program and AI Center of Excellence. She holds a Masters in Applied Statistics from Colorado State University. She is the developer and maintainer of the CameraTrapDetectoR project. Her research focuses on developing user-friendly tools to advance animal, agricultural, environmental, and health science. Corresponding author:[email protected] Hailey Wilmer is a Research Rangeland Management Specialist at the USDA-ARS Range Sheep Production Efficiency Research Unit in Dubois, Idaho, USA. She completed MS and PhD in rangeland ecosystem science at Colorado State University. Prior to joining the US Sheep Experiment Station staff in 2021, Hailey worked at the USDA-ARS in Fort Collins and as a social scientist with the US Forest Service in Juneau. Her current research questions include: what are the long-term effects of grazing, climate, and other disturbances on high elevation rangeland plant communities? How can researchers and managers work together to conduct useful and useable science? How do ranchers and managers make sense of the complex systems they manage? Can mainstream science integrate local knowledge to support ranching livelihoods, biodiversity, and food production? Ryan S. Miller is a senior ecologist with United States Department of Agriculture Center for Epidemiology and Animal Health. He received a Doctor of Philosophy in Zoology from Colorado State University working with Dr. Colleen Webb and the Webb Lab, a Master of Science in Forestry and a Bachelor of Science in Forest Management. His research spans landscape and population ecology, with special interests in disease ecology and conservation biology, mechanisms that drive contact and transmission of pathogens at the wildlife-livestock interface, and how policy can be established to mitigate transmission. J. Bret Taylor is the research leader of the USDA ARS, Range Sheep Production Efficiency Research Unit at the US Sheep Experiment Station near Dubois, Idaho. He holds PhD and MS degrees (New Mexico State University) in animal science with emphases in ruminant nutrition, molecular biology, and toxicology and BS degrees (West Texas A&M University) in chemistry and animal science. His research focuses on developing ecologically-oriented sheep genetic resources applicable toward grazing utility of sheep originating from extensive rangeland systems and discovery and application of unique antibiotic-replacement products. Patrick E. Clark is a Rangeland Scientist with the USDA Agricultural Research Service Northwest Watershed Research Center in Boise, Idaho. His research interests span disturbance ecology, range animal behavior, pastoralism, and remote sensing. This manuscript was invited for submission by the American Society of Animal Science (ASAS). The views expressed in this publication are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the views or policies of the ASAS, the journal, or the publisher. A.B.: Conceptualization; Writing—original draft; Writing—review and editing. H.W.: Conceptualization; Visualization; Writing—review and editing. R.S.M.: Conceptualization; Writing—review and editing. B.J.T.: Conceptualization; Writing—review and editing. P.E.C.: Conceptualization; Writing—review and editing Conflict of interest statement. None declared.
