PMID- 42563814
TI  - The Digital Diagnostic Odyssey: Artificial Intelligence and Social Media Crowdsourcing Amid Pediatric Neurology Access Gaps
FAU - Smith AM
FAU - Vassar R.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1002/cns3.70048 [doi]
AB  - Child neurology continues to face a workforce shortage in the United States. The average wait time to see a pediatric neurologist is 45 days, and 30% of children wait more than 8 weeks [1]. Because three-quarters of child neurologists work in urban settings [1], access to care is even more restricted for families in rural areas. The situation has left an access gap that is already partially being filled by artificial intelligence (AI)-assisted diagnosis and social media crowdsourcing for information. AI has also helped children with rare neurological conditions who did have access to pediatric neurologists but remained undiagnosed. Such tools can yield impressive breakthroughs for some families but also pose significant risks. One striking example of AI's use in pediatric neurology comes from a mother who spent 3 years seeking answers for her son's gross motor delay and back pain [2]. After consultations with 17 medical professionals yielded no diagnosis, the mother entered his symptoms and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) report into ChatGPT (OpenAI). It suggested tethered cord syndrome, which was later confirmed by a pediatric neurosurgeon and quickly repaired. This patient demonstrates AI's potential as a diagnostic partner when traditional care pathways fail or are unavailable, particularly for rare conditions. Emerging AI-based clinical diagnostic tools also show promise. One AI model trained on several thousand video clips of infantile spasms was able to identify a seizure with 85% accuracy [3]. Another AI tool analyzed caregiver questionnaires and home videos to identify behaviors consistent with autism spectrum disorder (ASD) with more than 98% sensitivity [4]. This suggests a potential role for AI in streamlining pediatric neurological diagnoses and partially filling the child neurologist access gap. Streamlined diagnosis for many pediatric neurological conditions leads to earlier treatment and provides an undeniable benefit in conditions such as ASD, infantile spasms, and other forms of epilepsy [5-7]. However, because AI is new, we do not have data on how many families trust and act upon these AI-assisted diagnoses, nor can we identify instances of unnecessary or harmful treatment of a child due to an incorrect AI-assisted diagnosis. It is also important to consider the potential complexities of AI-assisted diagnoses on healthcare coverage. For example, many insurance providers require a formal evaluation for ASD using the Autism Diagnostic Observation Schedule or other gold-standard assessment for service eligibility [8]. Policy change would be required before AI-based care can fully translate into earlier access to therapies for children. AI may speed diagnosis for some children, but this does not guarantee earlier therapy if families cannot access the necessary resources. Another success story for AI in pediatric neurology is described in a recent Medium article [9] in which a parent shared a breakthrough in communicating with his largely nonverbal son with ASD. He asked ChatGPT to create a cartoon picture exchange communication system (PECS)-style image showing four different activities. The father wrote, “When I showed the picture to Phillip, he immediately pointed—decisively and happily—to the [PECS-style image] with a swing. And just like that, we had a plan” [9]. With AI, this father was able to generate custom visuals on demand, even for very specific or unusual requests. His story presents another notable example of the utility of AI tools in child neurology. Recent studies underscore that large language models (LLMs), while improving in accuracy, remain imperfect diagnostic tools in pediatrics and neurology. In one study, neurologists achieved approximately 75% diagnostic accuracy compared with ChatGPT-3.5's 54% and Gemini's 46% [10]. Another recent study demonstrated impressive diagnostic accuracy of ChatGPT-4o in pediatric emergency medicine cases; however, the four other major LLMs were inferior to this model, particularly with complex cases [11]. Pediatric neurology cases are often difficult to diagnose, which is one of the motivating reasons for patients, families, and physicians to turn to AI for answers. This dilemma indicates that caution is still necessary when trusting AI-assisted pediatric neurological diagnoses. Similar to AI, social media crowdsourcing is increasingly being used by the public for neurological diagnosis, which presents risk of misdiagnosis and unnecessary or inappropriate treatment [12, 13]. Many parents share videos online asking for answers to their child's symptoms. A 2024 systematic review analyzed social media posts concerning epilepsy on multiple sites and found the highest amount of misinformation on TikTok, with 55% of video posts incorrectly claiming to show an epileptic seizure when the episode was truly a psychogenic nonepileptic seizure [13]. Seeking neurological diagnoses through social media thus presents a high risk of misinformation for parents and patients. The distinction between benign neonatal sleep myoclonus and epileptic spasms is a salient example of the importance of avoiding crowdsourced diagnosis. Benign neonatal sleep myoclonus is significantly more common than infantile spasms, only occurs during sleep, and stops with arousal [14]. These features distinguish it from epileptic myoclonus but are easy to miss in short, de-contextualized videos posted online. Misdiagnosis by social media crowdsourcing poses the risk of causing unnecessary anxiety for parents and increased emergency visits. Another pitfall of social media usage among pediatric neurology patients is the curious emergence of functional neurological symptoms. During the COVID-19 pandemic, there was an outbreak of tic-like behaviors in adolescents widely dubbed “TikTok tics.” This was identified as the first mass sociogenic illness propagated through social networks [15]. Patients, mostly girls, began displaying tic-like movements after watching popular TikTok creators with Tourette syndrome or tics. Notably, nearly 38% of these affected teens also reported psychogenic nonepileptic seizures alongside their tic symptoms [16]. In this example, social media not only spread tic mimicry but also coincided with a surge in stress-related seizure-like episodes in vulnerable teens. Although unvetted internet sources carry risks of misinformation, social media can still play a beneficial role in child neurology, particularly in providing community for families affected by rare neurological diseases [17]. Neurological condition-specific forums or social media sites can reduce feelings of isolation by connecting parents and patients and facilitate the exchange of helpful, high-quality information such as open clinical trials. Forming communities through social media has in fact allowed industry and academia to fortify rare disease registries [18]. In 2019, the Rare Disease Registries Patient Council was formed, which included global patient advocacy groups. The council produced several helpful products, such as plain-language summaries of current evidence-based medicine for specific diseases [17, 18]. Increased awareness of rare neurological conditions via social forums and crowdsourced registries may also benefit pediatricians or neurologists who have never encountered these diseases before, and thus any similar future patients. AI and social media tools have proved to be a double-edged sword for parents and patients in the realm of neurological and developmental health. They can offer valuable insights, personalized support, and even innovative ways to communicate or organize care, sometimes succeeding where traditional routes have struggled. From diagnosing a tethered spinal cord to generating innovative forms of PECS, these examples show the promise of internet assistance in neurological diagnosis and management. However, it is equally clear that AI and social media do not replace physicians; they can interpret presentations outside of proper clinical context, misdiagnose, and spread misinformation, potentially leading to anxiety and unnecessary emergency visits. Taken together, these examples suggest that AI is most effective when it is medically validated and complements, rather than replaces, medical professionals. Moreover, the best usage of social media in child neurology practice appears to be building supportive communities and rare disease networks. Audrey M. Smith: conceptualization, investigation, formal analysis, software, validation, visualization, writing – original draft preparation, and writing – review and editing. Rachel Vassar: conceptualization, project administration, supervision, validation, writing – review and editing. The authors declare no conflicts of interest. The authors received no specific funding for this work.
