PMID- 37477239
TI  - Artificial intelligence in public health: the potential and ethical considerations of artificial intelligence in public health
FAU - Suarjana IWG
FAU - Sudirham
FAU - Salam I
FAU - Aditama MHR.
DP  - 2023
AID - 10.1093/pubmed/fdad116 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editors, This is a correspondence regarding topics in the Journal of Public Health1 that explore the intersection between artificial intelligence (AI) and its potential applications in public health. The growing interest in AI calls for consideration of algorithm bias in public health. While the article highlights the potential benefits of AI in public health, it fails to adequately discuss its challenges and limitations. AI implementation may face barriers such as data quality, interoperability, resource constraints and the need for human oversight. Due to recent developments in AI in public health, AI can help doctors and medical personnel diagnose diseases with greater accuracy, and provide appropriate treatment recommendations based on the analysis of patient data.2 The use of AI in analyzing population health data can help in the prediction of disease risk and the spread of infectious diseases. This allows for more effective prevention measures and faster countermeasures.3 The implementation of AI in public health requires strict data privacy protection and strong security. Policies and practices that secure personal health information and prevent unauthorized access are required.4 AI algorithms can have inherent biases that affect clinical decisions and healthcare equity. There is a need for bias mitigation and policies that ensure equitable access and equitable health outcomes for all populations.5
