PMID- 34324643
TI  - Artificial intelligence: the pathway to the future of cardiovascular medicine
FAU - Lüscher TF
FAU - Lyon A
FAU - Amstein R
FAU - Maisel A.
DP  - 2022
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehab472 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is the centre stage in many fields of medicine and beyond. Indeed, PubMed lists >300 000 articles published on AI just in the field of medicine, not to speak about engineering, astronomy, and many other fields. Many of us were shocked when reading that in 1997 chess champion, Garry Kasparov, lost the second game against IBM’s supercomputer Deep Blue. That a machine was apparently smarter and faster than the world chess champion was a turning point for many. AI has remained an enigma, and indeed, it is a black box that we do not fully understand, even those working with it. In particular, deep learning, be it supervised and particularly if unsupervised, leaves us at a loss in understanding what a computer exactly does. Nevertheless, AI provides amazing results in many fields, including cardiovascular medicine that, if confirmed in independent large datasets, may be clinically useful. To that end, Alex Lyon, Thomas F. Lüscher, and Alan Maisel from London and San Diego decided to organize the first meeting in cardiovascular medicine focusing on AI (Figure 1). The Artificial Intelligence in Cardiovascular Disease 21, London. In medicine, we basically pursue four major aims, i.e. (i) to predict those at risk to acquire a given disease when still healthy; (ii) to diagnose a disease as the patient develops signs and symptoms; (iii) to understand the natural history of a given disease; and (iv) optimal prevention and management. In all these four areas, AI and machine learning provide algorithms that are increasingly valuable to help physicians in their everyday work, particularly in cardiovascular medicine. Indeed, the risk of acquiring a given disease today can be predicted with unforeseen precision using genetics, biomarkers, imaging, and other parameters. For instance, using computed coronary angiography has unforeseen predictive power using AI-based imaging analysis. Charalampos Antoniades from Oxford showed that using AI, events can be predicted using fat attenuation around the inflamed coronary arteries before symptoms have arisen.1–3 Similarly, Paul Friedman, using ECG information based on the vector electrocardiogram is able to determine whether a given patient has had episodes of atrial fibrillation beforehand, even if the current ECG is in sinus rhythm.4,5 Along these lines, he also showed that AI can determine whether left ventricular ejection fraction is normal or reduced in a given patient and about his probability to have aortic stenosis.6 Thus, AI is not only faster than humans, and cardiologists in particular, but also sees signals of value for clinical assessment that our eye, or brain, does not pick up. Furthermore, AI and machine learning can process an enormous number of variables. Indeed, this is a true problem for physicians working in the modern era. For instance, as Lars Wallentin demonstrated that the interaction between the two conditions and the resulting interpretation for a patient and its outcomes are of utmost importance.7 Again, the enormous amount of data from uncountable trials and those assessed during evaluation of an individual patient have to come together for precise risk prediction and appropriate management of such patients, a challenge that can only be addressed using this technology. Cardiovascular medicine lends itself particularly well to the use of machine learning, not only because of the vast amount of clinical and laboratory data, but also due to the fact that imaging is so important in this field. To that end, Paul Leeson, from Oxford, outlined the enormous potential of AI for the interpretation of echo data in general (Figure 2),8 the fact that Jeroen Bax, from Leiden, the Netherlands, further pursued for valvular heart disease. Again, here, AI is not only faster but also is able to pick up findings that escape the human eye.9 Participants of the panel discussion (left), Paul Leeson, Oxford, talking about artificial intelligence and echo (middle), and Folkert Asselbergs, Utrecht, on secondary prevention (right). The Artificial Intelligence 21 in Cardiovascular Medicine Conference aimed to bring an amazing faculty together to cover the entire field of AI for the epidemiology, diagnosis, and management of patients with cardiac disease, in an attempt to show the potential of this technology for clinical practice, but also to discuss the downsides and quality standards required. In particular, it was repetitively mentioned by the faculty members that an external, independent validation of any results obtained using this approach is mandatory, to confirm the validity and the usefulness of a given algorithm. Indeed, few studies provide externally validated results or compared the performance of their deep learning algorithms with healthcare professionals using the same dataset. Indeed, any algorithm ideally should be better than an experienced clinician to be of use in practice. In addition, poor reporting has been noted in such registries, which limits the reliability and accuracy of such studies and algorithms derived thereof. Thus, proper reporting standards that address these issues of deep learning are urgently required. The Artificial Intelligence 21 in Cardiovascular Medicine Conference was a hybrid meeting with a low number of onsite participants, because of the remaining fear of Covid-19 and in particular, the recently evolved Indian variant. However, there were a substantial number of participants from all over the world. It is hoped that this meeting will become a yearly tradition, hopefully face-to-face in the future, and we are looking forward to welcoming an increasing number of physicians realizing the importance and usefulness of this approach for research and clinical practice. Importantly, as Eric Topol pointed out, AI will not substitute physicians (with possibly the exception of radiologists, etc.)10 but indeed will become any doctor’s little helper accelerating any diagnostic work and decision-making, thereby allowing more time for the patient and their questions and fears. Thus, AI, in the end, will be patient centric, if properly applied as suggested by the faculty at the meeting. Conflict of interest: TFL, RA and AL have no conflicts related to this conference report except that the meeting was supported by unrestricted educational grants by Abbott, Amgen, Quidel and Roche. AM is founder of Brsainstorm.
