TI  - An evaluation of funded National Institutes of Health grants in nursing science and artificial intelligence
FAU - Dzikowicz DJ
FAU - Aktas M
FAU - Wang J
FAU - Dreisbach C.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1016/j.outlook.2025.102529 [doi]
AB  - The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) in nursing research holds significant potential. However, AI's development and deployment remains a crucial area of research. This study aims to evaluate the type (e.g., R01, R21, and&#xa0;K08), topic, and cost of National Institutes of Health (NIH)-funded grants focusing on AI in nursing science from 2013 to 2023. Using the RePORTER database, we identified NIH-funded grants with keywords related to nursing and AI from 2013 to 2023. We used topic modeling on the project abstracts to uncover key themes. We identified 370 NIH-funded AI and nursing-related grants, a 1,160% increase over time. Most NINR-funded projects were training-focused. Key themes included cognitive decline, inpatient predictive analytics, and chronic disease self-management. While AI-related funding is growing, nurse-led projects remain limited, highlighting the need for expanded nursing leadership in AI research. Strategic efforts are essential to support nurse scientists in shaping the future of AI-integrated nursing science.
