PMID- 38339876
TI  - How artificial intelligence can assist with ischaemic heart disease
FAU - Uzokov J
FAU - Alyavi A
FAU - Alyavi B
FAU - Abdullaev A.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehae030 [doi]
AB  - Ischaemic heart disease (IHD) is the most common type of cardiovascular disease (CVD) and the leading cause of death and morbidity among patients with CVD. Ischaemic heart disease is considered a major threat in the 21st century.1,2 The majority of patients with non-fatal IHD live with chronic conditions and impaired quality of life, which in turn leads to increased load on healthcare systems and demands effective and prompt treatment of the disease from doctors. It is predicted that the rate of IHD will increase not only due to population growth and increasing life expectancy but also due to advanced diagnostic techniques and an increased number of risk factors such as diabetes, obesity, and socio-economic conditions.3 Considering the high prevalence, morbidity, and mortality of IHD and its subsequent impact on healthcare, it is essential to quickly and timeously identify and stratify patients with IHD for further management using cloud-based innovative digital technologies and artificial intelligence (AI). With advances in technology and healthcare, AI has emerged as a powerful tool in medicine, demonstrating its potential to transform the landscape of medical diagnostics. In particular, AI holds promise for assisting the medical field in the early, timely, and precise identification of IHD, paving the way for more efficient intervention and improved patient outcomes. Artificial intelligence excels in processing vast amounts of medical data, including patient histories, lab results, and imaging studies. Machine learning algorithms can identify subtle patterns and correlations that might escape the human eye, enhancing the diagnostic accuracy of IHD. The electrocardiogram (ECG) is a fundamental tool in diagnosing ischaemic changes of the myocardium. Artificial intelligence algorithms can analyse ECG data swiftly and accurately, identifying any abnormalities in the heart’s electrical activity that may suggest ischaemic changes. This, in turn, may allow for quicker diagnosis and intervention. Artificial intelligence algorithms can be trained to interpret echocardiograms, a key imaging modality for assessing cardiac function. By analysing the intricate details of heart images, AI can detect abnormalities, such as reduced cardiac output, any kinetic changes, or impaired ventricular function, indicative of IHD. Coronary angiography analysis by AI represents a huge frontier in IHD diagnosis. Leveraging the power of AI, this modern approach offers a complicated and precise examination of the coronary arteries. By employing advanced algorithms, AI can rigorously evaluate angiographic images, identifying subtle anomalies and providing quantitative insights into vessel dimensions, flow patterns, and potential obstructions. This transformative technology not only enhances the efficiency of coronary angiography interpretation but also holds promise for early detection, personalized treatment planning, and improved patient outcomes in the realm of IHD. Artificial intelligence can assist in stratifying patients based on their risk of developing IHD. By analysing existing risk and medical history, AI can help identify individuals who may benefit from closer monitoring and preventive measures and it can also define how they might benefit from such measures. Seamless integration of AI with electronic health records (EHRs) and the above-mentioned diagnostic tools streamlines the diagnostic process. Artificial intelligence algorithms can analyse patient data stored in EHRs, interpret existing diagnostic tool results, and integrate their data for further processing. In addition, AI can provide healthcare professionals with comprehensive insights into a patient’s cardiovascular health and aid in the early detection of IHD and apply precise management strategies. Recently, Professor Alyavi A.L. and his team were awarded a Research Excellence Program Grant by the World Bank Group in the framework of Modernizing Uzbekistan National Innovation System (MUNIS) for their proposed project. The project brings together a nationwide community of cardiovascular and digital health researchers as well as physicians across tertiary care to study the early diagnostic and stratification possibilities of AI in IHD. The project’s prime objective is to solve problems with diagnostics and stratification of patients with IHD with regard to applying advances in digitalization. It aims to develop, design, and implement a personalized, innovative computer-based platform further integrated with digital tools that will be driven by AI. In the proposed project, three major diagnostic tools, such as ECG, echocardiography, and coronary angiography, will be used in one application to accurately diagnose and stratify patients with IHD for further management (Figure 1). Project overview Even though several studies have been conducted to diagnose IHD with specific diagnostic tools using AI, to date, there is no single study or development using three major diagnostic methods that are ECG, echocardiography, and coronary angiography in an integrated application to diagnose and stratify patients with CVD. All available studies are either limited to only one diagnostic method or include restricted cardiovascular conditions, which in turn creates a gap in terms of using AI and machine learning to diagnose and stratify patients with IHD. These issues and challenges encourage the team to focus on the proposed project and create an AI-based platform to diagnose and stratify patients with IHD using three major diagnostic tools. Project specific aims, its design and study cohort are summarised in Table 1. Project specific aims and study cohort The study brings together the two medical centres in Uzbekistan, the ‘Republican Specialized Scientific Practical Medical Center of Therapy and Medical Rehabilitation’ and the ‘Republican Specialized Scientific Practical Medical Center of Cardiology’, along with Tashkent Pediatric Medical Institute as well as researchers across three research institutes of the country. Furthermore, two leading cardiovascular and digital health key opinion leaders have been invited to form an advisory panel and two local, experienced doctors will test the developed model. Finally, reflecting the promptness of increasing attention on AI in the diagnosis of CVD particularly, IHD, plans and projects are also underway to run parallel studies in Uzbekistan and abroad. The implementation of AI in the diagnosis of IHD represents a significant step towards more effective and timely healthcare interventions. By harnessing the power of deep learning and data analysis, AI has the potential to increase diagnostic accuracy, improve patient outcomes, and contribute to the global effort to combat the burden of IHD. As research and technology advances, the collaborative partnership between AI and medical doctors holds great promise for a future where early identification and management become the norm in the fight against CVD. The authors would like to thank all Project Implementation Unit of ‘MUNIS’ and management of the ‘Republican Specialized Scientific Practical Medical Center of Therapy and Medical Rehabilitation’ for their continuous support. All authors declare no disclosure of interest for this contribution. This study is funded by REP25112021-84 grant under the MUNIS Project, supported by the World Bank and the Government of the Republic of Uzbekistan.
