PMID- 35869894
TI  - Artificial intelligence in proteomics: new frontiers from risk prediction to treatment?
FAU - Unterhuber M
FAU - Kresoja KP
FAU - Lurz P
FAU - Thiele H.
DP  - 2022
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehac391 [doi]
AB  - In cardiology, risk scores are used daily to estimate the cardiovascular disease (CVD) risk of a patient and eventually guide clinical decision making. To create accurate scoring systems, large populations are used, and clinically measurable parameters are included. These scores and calculations represent mostly linear and proportional relationships which correspond to the idea of ‘more A, therefore more B’. This allows for simple interpretability of causal relationships of risk factor and observed events, e.g. smoking or high blood pressure are harmful. Clinical risk models tend to perform well on average but show poor precision for individuals. There are subjects who despite having a high-risk profile will not develop significant CVD or experience events. Those ‘outliers’ may have a different clinical response to external conditions, e.g. heavy smoking, and still not develop any disease manifestation. Some may attribute this to fortune, but it is more probable that the individual is predisposed to a ‘natural resistance,’ or the exposure to a specific risk factor was not sufficient to cause damage. This naturally limits classical clinical risk scores, where the risk factor is always handled in the same manner depicting an overall risk, where outliers cannot be identified. To increase the precision of prediction, it is important to gain knowledge on which constellation of properties is hazardous and which is protective: For example, instead of mere smoking status, a more sophisticated approach is necessary which assesses whether smoking shifted the physiological constellation of an individual towards a hazard or not. This requires the use of novel techniques, and machine learning (ML) suggests itself for the task as it can identify outliers by deciding on an individual basis according to ‘When A is high, then B can be lower or higher, depending on C.’. Therefore, combining the analysis of circulating proteins with ML makes it an attractive method. The proteome is the result of the genomic composition, epigenetics, and environment and can therefore be seen as a surrogate marker for the product of our genetic legacy, predisposition, and external influences (Figure 1). Proteome as a link between genetics and the environment. The information contained in the proteome can be unravelled by reverse engineering through Machine Learning and used to predict the cardiovascular risk, find clinically meaningful phenotypes and drug targets. In a study, the proteome of 1524 healthy individuals was used to predict cardiovascular events such as myocardial infarction and stroke.1 Multiple ML models were derived and compared with clinical risk scores in 702 subjects of an external cohort. The area under the curve (AUC) achieved by the proteomics model was 0.71 in comparison with the 0.61 of the clinical risk scores (P < 0.001). A step further has been taken by constructing ML models with proteomics and all-cause mortality as outcome. Multiple algorithms analysed proteins in a cohort comprising 1998 patients and compared their performance with clinical risk scores such as the Framingham and European Risk-Chart-SCORE. The models were validated in an independent cohort of 772 subjects. The AUC increased from 0.65 as seen in conventional statistical models to 0.94 in the neural network (P < 0.001).2 These study results highlight four fundamentals: the necessity of using reproducible and standardized input data for ML models. the need to use most precisely defined outcomes, whereas death might be the most well-defined outcome of all. the observation that some proteins may interact differently based on the state of additional conditions. lastly, ML models performed better than the clinical risk scores without the need of including clinical parameters such as age, smoking status, or gender. This suggests that the proteome may be an unbiased and accurate reflection of the actual risk-influencing factors. By using Shapley values and plotting outcomes predicted by the models in different states of the proteins, we were able to show that assuming linear interactions between the variables leads to over-simplifying the responsible mechanisms. Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) was the most influential peptide for the ML algorithm to predict death, albeit it did not reach statistical significance in the multivariable Cox and logistic regression models (odds ratio 1.23; 95% CI 0.99–1.53, P = 0.058 and hazard ratio 1.20; 95%CI 1.00–1.45, P = 0.051, respectively). The reason was demasked as BNP was linearly correlated with death only within a pre-specified range; otherwise, it depended on other peptides involved in other processes such as tumor-necrosis-factor-related-apoptosis-inducing-ligand-receptor-2 (TRAILR2). TRAILR2 has been found to counterintuitively regulate also other non-canonical cellular effects and play a critical role in pulmonary hypertension3 and to be involved in protein changes in smokers.4 Outside of the pre-specified range, BNP showed a curvilinear mortality–probability relationship (Figure 2). All-Cause Death probability is influenced in a non-linear fashion. Simplified relationship graph depicting the probability of death in function to brain natriuretic peptide and its interaction with tumour-necrosis-factor-related-apoptosis-inducing-ligand-receptor 2 levels, adapted from.2 Death probability increases linearly only within a small range of brain natriuretic peptide levels ( = linear relationship zone). In lower brain natriuretic peptide levels, higher tumour-necrosis-factor-related-apoptosis-inducing-ligand-receptor 2 levels can be beneficial on mortality; in higher brain natriuretic peptide levels it can act adversely. These relationships are modelled by machine learning with infinite dimensions; therefore, interpretation with more than three variables can be difficult. When focusing on a specified target, it is possible to aim for pathways involved only in the outcome of interest and to follow the steps undertaken by specific peptides, all the way up to their origin in the genome. This has been already achieved in training neural networks to predict splicing from a pre-mRNA out from a genomic sequence to detect noncoding mutations in rare genetic diseases, paving the way for a reverse engineering of the human genome-protein processing.5 Chemical compounds can be designed to modify specific targets, but also to influence properties like solubility and bioactivity.6 This could lead to treatment response prediction, virtually interacting with the proteins to find novel drug targets, to monitor and predict response, towards a patient-tailored, cost-effective precision medicine. Complex events like major cardiovascular events or even the death of a human being are associated to and may by a substantial part be determined by circulating protein levels. Proteomics are a way to efficiently gain multidimensional information from a drop of blood, yielding promising results when combined with ML techniques. They represent a surrogate marker of the genetic predisposition, environmental influence, and the behaviour of an individual and can be used to predict the probability of cardiovascular events. By putting an effort in unravelling ML’s black boxes, reverse engineering of human physiology at a protein level might be a feasible way to gain new insights on manifold applications. This field merits further attention, as currently we only scratched on the surface of all the possibilities. None declared. Conflict of interest: The authors declare no conflict of interest. This article did rely solely on information based by the papers which were cited, therefore no data availability statement can be done.
JT  - Eur Heart J
OT  - Medicine
OT  - Proteomics
OT  - Artificial intelligence
OT  - Data science

