PMID- 37525522
TI  - The metamorphosis of education: an opinion on how artificial intelligence is changing education
FAU - Saputra R
FAU - Hambali I
FAU - Muslihati M
FAU - Setiyowati AJ
FAU - Lidyawati Y
FAU - Situmorang DDB.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/pubmed/fdad136 [doi]
AB  - Dear editor, Recently there was an article discussing the existence of the Metaverse in the post-COVID-19 era,1 but unfortunately it has not definitively discussed its impact on education. The advancement of technology, particularly Artificial Intelligence (AI), has brought sweeping changes to many areas of our lives.2 One of the areas that technology is revolutionizing is education.3 AI is transforming the way students learn and educators teach, making education more personalized, adaptive, and accessible to learners all over the world.4 AI has the potential to revolutionize education in various ways, as it allows teachers to customize learning experiences for individual learners and offers a more engaging and interactive approach to learning. 5 Moreover, AI ability to analyze students’ progress and learning patterns allows educators to identify areas where students are struggling and provide targeted interventions to help them succeed. 6 In addition, AI-assisted assessments can provide more accurate and efficient evaluation of students’ progress, reducing the time and resources needed for grading.7 However, as with any technological advancement, there are also potential ethical and social implications that must be considered.8 The emergence of AI in education is already evident, and the recently launched International Conference on AI in Education in 2017 explores this intersection. 9 AI is transforming education by offering personalized and interactive learning experiences, analyzing student progress to provide targeted interventions and providing efficient evaluation of students’ progress.6 Despite the potential benefits of AI in education, there are also challenges that must be addressed.10 One of the main concerns is that AI may take over the role of teachers, which could lead to a reduction in human interaction, creativity, and critical thinking skills.7
