DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1093/pm/pnae094 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editor, In the factsheet “Current Status of Pain Education and Implementation Challenges,” the International Association for the Study of Pain (IASP) highlights global deficiencies in pain education, noting that countries, regardless of their resource level, fail to meet recommended preclinical training standards. IASP encourages the identification of seminal resources to introduce new teaching approaches. Safe and effective pain management necessitates addressing barriers to pain education and identifying viable resources.1 ChatGPT, launched on November 30, 2022, by OpenAI,2 is based on generative language models and designed to provide immediate responses to user inquiries. It facilitates interaction with a knowledge base through an artificial intelligence (AI) system, offering relevant answers swiftly. This tool is freely accessible and can be used on any mobile device, laptop, or desktop without the need for installation. Among the various generative language models developed by leading technology companies, such as Google’s Gemini, IBM’s Project Debater, and Microsoft’s Turing-NLG, ChatGPT stands out for its rapid adoption. By January 2023, ChatGPT reached a record 100 million users within 2 months of launch, making it the fastest-growing consumer app in history. By June 2024, users increased to more than 180.5 million, an 80% rise since January 2023, showcasing unprecedented and sustained growth in the industry.3 Recent reports suggest that ChatGPT could significantly transform medical education, offering a vast range of applications, such as personalized tutoring, patient simulation, generation of examination questions, and streamlined information management.4 Nevertheless, although ChatGPT generates texts that seem plausible and coherent, it does not truly “understand” them.5 This can result in “hallucinations,” where the AI presents plausible but incorrect information.5 For end-users to place sufficient trust in its responses, it is imperative that these models be validated through formal evaluations, particularly in sensitive contexts like pain medicine education, where accuracy and consistency are crucial.6 To the best of our knowledge, although ChatGPT has b`een tested on standard medical exams such as the United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE),7 it has not specifically been evaluated for its proficiency in the nuanced areas of pain medicine. Although our analysis centers on a specific textbook, the methods and analytical techniques we use can be broadly applied to assess written educational materials. Our study focused on Chapter 5, “Neuropathic Pain,” from Multiple Choice Questions in Pain Management.8 This chapter presents 48 multiple-choice questions, each featuring 5 true or false options, cumulatively comprising 240 distinct queries. These are crafted to enhance education and training for, for example, the Fellowship of the Faculty of Pain Medicine of the Royal College of Anaesthetists (FFPMRCA) examination. Therefore, we aimed to (1) evaluate the accuracy of responses from GPT-3.5, which is free and one of the most widely used AI models globally, and GPT-4, a paid version, in answering 240 specific queries about neuropathic pain; and (2) assess the consistency of responses from GPT-4 by readministering the same queries in 2 separate sessions. This study was conducted in June 2024 at the Pain Management Department of Institut Guttmann Neurorehabilitation Hospital. All responses were generated by the AI models in situ. The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. In line with standard methodological practices for assessing AI chatbots,9 we formatted a prompt structure that included the questions’ text and the multiple-choice options separated by a new line. Authors A.G.-R. and D.S.-P. prompted each question to GPT-3.5 and GPT-4.0 one at a time, registering each answer in an Excel sheet (Microsoft). We used a scoring system to evaluate GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 on the set of 48 multiple-choice questions, each with 5 true/false options. Scores were assigned on the basis of the number of correctly identified options: 0 for none correct, 1 for one correct, and so on, up to 5 for all correct. Each model’s total score was the sum of scores across all questions, quantitatively assessing its accuracy in responding to the questions. To pinpoint concepts that might prompt incorrect responses from GPT-4, we analyzed questions for which the model achieved a score ≤1. This involved identifying key words or phrases within these questions that represent their central concepts. No prompt was provided as a reply to any of the chatbots in any case. For validating consistency, GPT-4 was prompted again with the complete sequence of questions in a separate session, with each answer registered in an Excel sheet. Our overall results, presented in Table 1, show that GPT-3.5 achieved an accuracy rate of 57.92%, with 139 correct answers out of 240 queries. GPT-4 reached an accuracy rate of 67.5%, with 162 correct responses. Contribution of scores obtained in questions to the total achieved score. The “Score” column indicates the number of options correctly answered as true or false within a single question. A score of 0 indicated that none of the 5 options was correctly identified as true or false; a score of 1 meant that 1 of the 5 options was correctly identified as true or false, and so forth. The “No. of questions” column represents the total number of questions for which the AI model achieved the corresponding score. The “Total score contribution” column is calculated by multiplying the score by the number of questions at that score. This column shows the cumulative contribution of each score level to the overall score of the model. Contribution of scores obtained in questions to the total achieved score. The “Score” column indicates the number of options correctly answered as true or false within a single question. A score of 0 indicated that none of the 5 options was correctly identified as true or false; a score of 1 meant that 1 of the 5 options was correctly identified as true or false, and so forth. The “No. of questions” column represents the total number of questions for which the AI model achieved the corresponding score. The “Total score contribution” column is calculated by multiplying the score by the number of questions at that score. This column shows the cumulative contribution of each score level to the overall score of the model. As detailed in Table 1, GPT-3.5 scored ≤2 in 16 questions, specifically achieving scores of 0 in 2 questions, 1 in 4 questions, and 2 in 10 questions. In contrast, GPT-4 scored ≤2 in 10 questions, achieving scores of 1 in 3 questions and 2 in 7 questions. We then focused on the 3 questions for which GPT-4 scored a 1, aiming to pinpoint key words associated with its challenges. The identified key words for these questions were as follows: Question 12: neuropathy, extremities, numbness, reflexes, fibre, and ganglion; Question 32: spinal cord injury, musculoskeletal, short-lived, incomplete lesions, psychological factors, and muscle spasm; and Question 46: central pain, thalamic, anterior cingulate, cerebral blood flow, excitatory amino acids, and sodium channels. We subsequently analyzed the consistency of GPT-4. In the second iteration, GPT-4 experienced 75 modifications across 240 responses, indicating a change rate of 31.25%. Table 2 details these variations, showing that no changes were noted in 14 questions, 1 change occurred in 12 questions, 2 changes in 9 questions, 3 changes in 7 questions, and 4 changes in 6 questions. Consistency of GPT-4 from first to second iteration. Consistency of GPT-4 from first to second iteration. According to our knowledge, this study represents the first systematic assessment of ChatGPT’s reliability and consistency in neuropathic pain management education. In one of the few studies examining GPT in pain management, GPT-3.5 achieved 65% accuracy in formulating low back pain guidelines, yet it often referenced incorrect or insufficient evidence.10 Mihalache and colleagues observed that GPT-4’s accuracy on the USMLE ranged from 82% to 90%7; however, it is crucial to note that although the USMLE covers aspects of pain management, it does not evaluate pain management specifically. Our study shows that the free version (GPT-3.5) did not reach a 60% accuracy rate, underscoring a potential drawback for users unable to afford the licensed version. This highlights a major challenge in democratizing access to advanced educational tools, as those unable to afford sophisticated versions face significant educational limitations. Despite the improved performance observed with the licensed version of GPT-4, which reached a 67.5% accuracy rate, there remains a notable level of inconsistency, as evidenced by a 31.25% change rate across responses in 2 subsequent iterations. Such a high level of variability is particularly concerning in educational contexts, where trust and consistency are paramount. Despite the rapid evolution of AI models, the issue of biased results remains a significant concern, particularly in educational contexts where the accuracy of information is crucial. Biases in AI-generated content can stem from the data used for training these models, which might inadvertently reflect historical or societal biases. Formal validation is one effective method to reduce bias in AI models. This can help identify specific areas in which the AI might be underperforming or showing skewed results. Using formal validation as a tool to reduce bias is crucial, but it is also important to combine this with other strategies, like diversifying training data and implementing ethics guidelines, to ensure comprehensive bias mitigation. We acknowledge that the exclusion of GPT-3.5 from consistency validation in our study represents a limitation. Including GPT-3.5 could have provided a comparative baseline, enhancing the robustness of our findings by allowing for direct comparisons between different versions of the AI models. Future studies should consider including several AI models in consistency testing to explore significant differences. We understand that the use of true/false questions does not capture broader educational aspects, such as critical thinking, problem-solving, and the ability to synthesize complex information, which are crucial in medical education. As such, our findings are best viewed as an initial step toward understanding the potential roles AI can play in educational contexts. Moving forward, we advocate for the inclusion of more complex assessment types, such as case studies or essay-based questions, in evaluating AI tools. These formats can better simulate real-world applications and deeper understanding, providing a more holistic view of an AI’s educational utility. Thus, although our present study focuses on the binary accuracy of ChatGPT in a controlled test environment, it can provide an initial step and launch broader discussion on more comprehensive future studies that could incorporate these diverse educational assessments. None declared. Conflicts of interest: None declared.
