PMID- 36685925
TI  - Editorial: Artificial intelligence for extracting phenotypic features and disease subtyping applied to single-cell sequencing data
FAU - Mallik S
FAU - Mukhopadhyay A
FAU - Li A
FAU - Odom GJ
FAU - Tomar N.
DP  - 2023
AID - 10.3389/fgene.2022.1083719 [doi]
AB  - In this current era, single-cell sequencing mechanism provides huge number of various kinds of omics data such as epigenomic, genomic or transcriptomic data profiles consisting of a lot of individual cells in the parallel-basis. For inferring the actual biological knowledge and constructing various efficient and robust predictive models from these biological data, new artificial intelligence (AI) and related methods such as soft computing-based frameworks are increasingly proposed and applied to those data that yield better accuracy, flexibility, user-friendliness as well as scalability. The motto of this research topic is to provide the opportunity to the computational biologists and biomedical researchers from around the world to contribute with their talent, idea and experience towards the various problems and their potential possible solutions in the sphere of computational research in next generation sequencing data especially, single cell sequencing data for numerous complex diseases (Mallik and Zhao) [1].Our research topic includes seven high-quality manuscripts from covering a range of topics in Artificial Intelligence (AI) and single-cell sequencing. Here traditional topics in transcriptomics, such as gene-gene interactions, cell type detection, cell-cell interactions, biomarker discovery, disease classification, disease subtyping and clinical diagnosis, are explored with the advanced translational twist. More emphases are provided to sort out complex real-life medically relevant problems and disorders. Altogether, these manuscripts present kinds of cutting-edge subjects in single-cell sequencing, suggesting that single-cell sequencing data analysis has an increasingly significant roles in advancing disease medical application of computational biology. In addition, it has been observed that new artificial intelligence algorithms are being applied to analysis of single-cell sequencing data to resolve multiple problems.In our Research Topic, Ligand-Receptor (LR) Hunting methodology employing Random Forest classifier model was developed to discover cell-cell interactions based on single-cell sequencing (scRNA-seq) gene expression data (Lu et al.) [2]. LR Hunting discovered the validated interactions between the myeloid cells and CD4+ T cells in Triple Negative Breast Cancer (TNBC). A convolutional neural network (CNN)-based framework for subtype identification was proposed in which both the transcriptome and methylome profiles had been utilized to develop the internal classification framework, that clearly separate three subtypes of Glioblastoma (GBM) with higher classification accuracy (Munquad et al.) [3]. Subtype-specific predictive biomarkers had been identified that are generally applicable to efficient disease diagnosis and treatment (Munquad et al.) [3]. Another work was the development of a regularized multi-task learning (RMTL) based framework for the simultaneously prediction of the subpopulation related to a specified cell type (Upadhyay and Ray) [4]. The regularization strategy had been utilized to modulate the multi-task model, smoothing the loss function and thus minimizing the time complexity of the model. Artificial neural network (ANN)-based deep learning model was proposed to classify breast cancer samples where the performance of various well-known classifiers or related strategies had been evaluated and compared (Jia et al.) [5]. Dimensionality reduction can resolve various major challenges to some extent in high dimensional scRNA-seq data. Remembering this, a comprehensive analysis of cluster-specific frequent biomarkers through the consecutive utilization of two strategies (viz., dimensionality reduction and Louvain hierarchical agglomerative clustering) had been identified from scRNA-seq data (Seth et al.) [6].Differential network analysis can be able to learn how gene-gene interactions will change along with different biological conditions. A sparse hierarchical Bayesian factor model was developed for scRNA-seq data from different biological conditions, while the methodology utilized a latent factor structure to make impact on the gene expression for cells for helping account for the zero-inflation as well as high cell-to-cell variability (Sekula et al.) [7]. Another computational framework was to investigate the molecular interaction techniques in melanoma where a melanoma-specific cell-cell interaction network was built, and a consensus clustering based on network embedding had been applied (Wang et al.) [8].Moreover, it has been observed that majority of the published works in this research topic basically cover drop-out finding, cell-cell interactions, cancer classification model, gene regulatory network inference, cluster-specific frequent biomarker discovery and multimodal data integration. The developed software tools or methods facilitate the interpretation of the multi-omics data profiles that can improve both the industrial and academic sectors. Finally, it is expected that upcoming computational tools will surely be benevolent for biomedical researchers to understand the biomedical application of Artificial Intelligence along with enhancing the quality of research in complex disease detection and solving other related clinical hurdles.
