PMID- 38308026
TI  - Letter to the Editor regarding How can artificial intelligence enhance the role of CT in arrhythmia management?
FAU - Khan AA
FAU - Khan MA
FAU - Cohen C.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/bjr/tqad031 [doi]
AB  - Dear Editor, Thank you for publishing the recent Cundari et al1 article centred around the role of cardiac CT scans to help guide interventional treatment for the management of arrhythmias. Arrhythmias range in severity; some manifest as no symptoms, while others can cause sudden death.2 As discussed in the original article, atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most common arrhythmia, and the prevalence is expected to double within the next 50 years.3 AF can precipitate various different complications including increasing the risk of stroke 5-fold and doubling the risk of death.4 Consequently, it is clear that arrhythmias, particular AF, need to be carefully managed to help protect and improve the quality of life of individuals suffering from these heart abnormalities. The discussion around the benefit of imaging to assess the composition of structures within the heart aiding the selection of the most appropriate catheter for ablation therapy, was particularly interesting especially as this would benefit patients with AF. Reading the original article, we recognized how imaging can help pioneer the way in which arrhythmias can be managed. Given the advancements of artificial intelligence (AI), we feel that this combined with imaging can help detect, prevent, and treat arrhythmias such as AF whilst also maximizing efficiency and saving time of clinicians. Deep learning, a subtype of AI, when used in conjunction with MRI scans can be used to help estimate atrial wall thickness, which can help develop knowledge of atrial remodelling, ultimately allowing for greater understanding and development in the management of AF.5 With the rise in prevalence of AF, it is important to monitor and manage those who have already been diagnosed with the condition. AI has the potential to help detect patients who are at greater risk of recurrence following a catheter ablation procedure.6 This demonstrates the power of AI and how it can be used in conjunction with imaging to enhance the treatment of AF. A meta-analysis conducted suggests late gadolinium enhancement cardiac MRI helps to predict ventricular tachyarrhythmia’s, illustrating the value of radiological imaging in supporting the management of arrhythmias.7 Although human analysis of the results requires a great deal of focus and is time-consuming, machine learning analysis has been shown to be both accurate and time efficient.8 The benefit of MRI’s in tackling the challenges of arrhythmias is undeniable given the studies discussed already. However, it is also worth appreciating the role of CT imaging and how this can also shape the management of heart abnormalities. Kowalewski et al9 aimed to understand whether fully automated CT scans could help to predict ventricular arrhythmias in patients who had previously suffered from a myocardial infarction. The study suggested that automated CT scan analysis of post-myocardial infarction scar can help improve identifying those at risk of ventricular tachycardia’s, particularly through focussing on the area of left ventricular wall thinning.9 Despite there being advantages of adopting AI within clinical medicine using radiological imaging, particularly in response to combating rising rates of arrhythmias, it is important to be aware of some of the limitations within this novel piece of technology. The algorithm generated through AI will potentially find it challenging to interpret every image generated ultimately making it difficult for the technology to be accurate every single time. However, through identifying these uncertainties, with time and more data, AI models will eventually improve their accuracy rendering them as indispensable for treating medical conditions. AI also runs the risk of exacerbating bias and prejudice which already exists against those from ethnic minority groups due to the algorithm mimicking patterns from past data where individuals from these groups have been underrepresented.10 To counter this, unbiased new data needs to be collected for AI to pass accurate judgement aiding treatment for patients. To conclude, we feel that a combination of using AI alongside radiological imaging and techniques can play an instrumental role in improving understanding and management of arrhythmias. Whilst this is a relatively new area of medicine, if further emphasis is placed on researching this field, it could redefine and improve the quality of life of those suffering from arrhythmias. None declared. None declared.
