DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/eurjpc/zwae015 [doi]
AB  - This editorial refers to ‘Development of deep-learning models for real-time anaerobic threshold and peak VO2 prediction during cardiopulmonary exercise testing’, by T. Watanabe et al., https://doi.org10.1093/eurjpc/zwad375. For more than 50 years, the concept of anaerobic threshold (AT) has been characterized by several controversies about its existence, meaning, definition, and even its name.1 Anaerobic threshold has been always linked to lactic acid production, which existence was first reported in 1780 by Carl W. Scheele (please see the history of lactic acid in Poole et al.1). Originally, AT was reported as the Owles point, a term that was introduced around 1930.2 Finally, Wasserman and McIlroy3 first reported the term AT in 1964. The hypothesis regarding AT, which led to the name AT, was based on the concept of insufficient oxygen delivery to the working muscle and consequent lactic acid production to overcome what was believed as oxygen delivery deficit. However, in a normal subject, oxygen uptake (VO2) during an incremental exercise increases linearly throughout exercise, even above AT. Indeed, we now have evidence indicating that there is no muscle hypoxia at AT and increased production and utilization of lactate is associated to the increased glycolytic flux. There is debate even about how to call the point when there is a misalignment between VO2 and carbon dioxide production (VCO2). Several names have been proposed, such as AT, lactic threshold, and ventilatory threshold. In our opinion, all three are wrong or at least misleading. The first, as above stated, is wrong because we know that there is no hypoxia during exercise at sea level in normal subjects; the term lactic acid threshold is wrong because lactic acid increases progressively during exercise, and, indeed, to identify a threshold, we need a logarithmic transformation of both VO2 and lactic acid values (Figure 1). Finally, also the term ventilatory threshold is misleading because in the AT definition by the V-slope method (see below), ventilation (VE) is not considered and actually a difference between V-slope and VE-derived methods (ventilatory equivalent and end-tidal pressure methods) has been reported in 10% of normal subjects and in 40–60% of heart failure patients.4–6 Indeed, AT is identified by the ventilatory equivalent method when VE/VO2 rises and VE/VCO2 remains flat and by the end-tidal method when end-tidal pO2 increases and end-tidal pCO2 is flat. We now recognize that the V-slope method identifies the so-called metabolic AT, while the ventilatory equivalent and end-tidal pressure-based methods identify the ventilatory AT. The former is related to what happens in the working muscles and the latter is related to the ventilatory response to increased CO2. An example of dual AT is reported in Figure 2. At present, it seems to us that the term ‘gas exchange threshold’ proposed by Poole et al. is the only scientifically accurate designation, albeit we still use the original AT name. Lactic acid vs. VO2 increasing during exercise (upper panel) and after log transformation (lower panel) to identify anaerobic threshold. AT, anaerobic threshold; VO2, oxygen uptake. On the left, the response of a healthy subject to an incremental work rate test and the three methods to calculate the anaerobic threshold are presented. The anaerobic threshold is clearly detectable, and it shows the same value with all methods, from top to bottom: V-slope method, ventilatory equivalent method, and the end-tidal oxygen tension (PetO2) and end-tidal carbon dioxide tension (PetCO2) method. On the right, the response of another healthy subject to an incremental work rate test is shown. In this case, the anaerobic threshold value, detected with the three different methods, has given two different measures. Specifically, the anaerobic threshold value detected with the V-slope method (met anaerobic threshold) (upper graph) is different from the anaerobic threshold value calculated using the ventilatory equivalent methods and the PetO2 and PetCO2 methods (vent anaerobic threshold). AT, anaerobic threshold; HR, heart rate; VE/VCO2, ventilatory equivalent for carbon dioxide; VE/VO2, ventilatory equivalent for oxygen (Rovai S. et al. Eur J of Prev Cardiol 2022; 29, 502–509 with permission). Professor Karlmann Wasserman taught to a multitude of fellows that to properly measure AT by the V-slope method, you need a squared graph with the same scale on both axes, with VCO2 on the vertical axis (Y-axis) and VO2 on the horizontal (X-axis) axis and a squared triangle (Figure 3). When the hypotenuse shows, the change in slope of the VCO2 vs. VO2 that is your AT. The ventilatory methods were used as a confirmation of AT,7 albeit even Karlmann Wasserman reported the existence of subjects who tolerated high pCO2 values in the arterial tree, which he called non-responder, i.e. reduced ventilatory response to CO2 stimulus. Professor Karlmann Wasserman’s suggestion for properly measure of anaerobic threshold using a square triangle on a graph with the same scale on both axes: VCO2 on the Y-axis and VO2 on the X-axis. S1 the VCO2/VO2 ratio is <1 and >1 at S2. AT, anaerobic threshold; VCO2, carbon dioxide production; VO2, oxygen uptake. The AT plays a relevant clinical role, being associated to severity and prognosis of several diseases such as pulmonary hypertension and heart failure or used to guide exercise training and rehabilitation programmes. However, in many cases, despite an increase in lactic acid, AT is not identifiable. Indeed, the presence of AT implies a homogenous oxygen delivery to the working muscles, a homogenous resistance to oxygen flow from the capillary to the mitochondria, and oxygen utilization by the muscle. If this is not the case, then AT is not identifiable as it happens in 10% of heart failure patients. The absence of an identifiable AT is an indicator of heart failure severity.8–10 The scientific community has even debated how to express the AT. Indeed, previously AT was reported as an absolute value, as a percentage of predicted peak VO2, or as a percentage of observed peak VO2. Only very recently Salvioni et al.11 clarified that VO2 at AT, both expressed as absolute value and as percentage of predicted peak, effectively stratifies the population in terms of prognosis, while VO2 AT as percentage of observed peak VO2 does not. The study concludes that reporting AT as percentage of predicted is the most effective method, exhibiting prognostic power comparable with peak VO2, especially in patients with severe heart failure. In the present issue of EJPC, Watanabe et al.12 propose a new real-time method to identify AT during exercise. This method utilizes artificial intelligence and is based on the differences in kinetic changes of several ventilatory parameters. This is certainly useful and represents a relevant step forward to a large use of cardiopulmonary exercise test even among non-experts. So, our congratulations for this achievement to our Japanese colleagues. However, as we stated above, the concept of AT is very challenging and it is not clear to us what occurs in the case of dual AT, i.e. when both metabolic and ventilatory AT are present. Consequently, two aspects are imperative in our opinion: (i) continuous improvement and work for the artificial intelligence that needs to be fed with new data and (ii) always supervise artificial intelligence results avoiding blind acceptance of outcome. No new data were used for this editorial.
