FAU - Lee JH
FAU - Jang HY
FAU - Park SH.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.3348/kjr.2025.0845 [doi]
AB  - most of our authors and editorial members are non-native English speakers.English remains the dominant language in scientific publishing, creating barriers for non-native English speakers [1].Recent advances in generative artificial intelligence (AI), especially large language models (LLMs), offer promising tools to help overcome language-related barriers in academic communication [2,3].Now, these tools can generate English-language podcast-style audio recordings directly from academic article content.For example, Google's NotebookLM (https://notebooklm.google) can generate such audio directly, while platforms like Google's AI Studio (https://aistudio.google.com)and the opensource Nari Dia models (https://github.com/nari-labs/dia)streamline the tasks of generating scripts and subsequently synthesizing multi-speaker audio.Furthermore, such software tools are likely to become increasingly available. Introducing KJR SummaryCastWith these backgrounds, in response to suggestions from several authors, KJR is launching an experimental initiative: KJR SummaryCast, now available on the KJR YouTube channel (https://www.youtube.com/@koreanjournalofradiology2259).Examples are currently available.Currently, unlike traditional journal podcasts, KJR plans to offer KJR SummaryCast through authors' engagement.
