AB  - Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD) is increasingly recognized in the aesthetic practice, yet it remains underdiagnosed and often misunderstood. With its high prevalence, particularly in cosmetic consultations, BDD poses significant ethical and clinical challenges. Aesthetic providers must be vigilant in identifying at-risk individuals and prioritizing psychological well-being alongside procedural outcomes. Artificial Intelligence (AI), with its capacity to analyze behavioral patterns, automate screening tools, and detect subtle indicators of cognitive distortion, presents a new frontier in managing BDD. However, integrating AI into clinical practice requires caution to prevent reinforcing appearance-focused biases and to ensure privacy and fairness. This commentary discusses the opportunities, limitations, and ethical considerations of leveraging AI to assist clinicians in detecting BDD, fostering safer patient outcomes, and advancing the compassionate practice of aesthetic medicine. AI should not accelerate aesthetic procedures but promote reflective, ethically sound decision-making. When integrated responsibly, it can enhance recognition of BDD, support psychological safety, and preserve patient trust through transparency, data protection, and clinician oversight.
