PMID- 31910752
TI  - Good news: Artificial intelligence in psychiatry is actually neither.
FAU - Simon GE
FAU - Yarborough BJ.
DP  - 2020
AID - 10.1176/appi.ps.201900464 [doi]
AB  - Discussions about artificial intelligence in healthcare have raised concerns about the dehumanization of healing relationships(1). Reliance on “big data” to inform treatment decisions might lead to ignoring experiences and values that cannot be reduced to discrete data elements. Computer-generated recommendations may carry a false authority that would override expert human judgement. Concerns regarding the disruptive effects of artificial intelligence on clinical practice, however, probably reflect more marketing hype than nearterm clinical reality. When we consider actual uses of big data and machine learning in mental health care, the term “artificial intelligence” is usually a misnomer.
