PMID- 34153987
TI  - The Universal Definition of Heart Failure, risk prediction in cardiogenic shock, artificial intelligence in cardiac allograft rejection, and the genetics of dilated cardiomyopathy
FAU - Crea F.
DP  - 2021
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehab370 [doi]
AB  - This Focus Issue on heart failure (HF) and cardiomyopathies contains a Special Article entitled 'The struggle towards a Universal Definition of Heart Failure: how to proceed?'. 1 In a first part, Johann Bauersachs from the Hannover Medical School in Germany provides an introduction to the topic overall. He notes that definitions and classifications are crucial in medicine and provide the basis for a proper and timely diagnosis to enable prompt, precise, and efficient therapies. The diagnosis of HF currently relies on signs (pulmonary crackles and peripheral oedema) and symptoms (dyspnoea on exertion and fatigue) that are not always objective, and neither sensitive nor specific. 2-4 Subsequently, Professor John Cleland from the University of Glasgow in the UK, and colleagues, take the stance that a universal definition of HF is needed. The concept of a universal definition of HF proposed by Cleland et al., comprising cardiac dysfunction (identified by imaging) and congestion (identified by natriuretic peptide measurement) (Figure In their counterstatement, Pfeffer and Teerlink from Harvard Medical School in Boston, MA, USA point out several important caveats precluding the simple introduction of natriuretic peptide elevation for HF diagnosis.
