PMID- 40795818
TI  - Reliability of large language models for reviewing research with artificial intelligence in cardiac electrophysiology using the European Heart Rhythm Association artificial intelligence checklist
FAU - Sluha A
FAU - Hillmann HAK
FAU - Svennberg E
FAU - Duncker D.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1093/europace/euaf173 [doi]
AB  - The potential of artificial intelligence (AI) in cardiac electrophysiology (EP) is expanding.1,2 To standardize AI reporting in EP, enhance research quality, and improve reader understanding, the European Heart Rhythm Association (EHRA) AI checklist was recently published.3 It was validated through an expert panel by applying studies from three different EP domains to the checklist: atrial fibrillation (AF) management, sudden cardiac death (SCD), and AI application in the EP lab. The validation process revealed gaps in reporting AI research and highlighted the need to improve transparency and reproducibility to successfully integrate tools into clinical settings. To achieve these goals, the authors recommended a broader implementation of the checklist,3 including its application to future research and the integration into peer review processes. The aim of this study was to explore whether large language models (LLMs) could assist in analysing and evaluating scientific manuscripts based on the EHRA AI reporting framework.3 In May 2025, we conducted a study using a LLM (ChatGPT, GPT-4o, OpenAI, San Francisco, USA) to analyse all studies used for the validation of the EHRA AI checklist3 and to evaluate these by applying the checklist items. The generated LLM responses were subsequently compared with those provided by the expert panel. Members of the EHRA AI in EP writing group were nominated by the EHRA Scientific documents committee and contributed to the creation of the checklist itself and applied AI-related studies to the checklist during validation. As an initial step, we uploaded the checklist3 and queried ChatGPT about its ability to process the pdf file of the published manuscript. After ensuring that each item of the checklist3 could accurately be extracted by the LLM, we additionally uploaded a publication and asked the LLM to create its own prompt. The prompt was then further refined by us through iterative prompt engineering. For the main analysis, each study from the EHRA AI manuscript together with the checklist3 was successively uploaded to the LLM. For each analysis, the previously developed prompt was applied without modification and was structured as follows: (i) asking the LLM to act as a reviewer in clinical EP, (ii) short explanation of the two uploaded files stating that one pdf is a published AI-based study in EP or cardiology and the other file is the EHRA AI checklist of 29 items,3 (iii) a description of the task to decide for each of the 29 items of the checklist3 whether it is ‘applicable’ or ‘not applicable’, (iv) a description of the desired output format: table with the two columns ‘Checklist Item’ and ‘Applicability’ and to colour code the answers with green (fulfilled) or red (not addressed/missing/not applicable), and (v) additional rules such as not to change or remove any item from the list3 and not to generate any text outside the table. All 55 manuscripts from the EHRA AI paper,3 including 31 studies in AF management, 18 studies in SCD, and 6 studies in AI application in the EP lab were analysed by the LLM. Results of the initial LLM assessment compared to the initial expert assessment are shown in Figure 1. (A) Visualized summary of the study. (B) Comparison of human and LLM assessment: rows show the evaluated papers from the EHRA AI manuscript.3 Columns show the item of the checklist. Each cell is divided into two: the left side shows the reviewer’s evaluation, and the right side shows the LLM’s evaluation. Items are rated as ‘applicable’ (green) or ‘not applicable’ (red). Inconsistencies are visually highlighted by darker shades of green/red. AF, atrial fibrillation; AI, artificial intelligence; EHRA, European Heart Rhythm Association; EP, electrophysiology; LLM, large language model; SCD, sudden cardiac death. Each manuscript was evaluated for all 29 checklist items, resulting in a total of 1595 item ratings by the LLM. In the AF section, LLM ratings of the items differed in 24% (n = 211) of 899 LLM ratings compared to the human reviewer’s assessment. In the SCD section, inconsistencies between the LLM and the human reviewer occurred in 28% (n = 146) of 522 ratings. In the AI in EP lab domain, 20% (n = 34) of 174 ratings were inconsistent. Per manuscript, none of the LLM results matched the human results completely, and the mean number of inconsistencies between human and LLM rating was 7 ± 2.8. Per EHRA AI checklist item,3 only Item 11, which refers to the description of the data type used in the study, was rated identically to the human reviewer for every manuscript. Per checklist item,3 the mean number of inconsistencies among all papers was 13 ± 9.0. In this study, we evaluated an LLM’s capabilities to review AI-related scientific research manuscripts based on the EHRA AI checklist.3 The main findings were as follows: (i) approximately one quarter of ratings by the LLM across all three EP domains differed from human ratings; (ii) none of the 55 manuscripts were rated in the same way as the human reviewer; and (iii) only Item 11 was rated consistently without any deviation from human assessment. These results underscore that regardless of EP domain, the LLM could not mimic human assessment with sufficient reliability. The only exception was Item 11, which describes the used data type or source in the studies. It can be observed that among the human evaluations in the EHRA AI manuscript, only Item 11 was reported across all 55 reviewed publications.3 No other item from the checklist achieved this level of consistency. This suggests that researchers tend to describe their used data sources clearly and without ambiguity, likely facilitating information identification and extraction for LLMs. The inconsistencies between human and artificial reviewer across all other items indicate that the LLM currently still lacks capacity to accurately evaluate more complex or variably reported content. Previous studies have nevertheless shown that, especially when pre-trained, LLMs can add a benefit to clinical processes and increase productivity.4,5 They can be utilized for purposes such as mortality risk stratifications, assistance in disease diagnosis, or analysis of clinical data.6–8 In accordance to other studies, our results suggested that while LLMs show promising results for some tasks, their generated outputs cannot be fully trusted yet.9,10 The LLM analysis of manuscripts based on a checklist3 was unreliable, indicating that LLMs cannot currently be utilized for reviewing processes without expert oversight. However, our results may be limited because we used only one LLM for the analyses. Other LLMs, as well as other prompts, could have achieved different results. Standards for reporting and understanding AI in EP are important. Although LLMs can help with the evaluation and analysis of scientific manuscripts using frameworks like the previously published EHRA AI checklist,3 there are currently still limitations in terms of reliability. Therefore, human intelligence is still required to ensure compliance with standards. The data underlying this article will be shared on reasonable request to the corresponding author.
