PMID- 39925037
TI  - Using Facial Skin Artificial Intelligence (AI) to Capture Atopic Dermatitis Patients' Response to Therapy: A Single‐Center Prospective Study
FAU - Parraga SP
FAU - Duong JQ
FAU - Agner ML
FAU - Shanmugam SK
FAU - Taylor SL
FAU - Feldman SR.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1111/ijd.17688 [doi]
AB  - Atopic dermatitis (AD) commonly affects the face. Clinical features of AD include pruritus, erythema, and scaly skin [1, 2]. Patients may have lower eyelid involvement, known as Dennie-Morgan lines. Eyelids can become thickened, dark, and wrinkled [3]. Facial skin artificial intelligence (AI) systems can help monitor AD progression through analysis of objective, quantitative data on facial characteristics that may assist clinical evaluation. We evaluated the validity of facial AI technology in detecting changes in facial AD following clinical treatment. Upon institutional review board approval, patients were recruited from Wake Forest Baptist Health's dermatology clinics from 2023 to 2024. Patients began an individualized treatment plan tailored to the severity of their AD symptoms. Inclusion criteria included ≥ 11 years of age, diagnosis of AD, and Fitzpatrick skin type (FST) I-VI. Exclusion criteria included facial tattoos, sunburns, or significant facial scarring. Images were captured using an iPad-based portable facial imaging device with a frontal facing camera (AI Skincare, Perfect Skincare Corporation Pro) and a “gold-standard” computer-based facial imaging device (VISIA Complexion Analysis Model Generation 7, Canfield Scientific Inc.). Both instruments provided raw numerical scores for facial characteristics. Lower scores indicated fewer desirable characteristics. The primary endpoint was the redness score. Changes in under-eye characteristics served as secondary endpoints. Scores for wrinkles, eyebags, and dark circles were recorded. Changes in scores were calculated from a baseline visit and one follow-up visit occurring 4 to 8 weeks after treatment initiation. The Pearson correlation coefficient was calculated to determine statistically significant correlations in scoring for facial characteristics analyzed by both devices (redness and wrinkles). An intraclass correlation coefficient and Cronbach's alpha measure of internal consistency were used to compare the inter-reliability of the two devices and a physician's assessments. Ten patients were recruited with a mean age of 15 years (range: 11–24). Participants consisted of females (50%) and males (50%) and self-identified as Asian (10%), Black (30%), and White (60%). Patients had FST I-III (n = 6) and IV-VI (n = 4). AI Skincare mean baseline and final raw scores for facial redness were 81 (standard deviation [SD] 16) and 87 (12), respectively (p = 0.046, Figure 1A). Changes from baseline to final scores for the secondary outcomes were not statistically significant; scores for wrinkles improved the most from pre- to post-treatment: 87 (10) and 89 (10), respectively (p = 0.090, Figure 1B). Computer AI mean facial redness scores at baseline and follow-up visit were 48 (30) and 51 (29), respectively (p < 0.001); wrinkles scores changed from 45 (25) to 51 (18) (p = 0.910). The correlation between the portable and computer AI scores was strongly positive (r2 = 0.71, p = 0.049). Physician agreement with the portable facial AI was highest for facial redness (84% agreement; p = 0.030) and eye bags (76% agreement; p = 0.840). Both devices captured changes in facial redness and under-eye involvement in patients with AD after receiving treatment. Scoring variability between the two devices may arise from differences in technology, algorithms, and their intended application. The portable AI focuses on accessibility and convenience for general users, which allows images to be captured in diverse lighting and framing conditions. This approach differs from traditional computer AI devices intended for controlled clinical settings. A portable facial AI may be useful in clinical trials and clinical practice to assess treatment response in AD quantitatively; however, the small sample size and lack of stratification by FST in this study are potential limitations to drawing definitive conclusions. This technology can permit patients to track the improvement of their AD facial characteristics objectively. Obtaining patient images using portable facial detection software at baseline and interval appointments may be valuable to personalize care, enhance patient satisfaction, and improve outcomes [4]. Reviewed and approved by the Institutional Review Board of Wake Forest University; IRB#00085220. Consent for the publication of recognizable patient photographs or other identifiable material was obtained by the authors and included at the time of article submission to the journal, stating that all patients gave consent with the understanding that this information may be made publicly available. Dr. Feldman has received research, speaking, and/or consulting support from AbbVie, Accordant, Almirall, Alvotech, Amgen, Arcutis, Arena, Argenx, Biocon, Boehringer Ingelheim, Bristol-Myers Squibb, Dermavant, Eli Lilly and Company, Eurofins, Forte, Galderma, Helsinn, Janssen, Leo Pharma, Micreos, Mylan, Novartis, Ono, Ortho Dermatology, Pfizer, Regeneron, Samsung, Sanofi, Sun Pharma, UCB, Verrica, Voluntis, and vTv Therapeutics. He is the founder and part owner of Causa Research and holds stock in Sensal Health. The other authors have no conflicts of interest to declare.
