PMID- 39657699
TI  - ASHP/ASHP Foundation 2025 Pharmacy Forecast Report: Taking a seat at every table
FAU - Cobaugh DJ.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1093/ajhp/zxae363 [doi]
AB  - The 2025 ASHP/ASHP Foundation Pharmacy Forecast report,1 published in this issue of AJHP, contains 32 recommendations to guide strategic planning and thinking in hospitals and health systems over the next 5 years. This 13th edition of the Pharmacy Forecast addresses several topics that have been covered previously, including artificial intelligence (AI), healthcare financing, managing high-cost medications, and the pharmacy workforce. The inclusion once again of these topics reflects their complexity and, in the case of AI, its rapid evolution as a potentially disruptive force in healthcare delivery. New to the 2025 Pharmacy Forecast are chapters on pharmacists’ roles in responding to the primary care crisis in the United States and the introduction of the novel concept of whole person health. A close review of the recommendations in the 2025 Pharmacy Forecast elucidates the need for diverse types of leaders to represent the pharmacy enterprise in health systems’ preparation for and response to the potentially momentous changes that are likely to occur over the next 5 years. Along with leadership at the executive level, individuals with expertise in clinical practice and therapeutics, pharmacy and health-system operations, informatics, the business and finance of healthcare, education and training, and advocacy must have a seat at the table where decisions are made. A single recommendation in the business of healthcare chapter—related to determinations about use of selected medications in the home setting—demonstrates this need for diverse types of leadership. The authors recognize that successful implementation of an initiative of this magnitude will require deep clinical acumen to determine which agents can be used safely and effectively in the home, financial modeling capabilities to understand the financial opportunities and risks related to this practice change, and operations expertise to address the logistics of home use of medications typically administered in a healthcare setting. Likewise, the 6 recommendations in the primary care chapter are dependent upon leadership and action by pharmacists with different types of expertise. Recognition of pharmacists as part of the solution to the paucity of primary care providers requires pharmacy executive-level leadership in the c-suite and with key physician leaders. The recommendations that address pharmacists’ scope of practice, uniformity in the primary care process, and increased establishment of collaborative practice agreements demand the expertise of clinicians who understand patients’ medication-related primary care needs. Action on pharmacist reimbursement and optimization of remote pharmacist programs will require both business and informatics expertise. Effective advocacy skills will be required to achieve the final recommendation on partnering with federal agencies to address the primary care crisis by leveraging the capabilities of pharmacists as members of the primary care team. Throughout the 2025 Pharmacy Forecast report, it is clear that success in responding to the recommendations will require diverse pharmacy expertise and leadership. Additional examples from the ultra-high-cost drugs and AI chapters further emphasize this point. The chapter on ultra-high-cost, and ultra-complex, therapies includes recommendations that call for deep clinical knowledge of the underlying diseases, expert-level understanding of emerging therapies, business expertise to address the complex financial implications of these treatments, operational skills to effectively plan for their incorporation to practice, and advocacy to ensure public and private sector solutions aimed at the effective safe and equitable use of these therapies. Likewise, each of the recommendations in the AI chapter requires different types of leadership ranging from executive responsibilities for ensuring pharmacy’s participation in the health system–wide AI agenda to pharmacy informatics expertise and leadership in ensuring that the electronic health record is prepared to incorporate AI. It is also clear from the report that these leaders cannot exist in silos, but rather they must collaborate to produce the desired change. Many of the recommendations in the chapters are interdependent, and the themes of the chapters themselves are crosscutting. There is explicit overlap between the business of healthcare, ultra-high-cost drugs, primary care, and whole person health chapters. Similarly primary care and whole person health draw in workforce and AI themes. This overlap emphasizes that while diverse expertise and leadership will be required to achieve the Pharmacy Forecast 2025 recommendations, it is imperative for pharmacy leaders to partner, and avoid isolation, as they address these highly complex issues. The 2025 ASHP and ASHP Foundation Pharmacy Forecast report demonstrates that pharmacy leaders with diverse types of expertise must have a seat at leadership tables across the health system to ensure the pharmacy enterprise is positioned to prepare for and respond to the complexities that have become inherent to 21st-century healthcare delivery in the United States. Disclosures: Dr. Cobaugh is the senior vice president for professional development and publishing at ASHP and the editor in chief of AJHP.
