PMID- 40213220
TI  - Revolutionizing aneurysm risk prediction: artificial intelligence’s promise and challenges
FAU - Okon II
FAU - Precious FK
FAU - Chaurasia B
FAU - Okon E
FAU - John OO
FAU - Kankam SB
FAU - Akpan UU
FAU - Lucero-Prisno Iii DE.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1097/MS9.0000000000002917 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is an arithmetical method that self-operates intellectual functions and has significant advantages in various medical fields[1]. Recently, AI technology has conveyed favorable outcomes in the operation of preliminary neurological diseases and is foreseen to supply clinicians with counsel, ensuring higher precision and effectiveness at all phases of intracranial aneurysm (IA) administration[2]. AI techniques have captured attention for their ability to recognize designs from a wide illustrative dataset with multiple variables using a thoroughly constructive technique that facilitates the model establishment for computer-controlled prognostic or categorization. Evidence has indicated that AI algorithms are surpassing traditional equivalents in contexts in which data input is plentiful and has the potential for compound interchange. AI has also been used in the classification of aneurysm rupture level with proportionately high accuracy[3]. IA is a widespread cerebrovascular sickness, affecting 3% to 7% of full-grown citizens, and is related to a potentially fatal subarachnoid hemorrhage, leading to high death and dysfunctional rates[4]. In spite of the developments in IA administration, including detection, fracture risk evaluation, treatment option preference, and investigative inspection, it remains heavily reliant on individual clinician experience. The multifaceted challenges associated with aneurysm management cannot be resolved with conventional approaches. Unruptured intracranial aneurysms are regular, with a universal prevalence of 3.2% in adults worldwide. In the past decades, expanding unruptured aneurysms have been detected because of the wide application of CTA and MRA. Particularly, a large number of accidentally detected aneurysms (≤87.6%) have small sizes (<3–4 mm) and are generally without symptoms[5]. To date, small aneurysms account for 35% to 47% of ruptured aneurysms and may inflict a substantial burden on intracranial vessel diseases. The care of sufferers with unruptured small aneurysms remains contended. Some researchers endorsed no inhibitory treatment or imaging follow-up for patients with aneurysms <3 mm based on the confirmation of small yearly development and rupture rates of small aneurysms[6]. Ongoing recommendations from the American Heart Association and American Stroke Association have no concurring belief concerning the management of unruptured aneurysms with small (3–5 mm) and extra-small (≤3 mm) sizes. Thus, it is vital to assess the value of the rupture risk of small aneurysms to obtain optimal clinical policy-making for more advanced therapy and follow-up. Numerous rupture threat factors and constructed scoring systems have been recommended by researchers. The connection of risk factors (such as clinical, morphologic, and hemodynamic parameters) makes the vaticination of aneurysm rupture elaborate, resulting in the dodginess of standard routines such as delivery retrogression. The present-day scoring approach is not strong, mainly for small aneurysms, which have to be portrayed particularly due to their distinctive histologic attributes[7]. Therefore, a new approach is needed to establish rupture-risk representations for small aneurysms to ease clinical resolutions. Aneurysms were frequently treated only if any switch in size or morphology of the aneurysm was recognized during follow-up, which would result in a significant prejudice in the longitudinal study. It is achievable to carry out a prevalence study to distinguish ruptured aneurysms and, additionally, to add relevance to the imitation in predicting the rupture risk of unruptured aneurysms[7,8]. AI has significant potential for predicting aneurysm rupture by analyzing parameters such as medical history, imaging data, genetic predispositions, lifestyle factors, and demography. Its capacity to handle huge data sets quickly allows for early diagnosis and intervention, particularly when spotting small changes in aneurysm morphology or growth rates over time[2]. AI can deliver a thorough risk assessment by combining data from numerous sources and constantly updating predictive models as it learns from fresh information. Furthermore, AI offers personalized risk classification based on individual patient variables such as age, gender, and comorbidities[2,9]. AI models can integrate morphological data and hemodynamic factors into predictive models using machine learning algorithms such as convolutional neural networks, support vector machines, and random forests. These models can be trained on enormous datasets, divided into training and validation sets, and then tested for specificity, sensitivity, and precision[3,10]. According to studies, AI-based prediction systems outperform traditional scoring methods in predicting aneurysm rupture risk. Furthermore, AI-based prediction systems can be constructed based just on clinical factors, allowing for widespread clinical screening without the need for complex imaging examinations. Despite these advances, further validation and enhancement of AI models will be required to assure their dependability and effectiveness in clinical settings[11]. AI confronts difficulties in projecting the probability of aneurysm rupture due to the small sample sizes in previous studies, which necessitate larger datasets and more complete parameters. Multicenter randomized controlled trials are required to test AI models’ prediction abilities. Selection bias and variability in imaging processes are other issues. Variances in algorithm performance can be attributed to variances in training data, machine learning methodologies, and imaging technologies[11]. A prospective study using standardized techniques is recommended for reproducible results and consistent performance. The lack of a diagnostic gold standard reference, as well as the biased inclusion of studies based on specific imaging modalities create further obstacles[12]. In conclusion, the incorporation of AI technology into the medical profession has intriguing potential for improving the prediction of IA rupture risk. Moving forward, it is critical to build on existing projects and streamline procedures to improve the effectiveness of AI-based risk assessments. To address present constraints, the use of sophisticated network topologies and training methods such as convolutional residual networks, active learning, one-shot learning, and generative adversarial networks has great potential. However, further research is needed to properly investigate and understand the capabilities and limits of these techniques for accurately anticipating aneurysm risk[2,11,13].
