PMID- 38321364
TI  - Artificial intelligence and machine learning in rheumatology
FAU - Dubey S
FAU - Chan A
FAU - Adebajo AO
FAU - Walker D
FAU - Bukhari M.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/rheumatology/keae092 [doi]
AB  - One of the big news stories of 2023 is the direct application of artificial intelligence (AI) in medicine—several AI modules have passed the medical licensure examination in the USA, and there is a new AI story every week [1]. In recent years, the use of digital biomarkers and therapeutics, real-time activity monitoring, and remote medicine has revolutionized the field of rheumatology. One key development has been around gathering real-time patient data to better understand disease status. Wearable accelerometry-based activity monitors, for instance, have been used to identify responses to DMARDs in RA [2]. Objective data are particularly important, as there remains significant discordance between self-reported and objective measures. For example, a total knee replacement study found that, post total knee replacement, at 3 months patients were reporting a substantial increase in activity but were only doing 20% more, yet at 6 months they were doing 80% more but reporting less activity [3]. Discordance between self-reported and measured activity indicate that different outcomes were being measured. Accelerometers have been shown to demonstrate differences in activity levels between patients with RA and healthy controls [2]. Such technologies can provide clinicians with valuable insights into patient behaviour, enable continuous measurements rather than single-time-point assessment, predict patterns of disease activity and help inform management decisions. Imaging lends itself particularly nicely to application of AI-based innovations, and AI-based deep-learning models have been successfully applied in OA, RA, AS, bone lesions, tenosynovitis, fibrosis and vasculopathy [4]. Another area of interest is the use of digital biomarkers for detecting risk of disease development, predicting disease outcomes and guiding treatment decisions. Digital biomarkers are objective measures of disease derived from digital health data, such as wearable data, electronic health records, and patient-reported outcomes. Using machine learning (ML), which is an application of AI using mathematical models of data to help a computer learn without direct instruction, can help clinicians identify the presence of inflammatory arthritis and predict disease outcomes. For example, a recent study found that ML algorithms could accurately predict the presence of inflammatory arthritis from analysis of heterogeneous data in primary care, thus facilitating better quality referrals to secondary care [5]. ML algorithms are helping to create novel biomarker profiles that enable diagnosis and mechanistic classification of patients. This enables stratification through linked treatment strategies and personalized care [6, 7]. With the increased use of electronic health records, ML has also been applied to analysing these datasets and identifying potential biases in the algorithms used for prediction of outcomes in patients with rheumatic diseases [8]. ML techniques (such as artificial neural networks, support vector machines, and deep-learning algorithms) offer the potential to analyse large datasets, identify patterns, and make accurate predictions. There is a significant opportunity for ML to improve diagnosis and prognosis through analysis of complex patterns in clinical data, medical imaging, and laboratory results. Rheumatology research has witnessed significant advancements in high-throughput technologies, enabling the generation of multi-omics data. ML algorithms can integrate and analyse these complex datasets to further unravel disease mechanisms, identify novel therapeutic targets critical to the development of the disease, and aid identification of patient subgroups that respond better to specific treatments, enabling personalized treatment strategies [9]. A recent study reported that ML has revealed a biomarker for non-response to MTX [10]. Patient- and data-powered AI opens a realm of possibilities for novel insights and enhancing of research, including rheumatology-related drug discovery, with shorter development times and associated cost savings. Collaborative efforts between researchers, clinicians, funding bodies, and industry stakeholders are critical for harnessing the full potential of ML and AI in rheumatology. While the integration of ML in rheumatology holds great promise, several challenges must be addressed. First, the availability and quality of large and diverse datasets are crucial for developing robust ML models. Patients from ethnic minorities or deprived backgrounds often have poorer outcomes, and there are health inequalities that need addressing through better patient recruitment strategies, collaboration, and data-sharing initiatives. AI can help with real-time monitoring of diversity in research trials and datasets. It has the potential to aid data entry and amalgamate data from primary and secondary care, enabling enhanced data quality. There are challenges around patient engagement, the educational needs of patients, digital exclusion, and access to care, and these could be improved through better technological support. There are significant workforce constraints, and technology can provide some solutions through more efficient systems, reducing clinician burden. Virtual assistants and chatbots can provide patients with real-time access to personalized information, self-management strategies, and answers to common questions. These AI-powered tools can empower patients, improve adherence to treatment plans, and enhance communication between patients and health-care providers. Furthermore, AI technologies such as ChatGPT can facilitate communication such as coding, hospital discharge summaries, clinical letters, and remote monitoring of disease activity, enabling timely interventions and reducing administrative tasks for clinicians [11]. Governance and monitoring of the use of AI technologies, and of the interpretability and transparency of ML models, are essential for gaining the trust of clinicians and patients. Efforts to enhance model interpretability and understanding should be prioritized, to ensure the responsible and ethical deployment of these technologies. There remains the need for standardization of digital health technologies, as the potential for bias in algorithms and data privacy concerns persist. As we have seen with COVID-19 vaccines, winning over sceptics will be key. However, our objectives must be very clear; otherwise, technology will fail and the outcome may not be improved patient care. Despite these challenges, the use of digital health platforms is expected to grow and lead to improved patient outcomes. With the integration of AI technologies, the future of rheumatology services holds great promise. From augmenting clinical decision-making to improving diagnostic accuracy, personalizing treatment strategies, and optimizing patient care, AI has the potential to transform rheumatology practices. However, addressing ethical considerations and safety issues is paramount to ensuring responsible and effective implementation of AI in health care. All data relevant to this article have been included in the text. All listed authors provided substantial contributions to this work. All authors contributed to the critical revision of the manuscript and agreed on the final version. No specific funding was received from any funding body in the public, commercial and not-for profit sectors for this article. Disclosure statement: S.D. has declared receiving honoraria from Janssen and Boehringer Ingelheim. A.C. has declared receiving honoraria from Novartis, Janssen, Abbvie and UCB. A.O.A. and D.W. have declared no conflicts of interest. M.B. has declared receiving sponsorship for regional, national and international meetings from UCB Celltech, Roche/Chugai, Pfizer, Abbvie, Merck, Menarini, Janssen, Bristol-Myers Squib, Novartis and Eli-Lilly; he has received honoraria for speaking from Bristol-Myers Squib, UCB Celltech, Roche/Chugai, Pfizer, Abbvie, Merck, Menarini, Sanofi-Aventis, Eli-Lilly, Janssen, Amgen, Novartis and Gilead and also attended advisory boards for them.
