AB  - The craniovertebral junction (CVJ) is an anatomically complex and surgically challenging region, where critical neurovascular structures converge. Traditional surgical approaches to this area have involved high risks of morbidity due to the intricate anatomy and limited surgical corridors. Recent advancements in minimally invasive techniques, imaging, robotic assistance, and artificial intelligence are revolutionizing CVJ surgery. This article reviews current innovations shaping the future of CVJ surgery, emphasizing improved safety, precision, and patient outcomes. • Minimally invasive and endoscopic techniques are transforming CVJ surgery by reducing morbidity, preserving anatomical structures, and enabling faster recovery. • Advanced intraoperative imaging and navigation systems, including 3D scans and augmented reality, are enhancing surgical accuracy and reducing complication rates. • Robotic-assisted platforms and artificial intelligence are ushering in a new era of precision, safety, and personalized planning in craniovertebral junction procedures. • Despite technological progress, the surgeon’s expertise remains central to interpreting and applying innovations safely within the CVJ’s complex anatomy
