PMID- 40884392
TI  - Performance of artificial intelligence-generated vs human-authored abstracts in a real-world setting
FAU - Kresoja KP
FAU - Schöber AR
FAU - Lüscher T
FAU - Thevathasan T
FAU - Lurz P
FAU - Papoutsis K
FAU - Baldus S
FAU - Blankenberg S
FAU - Hinkel R
FAU - Thiele H.
DP  - 2026
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehaf654 [doi]
AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly evolving and has significant potential to impact modern medicine. Recognizing its impact, international societies, including the European Society of Cardiology (ESC), have launched initiatives such as the committee on Digital Cardiology and Artificial Intelligence1 and, more recently, ESCChat, an AI-enhanced Chatbot containing all ESC Clinical Practice Guidelines. AI-based large language models, with ChatGPT being among the most well-known, are set to transform the way medical literature is written and reviewed.2 The use of ChatGPT in scientific writing has sparked debate and critical discussion,3 prompting major publishers to implement specific policies.4 However, like any innovation, AI can not only be helpful, but also be misused, for instance, to generate and disseminate misinformation or fraudulent scientific work, as seen with paper mills.5 It is excessively difficult even for human experts to distinguish between human written abstracts based on true data or large language-based models such as ChatGPT-manufactured abstracts based on imaginary data. We hypothesized that AI-generated abstracts would not receive lower ratings than human-written abstracts in a real-world setting where reviewers were unaware of their presence and had no prior knowledge of the baseline rate of fraudulent submissions. Additionally, we hypothesized that even the highest-rated AI-generated abstracts could be identified using dedicated detection software. To test this, we aimed to generate ∼10% of all submitted abstracts for the German Cardiac Society Annual Congress using AI. Historical submission data were analyzed to ensure proportional representation across subcategories. The ChatGPT-4o web interface was programmed using a hybrid of ‘instruction’ and ‘few-shot’ prompting to generate fabricated abstracts and data with predefined word counts, structures, and appropriate categories according to abstract submission instructions. Fabricated randomized controlled trials were prohibited from creation to avoid ethical concerns and prevent hypothetical results from being misinterpreted or reported as factual. The AI-generated content, including wording and data, was not modified by the authors. Additionally, ChatGPT’s integrated Python tool was used to generate figures for 50% of the AI-generated abstracts. More details on prompting and all generated abstracts can be found at https://zenodo.org/records/16489919. These abstracts were submitted alongside regular submissions, with all reviewers involved in the abstract assessment blinded to the inclusion of AI-generated content and unaware of the experimental design. After evaluation, all AI-generated abstracts were immediately retracted to prevent any influence on the congress proceedings. The primary outcome measure was the abstract rating on a scale from 1 (lowest) to 5 (highest). Furthermore, the licensed ‘essential’ version of GPTZero (https://gptzero.me), a tool designed to detect AI-generated content, was used to assess the probability of AI-generated text in the 12 highest-rated AI abstracts, as well as in 12 human-authored abstracts that were selected for a ‘Young Investigator Award’ competition during the annual congress in 2025 based on reviewer grading and 12 from 2022 as absolute controls for the same categories, as ChatGPT was not released at the time of abstract submission deadline in 2022. Across 19 categories, a total of 1348 abstracts were submitted. As part of this experiment of those 136 (10%) were deliberately generated by the research team using ChatGPT-4o. Overall, there was no significant difference in ratings between human-authored and AI-generated abstracts [human median 3.3 (IQR 3.0 to 3.7), AI median 3.4, (IQR 2.9 to 3.7); P = .85 Mann–Whitney test, Figure 1). The inclusion of fabricated figures in AI-generated abstracts did not significantly affect their ratings compared with those without figures (P = .57). Performance of artificial intelligence fabricated vs human-authored abstracts. The top panel shows design, findings and potential solution for the presented study which investigated the artificial intelligence vs human-authored abstracts. The table shows the performance of the ChatGPT generated abstracts according to subcategories, with the total number of submitted abstracts, the number and fraction of ChatGPT submitted abstracts, the highest ranked ChatGPT abstract, the lowest ranked ChatGPT abstract and whether ChatGPT or humans had significantly higher scores on overall evaluation. Lastly medals indicate whether ChatGPT received first (gold), second (silver) or third (bronze) best abstracts in the respective categories. Comparison of parametric values was done with Mann–Whitney test. Box plots represent median and corresponding interquartile range, whiskers represent respective minimal and maximal values. NA* no statistical comparison performed due to low number of observations (i.e. n = 2) Performance varied by category: AI-generated abstracts were rated lower than human-authored ones only in rhythmology (P < .001), whereas they were rated significantly higher in cardiovascular imaging (P = .011). AI-generated abstracts ranked highest in six categories, second-highest in three categories, and third-highest in four categories (Figure 1). Two AI-generated abstracts were flagged by reviewers due to suspected prior publication, but no evidence of comparable studies was found. No other AI-generated abstract was flagged as suspicious. GPTZero effectively distinguished AI-generated abstracts from human-authored ones, assigning a significantly higher probability to AI-generated texts [AI: median 100%, 95% confidence interval (CI) 98% to100% vs humans 2025: median 7%, 95% CI: 1% to 78%; P < .001]. Receiver operating characteristic analysis demonstrated excellent discrimination, with an area under the curve of 0.97 (95% CI: .91–1.0; P < .001), a sensitivity of 90% (95% CI: 74% to 100%), and a specificity of 92% (95% CI: 62% to 99%) at a Youden-Index derived GPTZero probability threshold of ≥90%. There was a trend for an increase in the AI probability prediction for human written abstracts from 2022 to 2025 (2022: median 1%, 95% CI 1%–5% vs 2025: median 7%, 95% CI: 1%–78%; P = .078). The main findings of this study indicate that human reviewers, when left uninformed, did not rate AI-generated abstracts differently from human-authored ones, demonstrating neither overall inferiority nor superiority. However, a dedicated AI detection software was effective in identifying fabricated abstracts, even among those ranked highest by human reviewers. Our results, though concerning, align with prior research showing that human reviewers struggle to identify AI-generated content, even when explicitly asked to do so.5 In contrast, detection software proved highly effective, confirming earlier findings.2,6 Using AI to generate or refine scientific texts is not inherently problematic. However, lack of disclosure,4 and risks of misuse raise serious ethical concerns.5 Generative AI depends on existing literature, and unchecked fabricated content could trigger a snowball effect: spreading misinformation, undermining reproducibility, and potentially endangering patient safety.7,8 These issues highlight the urgent need for a global AI strategy to uphold scientific integrity, as in the end submitting authors are responsible for the scientific accuracy.1 This study's limitations include the absence of a reporting option for suspected AI content and congress policies that do not yet mandate AI disclosure. Further, this study addresses the application and detection of generative AI in the context of scientific publishing and fraud detection, which may extend beyond medical domains. AI-generated abstracts achieved comparable reviewer scores and acceptance rates to human-authored submissions, underscoring the challenge of detecting fraudulent content. These findings emphasize the need for mandatory AI disclosure policies and the implementation of reliable detection tools to safeguard research integrity in the future. As a key takeaway from this study, scientific congresses, societies and journals may wish to explore clearer guidance regarding the disclosure of AI-assisted writing tools during abstract submission. The integration of AI-detection software, such as GPTZero, could be considered as a supportive measure to enhance transparency during the submission and review process. While effective, its accessibility may be limited by licensing, and the software primarily analyses syntactic and not semantic features. While such tools may help identify AI-generated content, decisions around exclusion should be approached with caution and informed by further validation studies. Reviewers, in turn, could benefit from awareness of any preliminary screening measures. Finally, larger datasets will be needed to determine whether variations in abstract evaluation are consistent across different thematic areas. None declared. Supplementary data are not available at European Heart Journal online. ChatGPT, was used for minor language editing (shortening) of this manuscript. All scientific content, analyses, discussion and interpretation were developed by the authors. K.-P.K. is a consultant to Edwards Lifesciences. T.L. does no longer accept any honoraria from industry, but outside this work received research and educational grants to the institution from Abbott, Amgen, AstraZeneca, Boehringer-Ingelheim, Daichi-Sankyo, Menarini Foundation, Novartis, Novo Nordisk, Roche Diagnostics, Sanofi and Vifor. All other authors report no conflicts of interest. Data sharing is possible based on a reasonable request (e.g. for individual data metaanalyses). All authors declare no funding for this contribution. Ethical approval was not required. None supplied.
JT  - Eur Heart J
OT  - Medicine
OT  - Artificial intelligence

PMID- 40607618
TI  - From lipids and inflammation to artificial intelligence-driven therapeutics: the 93rd European atherosclerosis society (EAS) congress 2025 in focus
FAU - Gurgone D
FAU - Kroon J
FAU - Antoniades C.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1093/cvr/cvaf110 [doi]
AB  - Set in Glasgow, a city renowned for innovation, the 93rd EAS Congress 2025 was the ideal stage for sharing breakthroughs and shaping the future of cardiovascular health. The congress welcomed 2100 delegates from 82 countries, opening with the suggestive echoes of Scottish pipe music, setting a fitting tone for the EAS President, Professor Børge Nordestgaard (University of Copenhagen, Denmark), who reminded us that passion and science drive progress against cardiovascular disease. The ceremony continued with greetings from several eminent figures in the field, including Professor Dame Anna Dominiczak (University of Glasgow, UK), Professor Christian Delles (University of Glasgow, UK), and Professor Dirk Blom, president of the International Society of Atherosclerosis (University of Cape Town, South Africa). Professor Tomasz Guzik, president of the British Atherosclerosis Society; BAS (University of Edinburgh, UK), reflected on the vital link between past and present discoveries in the cardiovascular arena. He honoured Glasgow’s rich cardiovascular legacy and traced it back over 400 years to William Harvey’s discovery of blood circulation, reminding us that progress stands on the shoulders of history.1 However, the past is not far from us, as we are witnessing today at the EAS Congress how great minds continue to shape contemporary cardiovascular medicine. The invaluable contribution of the BAS in bringing this congress to life has been duly acknowledged. As highlighted by Professor Charalambos Antoniades, EAS Congress Chair and Co-Chair of the Scientific Programme Committee (University of Oxford, UK), the unwavering support and collaboration of the scientific community are crucial to the success of EAS Congress 2025. Together, we can prove that even Glasgow’s notoriously grey skies can be brightened. From the evocative traditional dances of Scottish folklore to the recognition of scientific excellence, the 93rd EAS Congress Opening Ceremony offers a rich and memorable experience. Since 2007, it has featured one of its most anticipated moments: The presentation of the Anitschkow Prize, the Society’s highest honour. This year, the distinction was awarded to Professor Paul Ridker, Eugene Braunwald Professor of Medicine at Harvard University and Brigham and Women’s Hospital, USA (Figure 1A). (A) Distinction Anitschkow Prize at the EAS Congress 2025 awarded to Professor Paul Ridker (right), presented by the EAS President, Professor Børge Nordestgaard (left). (B) Professor Göran Hansson during his talk, ‘How to win the Nobel Prize’, at the EAS Congress 2025. (C) The honorary Hugh Sinclair Prize at the EAS Congress 2025 awarded to Professor Joseph Wu (right), presented by the BAS President, Professor Tomasz Guzik (left). Professor Børge Nordestgaard opened the session by celebrating Professor Ridker’s unique blend of scientific brilliance and personal humility, a timely reminder that behind great science stand human qualities that truly endure. In his keynote lecture, Professor Ridker traced the evolving chronicle of atherosclerosis research. Reminding us that while the story has long centred on the conquest of cholesterol,2 it has now decisively shifted to targeting inflammation. Professor Ridker outlined how this understanding has redefined cardiovascular prevention and therapy. The lecture highlighted pivotal clinical milestones, emphasizing how progress blends insight, perseverance, and the occasional stroke of luck. Yet, as Professor Ridker thoughtfully observed, what matters most in research is not fortune itself, but the capacity to turn setbacks into opportunities for discovery. The defining climax of the session came with a reflection on the landmark CANTOS trial, conducted with Professor Peter Libby (2013 EAS Anitschkow Prize). CANTOS was the first large-scale clinical trial to demonstrate that targeting inflammation—specifically by canakinumab, an interleukin-1β blocking antibody—could significantly reduce cardiovascular event rates, independent of lipid-lowering regimens.3 But as Professor Ridker reminded the audience, science rarely moves forward in a straight line. Buoyed by CANTOS’ success, the subsequent CIRT trial aimed to evaluate a broader anti-inflammatory approach using low-dose methotrexate. However, unlike its predecessor, CIRT did not produce the hoped-for cardiovascular benefits, offering a sobering, yet valuable lesson: not all inflammation is the same and non-specific interventions may fall short in reducing vascular risk.4 Professor Ridker frames CIRT’s outcomes not as a disappointment, but as a turning point, a call to return from the bedside to the bench, and refine our understanding of the molecular drivers of residual inflammatory risk in cardiovascular disease.5 In closing, he stressed that the future of cardiovascular prevention lies in combining cholesterol control with targeted, anti-inflammatory strategies, calling for inflammatory biomarkers to enter primary routine care. Safeguarding the future remains central to EAS’s mission, with this year’s congress reaffirming its commitment to the United Nations’ 2030 Sustainable Development Goals, including gender equality. Professor Jeanine Roeters van Lennep (Erasmus Medical Center, Netherlands, and EAS Vice-President) opened the first plenary session on a long-neglected topic in cardiovascular research: ‘Sex and gender in cardiovascular disease’. An outstanding panel of eminent women leaders in the field, Professor Vera Regitz-Zagrosek (Charité—University Medicine Berlin, Germany), Professor Karen Reue (University of California, USA), Professor Gemma Figtree (University of Sydney, Australia), and Professor Lale Tokgözoğlu (Hacettepe University, Turkey, and EAS Past-President), challenged the outdated ‘one disease, one sex’ model, highlighting how sex and gender profoundly influence cardiovascular risk, disease progression, and treatment outcomes. Yet, women remain under-represented in clinical trials, and sex-specific analyses are often absent. The speakers called for imperative change, stressing that genetic, hormonal, and biological differences shape cardiovascular health across the lifespan, and that primary care must reflect this. To drive this essential shift, funding bodies, policymakers, and research institutions must collaborate in supporting studies specifically designed to address sex and gender differences in cardiovascular disease. A particularly insightful session drew connections between ground breaking scientific discoveries, their impact on human health, and the path to the Nobel Prize. Professor Göran Hansson, Secretary General of the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences and Vice Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Nobel Foundation (Karolinska Institute, Sweden), was joined by pioneers who helped shift the perception of atherosclerosis from being solely a lipid-driven disease to one deeply rooted in immune and inflammatory mechanisms.6 The question posed: ‘How do you win a Nobel Prize?’ With wit and warmth, Professor Hansson shared anecdotes showing that even the most outstanding scientists often doubt their worthiness. He recalled Professor James E. Rothman’s 2013 Nobel for vesicle trafficking, the perseverance of Professor Barry Marshall and Dr Robin Warren, whose H. pylori discovery won the 2005 Nobel after years of rejection, and Professor Sir David MacMillan, who thought news of his 2021 Nobel was a prank. Professor Hansson went on to reflect on Nobel laureates whose work transformed the fields of cardiovascular physiology and medicine, honouring those who fundamentally reformed our understanding of human health. He also highlighted the pressing issue of addressing gender imbalance in Nobel Prize nominations. Rooted in a complex history of biases and the under-representation of women in senior medical and scientific roles, Professor Hansson called for a change that, while slow, is gradually taking place. In closing, with characteristic acuity, Professor Hansson reminded the audience that while created by men, the Nobel Prize exists to honour those whose work advances civilization, a timeless reminder of its true purpose (Figure 1B). History shows transformative health breakthroughs stem from revolutionary ideas. Like Anitschkow’s studies, which first linked cholesterol to atherosclerotic plaque formation, this year’s second plenary session stressed expanding beyond classical cholesterol management to understand cardiovascular risk better. The session spotlighted key but often under-recognized contributors to cardiovascular health. Professor Thomas Münzel (Johannes Gutenberg University Mainz, Germany) discussed environmental pollution as a modifiable external factor, while Professor Ziad Mallat (University of Cambridge, UK) explored the immune system as a key internal driver of chronic vascular inflammation. EAS Young Investigator Award, Elias Björnson (University of Gothenburg, Sweden), challenged the ‘LDL-only’ paradigm, advocating for particle-specific lipid risk assessment. Concluding the session, Professor Naveed Sattar (University of Glasgow, UK) delivered a provocative message: while lipid management remains vital, it must be integrated with a focus on metabolic health, lifestyle factors, and the broader societal influences that shape dietary choices and public health. A powerful reminder that tackling cardiovascular disease requires an all-encompassing and community-based approach strategy. The Hugh Sinclair Award, presented by the BAS, celebrates enduring excellence in cardiovascular science. This year, the honour was bestowed upon the renowned expert in cardiovascular research, Professor Joseph Wu, Director of the Stanford Cardiovascular Institute, USA7 (Figure 1C). In his memorial lecture, ‘Stem Cells, Genomics, and AI for Drug Discovery’, Professor Wu offered a forward-looking perspective on how the academic and clinical landscape is rapidly evolving. He emphasized the shift towards personalized medicine, with AI poised to play a central role in improving cardiovascular health; Professor Wu has contributed extensively to our understanding of the role of Lamins at the nuclear periphery as a new factor increasingly recognized as critical in the pathogenesis of cardiovascular diseases.8 His work using induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) to model cardiovascular disorders has also opened new avenues for personalized therapeutic strategies.9 He furthermore underscored how AI-driven drug discovery is reshaping the future of cardiovascular medicine, not only by accelerating the identification of novel therapies but also by repurposing existing drugs.10 He stressed that AI, iPSCs, and personalized medicine are no longer isolated disciplines but are converging with a shared goal: to improve patient outcomes while reducing reliance on animal models. Continuing the momentum of recognizing AI as a powerful ally in tackling cardiovascular disease, the final plenary session of the congress, ‘Artificial Intelligence and Atherosclerosis: A Glimpse into the Future’, offered a compelling vision of what lies ahead. Professor Thomas F. Lüscher (Royal Brompton and Harefield Hospitals, UK), Professor Sir Rory Collins (University of Oxford, UK), Professor Ron Blankstein (Brigham and Women’s Hospital, USA), and Professor Manuel Mayr (Imperial College London, UK) came together to show how AI, when paired with access to vast resources like the UK Biobank, is not a tool to fear but an invaluable partner in transforming cardiovascular diagnostics, risk prediction, and personalized care. The speakers highlighted how AI can shift traditional risk assessment towards dynamic, individualized predictions, advancing the long-standing goal of precision medicine. Combined with multi-omics technologies, AI provides unprecedented insights into the molecular, genetic, and metabolic layers of cardiovascular diseases, turning what once seemed futuristic into today’s emerging reality. As EAS Congress 2025 wraps up, we leave inspired by the incredible science, new ideas, and connections made over these past few days. The work presented here matters, and we are excited to see where it takes us next. Like two good friends saying farewell on the Bonnie Banks of Glasgow, heading for the legendary shores of Greece, this is not a goodbye; it is just an Arrivederci. See you at the 94th EAS Congress 2026 in Athens! We gratefully acknowledge the European Atherosclerosis Society Office and Professor Pasquale Maffia (University of Glasgow, UK, and BAS Committee Member) for kindly providing the photographs used in this commentary. J.K. was supported by the Dutch Heart Foundation (Senior Scientist Dekker grant 03-004-2021-T045), funded by the European Union (ERC, ENDOMET-STEER, 101076407), and NWO-Vidi grant (09150172310053). C.A. is supported by British Heart Foundation (CH/F/21/90009 and RG/F/21/110040), the NIHR Oxford Biomedical Research Centre (Cardiac and Imaging themes), and Oxford British Heart Foundation Oxford Centre of Research Excellence. Danila Gurgone is currently a Lecturer in Pharmacology at the School of Cardiovascular and Metabolic Health and an Affiliated Researcher at the School of Infection and Immunity, University of Glasgow, UK. Her research focuses on the role of the immune response in cardiovascular diseases (CVD), encompassing both basic and clinical studies, with a particular interest in targeting immune and inflammatory mechanisms underlying conditions such as atherosclerosis, hypertension, and heart failure. She is an active nucleus member of the British Atherosclerosis Society (BAS) Young Scientist Community (YSC) and has previously contributed to projects supported by the European Society of Cardiology (ESC), the British Heart Foundation (BHF), and the Italian Ministry for Universities and Research (MUR). Jeffrey Kroon is an Associate Professor, Principal Investigator, and Deputy Head of the Department of Experimental Vascular Medicine at Amsterdam University Medical Centers location AMC, the Netherlands. He is also cross-appointed to the Laboratory of Angiogenesis and Vascular Metabolism at VIB-KU Leuven, Belgium. His research focuses on the intersection of vascular metabolism and inflammation, specifically exploring how the immunometabolic-inflammatory axis drives cardiovascular disease. Currently, Jeffrey and his team are investigating endothelial cell metabolic and immunomodulatory changes in response to atherogenic stimuli using 3D organ-on-chip systems, pre-clinical murine models and human studies. Their work spans translational, pre-clinical, and clinical research, aiming to bridge the gap between the lab and clinical practice. Jeffrey earned his PhD from Sanquin Research and the Landsteiner Laboratory at the University of Amsterdam, where he studied the molecular mechanisms of leucocyte transendothelial migration. His long-term goal is to strengthen endothelial function by selectively targeting intracellular metabolism and modulating the endothelium’s immunomodulatory roles to combat cardiovascular disease. Charis Antoniades holds a BHF Chair of Cardiovascular Medicine at the University of Oxford, and he practices as a Consultant Cardiologist at Oxford University Hospitals. He was awarded his PhD title with hons the University of Athens, and during his PhD studies he won multiple Young Investigator award competitions, including those of the European Society of Cardiology (ESC) twice, American College of Cardiology, ISHR and others. He has been awarded the Outstanding Achievement Award of the basic Cardiovascular Science Council of the European Society of Cardiology in 2016, a National clinical excellence award in 2021 and an N3 National Clinical Impact Award in 2024, while he has given various named lectures. He is the Director, Acute Multi-disciplinary Imaging and Interventional Centre, University of Oxford, the Deputy Head of the Division of Cardiovascular Medicine. He also served as the Chair of the British Atherosclerosis Society 2021–2024, while he is involved in several leadership roles within the ESC. His research is focused on the study of the cross-talk between adipose tissue and the cardiovascular system, with specific interest in the non-invasive imaging of inflammation and the use of artificial intelligence (AI) for risk prediction. He is deputy editor of Cardiovascular Research and serves at several editorial boards, while he is one of the founders of the ESC Scientists of Tomorrow. He has published more than 400 peer review scientific papers in high impact journals like the Lancet, Science Transl Med, Circulation, JACC, EHJ and others. Finally, he is founder and Chief Scientific Officer of Caristo Diagnostics, a university of Oxford spinout company.
