AB  - Generative artificial intelligence can be used to create realistic new data, even for complex real-world processes that cannot be exhaustively modeled: the model is simply learned from preexisting data. Generative artificial intelligence is therefore expected to be a game changer in omics research, where data collection is hampered by considerable experimental constraints. However, it can also "hallucinate"-i.e., create data that are too original to be realistic-which is a critical issue in molecular biology, as hallucinated inferences could have devastating consequences. The author thus explores various use cases to mitigate hallucination-induced risks and to safely unleash the full potential of generative methods.
