OT  - Surgery

PMID- 38327005
TI  - Digital technology and artificial intelligence for improving congenital heart disease care: alea iacta est
FAU - Bruce C
FAU - Gatzoulis MA
FAU - Brida M.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/eurheartj/ehad898 [doi]
AB  - Congenital heart disease (CHD) comprises a heterogeneous patient population who require specialist care throughout their life. The global prevalence of CHD is constantly growing with progress in surgical and medical interventions resulting in >90% of new-borns with CHD surviving into adulthood. Innovation has long been alive in cardiology with CHD often leading the way, as seen with the first percutaneous valve implantation carried out in 2000 on a CHD patient with right ventricle to pulmonary artery conduit stenosis.1 Rapid evolution is often born out of enforced change, and this is true with digital technology; during the Covid pandemic, digital technology and artificial intelligence (AI) were propelled to the forefront in healthcare. A number of papers on digital technology and AI in CHD have exponentially increased since the beginning of this century; in the early 2000s, PubMed recorded a handful of papers a year, but since 2020, over 300 papers have been published and numbers are increasing almost daily (Table 1). Digital technology and AI can be helpful in different areas of CHD including identification, diagnosis, imaging, risk stratification, intervention, and personalized management (Figure 1). Its implementations are designed to augment clinical care, improve diagnostic accuracy, and save time, but will we come to a point where the clinician is a thing of the past? Digital technology and artificial intelligence for congenital heart disease. Original illustration created with the help of imaging software BioRender.com A systematic overview of original research in digital technology and artificial intelligence relevant to the management of congenital heart disease published in the last 5 years 6MWT, 6-min walk test; AI, artificial intelligence; CCTGA, congenitally corrected transposition of the great arteries; CHD, congenital heart disease; CNN, convolutional neural network; DORV, double outlet right ventricle; FCN, fully convolutional network; GAN, generative adversarial network; LV, left ventricle; MDT, multi-disciplinary team; NYHA, New York Heart Association functional class; PAP, pulmonary arterial pressure; RHC, right heart catheterization; RV, right ventricle; TGA, transposition of the great arteries. Digital consultation is now common in cardiology outpatient clinic and is particularly well suited to CHD, where patients may be geographically spread, but necessary skills and knowledge are concentrated centrally. Furthermore, with a young patient population, this form of consultation allows for freedom of movement with schooling, careers, and travel. This is often facilitated through video consultations, allowing for personalized care, albeit with inherent limitations in comparison with clinical settings that allow for vitals measurements, auscultation, etc. Home monitoring increased as patients became more aware of their own health management and needed distant monitoring. Anecdotally, more individuals now have home monitors with the ability to provide heart rate and blood pressure readings, more reflective of true systemic blood pressure. Smartwatches provide continuous data for heart rate variability, whereas mobile devices enable recording of diagnostic quality electrocardiogram (ECG) traces. Auscultation is the next barrier to telemedicine that is being addressed; Stagg et al.2 looked at the feasibility of monitoring a small cohort of 15 paediatric inter-stage surgical palliation patients with digital stethoscopes. Parents were taught stethoscope positioning to allow remote auscultation that led to gathering important additional information in 12% of the remote visits and led to expedited life-saving care in two patients. Development of AI algorithms has also been carried out to enhance the use of digital stethoscopes to enable the detection of CHD without clinician input.3 These developments could also allow for longitudinal monitoring of patients, with accurate comparison year on year of auscultation sounds, removing operator variability. Convolutional neural networks can be trained to classify heart sounds as abnormal with characterization done using phonocardiograms. Furthermore, wearable biosensors offer continuous assessment of physiological data that could make routine follow-up unnecessary activity. This has already happened in the pacing world where home monitors allow for remote downloads and rhythm and lead measurements are automatically sent to central hospital systems in real time. Wearable biosensors potentially applicable to CHD include monitoring of oxygen saturations, cardiac rhythm, heart rate variability, blood pressure, step count, seismocardiography, impedance/volumetry, and sweat lactate and electrolytes.4 Small patient studies have shown non-invasive seismocardiography measurements of stroke volume in CHD to be comparable with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) values. These wearable biosensors have been used in conjunction with ECG monitoring and machine learning in heart failure to detect cardiac decompensation.5 Further developments in digital technology and AI are aimed at improving non-invasive assessment; AI is now used in adult CHD imaging to reduce acquisition time and improve image optimization, with studies showing its applicability in diagnosis and surveillance. Machine learning algorithms have been applied to echocardiography to detect specific lesions and enable segmentation analysis with clear clinical implications; AI has been shown to detect a morphological right ventricle in the systemic position with an overall accuracy of 98%; this deep learning model was also able to delineate the right ventricle for segmentation analysis.6 Post-processing software is used in cardiac MRI to automatically analyse ventricular size and function, but this relies on accurate ventricular contouring; standard software at present is less accurate in CHD leading to time-consuming manual evaluation. However, recently developed dedicated AI methodology has shown strong agreement between automatic calculation and manual evaluation.7 The latest area where digital technology and AI are making inroads is invasive cardiology. Assessing pulmonary pressures is of great importance in CHD management, and to date, there is insufficient correlation between non-invasive measures and the gold standard, invasive catheterization. The latest handheld device, Acorai multi-sensor, is looking to change this8; using a combination of sensor information, initial AI modelling has shown better than expected correlation of mean pulmonary arterial pressure values compared with invasive data (P = .75, mean difference of 0.78 mmHg). In addition to right heart pressures, preliminary data have shown accurate detection of pulmonary capillary wedge pressures greater than 22 mmHg with 95% sensitivity and 96% positive predictive value, though specificity and negative predictive values were low. With the ability for continuous data collection and expedited imaging analysis, we are building up a wealth of data in CHD cohort. This allows for improved monitoring of the individual patient and also opens the door for large data analysis, prognostic models, and risk stratification. Machine learning algorithms can collect large volumes of data quickly and efficiently and have been used in CHD to estimate prognosis and guide therapy.9 So, what does the future look like? With these advancements, ‘routine’ follow-up can become a thing of the past, with patients wearing their monitoring every day. Their mobile phone will alert them to a change in heart rhythm; a patch worn on the skin will recognize the fact they are becoming fluidly overloaded and recommend diuretic regime changes; a digital stethoscope will detect a change in murmur suggesting valvular disease progression or endocarditis and book them for an urgent echocardiogram; every time they go for a run, their multi-sensory device will capture the response to exertion on heart rate, blood pressure, saturations, ECG tracing, capillary wedge pressure, mean pulmonary arterial pressure, and cardiac output, all of which may indicate the need for intervention. This constant monitoring of longitudinal data will then be fed continuously into an algorithm that will risk stratify and recommend, for example, the timing of ICD implantation with a personalized risk factor profile. Luckily for cardiologists, we do not suggest these technical advances will make us obsolete. A recent systematic review of patients’ views of AI in healthcare showed a general acceptance, with the perceived strengths of increased accuracy, efficiency, and patient empowerment. There was, however, a strong preference to keep providers ‘in the loop’ and a perceived weakness in inability to communicate and empathize, and depersonalization, with a concern of dependence on technology.10 The complexity of human interactions and the impact of non-quantifiable aspects are paramount and the good physicians’ ability to tease apart the shortness of breath stemming from cardiac decompensation compared with co-morbidities and deconditioning, or palpitations from anxiety is still a skill derived from years of experience and knowledge of the human condition. All authors declare no disclosure of interest for this contribution.
