PMID- 38237066
TI  - Reply: Efficacy of segmented axial length and artificial intelligence approaches to intraocular lens power calculation in short eyes
FAU - Kozhaya K
FAU - Kenny PI
FAU - Wang L
FAU - Weikert MP
FAU - Koch DD.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1097/j.jcrs.0000000000001400 [doi]
AB  - We thank Cione et al. for their comments on our manuscript.1 We appreciate them informing us of their corrected axial length (AL) formula, which was previously unknown to us. We agree with them that the corrected AL could have been used as an alternative to the Cooke-modified AL used in our study. For certain data sets, we recommend against adjusting the mean error to zero. Outlier data sets that do not reflect the normal population, such as short eyes, are one example.2 For such a cohort, optimizing the intraocular lens (IOL) constant may be misleading or unhelpful for surgeons for 2 reasons: (1) for short eyes, surgeons typically use constants optimized for the population as a whole and (2) for data sets like short eyes, we have no assurance that our sample accurately represents the larger population of short eyes, which have high anatomical variability for parameters such as corneal power, anterior chamber depth, and corneal diameter. When comparing non-Gaussian and/or heteroscedastic prediction errors in which the mean errors are not zero, the root mean square absolute error and mean absolute error (MAE) should be reported. It is important to emphasize that the nonparametric bootstrap t-method function we used in our analysis of MAE is designed to deal with nonnormal distributions. As such, we believe that the metrics we chose are valid for analyzing these formulas' performances for short eyes. Reporting the median and the median absolute errors, as suggested by the authors, would also be a valid alternative.2 To test formula precision in data sets like short eyes, rather than constant optimization, the preferred method is to calculate the SDs of the prediction errors. We therefore show in the attached updated table, the SDs for the 15 formulas listed in Table 2 of the original manuscript (Table 1).1 Table 1. - Prediction error SDs of 15 formulas using Td-AL vs CMAL measurements Formulas Td-AL CMAL SD RMSAE SD RMSAE ZEISS AI 0.52 0.55 — — K6 0.52 0.53 0.52 0.55 EVO 2.0 0.60 0.60 0.59 0.59 RBF 3.0 0.58 0.58 0.58 0.64 Kane 0.57 0.59 0.57 0.58 Pearl-DGS 0.59 0.60 0.59 0.60 Barrett 0.60 0.60 0.60 0.64 Haigis 0.60 0.60 0.60 0.65 Holladay 1 0.59 0.62 0.60 0.60 Castrop 0.66 0.66 — — Olsen 0.63 0.64 0.65 0.68 SRK/T 0.60 0.61 0.61 0.62 Hoffer QST 0.59 0.66 0.58 0.59 Holladay 2 0.62 0.72 0.62 0.63 Hoffer Q 0.62 0.73 0.61 0.63 CMAL = Cooke-modified axial length; RMSAE = root mean square absolute error; Td-AL = traditional axial lengthThe RMSAE values reported in the paper are included for reader convenience The ZEISS AI formula is unique in that it is optimized for each IOL model without relying on its constant. Currently, the ZEISS AI calculator is exclusively available in the United States and on the ZEISS VERACITY Surgery Planner software (Carl Zeiss Meditec AG). For the purposes of this manuscript, we shared our database with ZEISS to calculate the ZEISS AI predictions.
