PMID- 38894669
TI  - Artificial intelligence vis-à-vis Foucault’s medical gaze: implications for healthcare, public health, and prison
FAU - Octaviano ATB
FAU - Baac MBG
FAU - Selso TBS
FAU - Estoya EAV
FAU - Buena ZME
FAU - Hinlo HKL
FAU - Gernale LAM
FAU - Hesido SA
FAU - Pacaol NF.
DP  - 2024
AID - 10.1093/pubmed/fdae106 [doi]
AB  - In the April 2024 editorial, the editors concluded that ‘public health has a role to play to garner the possibilities and benefits of AI while carefully considering the challenges and unintended consequences of AI’.1 This correspondence uses Michel Foucault’s concept of the ‘medical gaze’ as an attempt to explain how the current developments in AI-driven healthcare can reduce patients to mere objects of clinical knowledge, devoid of personal and social contexts—a process known as ‘algorithmic dehumanization’. That is, ‘the aspect of the patient [or individual] that matters most is no longer the aspect that talks, thinks and feels, the one that can be interviewed and examined’.2 The gaze can be extended to public health, where populations are monitored, categorized and managed through large-scale data collection and statistical analysis to track disease outbreaks, assess health risks and implement interventions. Or, in prison, risk assessment algorithms are used to assess potential recidivism risk. This reductionism carries a risk as it may not capture some important determinants of health beyond biomedical data, such as sensitivity to cultural and socio-economic factors. The medical gaze in medicine, public health and prisons shares common elements: the use of surveillance, categorization and data-driven decision-making to manage or treat individuals. It has the potential to diminish the personal and empathetic aspects of healthcare. Traditionally, the medical gaze can already distance healthcare providers from the personal experiences of the patient or the general population. The AI integration even intensifies the shift by turning people into numbers, probabilities and trends within datasets rather than individuals with unique experiences and needs. Consequently, healthcare providers hinge heavily on algorithmic outputs, which, though at some point efficient, lack the ability to account for the compound lived experiences that are essential for holistic care. Here, people’s experiences can be adversely affected. It may undervalue or ignore a person’s sense of agency and individuality, thereby, eroding trust and confidence in the healthcare system. It is crucial to nurture human empathy and personal connection with individuals within and beyond healthcare institutions. In solidarity with the growing research in the field to fill the gap and address the current limitations of artificial intelligence, especially in public health applications, we offer the following recommendations to state leaders and researchers: (i) collaboration between healthcare professionals, data scientists, ethicists, social scientists and community stakeholders to guarantee sensitive and responsive AI-driven public health initiatives across cultural, social and ethical dimensions; (ii) maintain human oversight and accountability in AI-driven public health interventions; (iii) engage with communities to build trust in AI by being transparent and accountable about its limitations and benefits in healthcare and (iv) incorporate cultural competence and sensitivity training into the education and training of healthcare providers and AI developers. For this paper, the authors have disclosed no conflicts of interest. No funding was received for this paper.
