AB  - TO THE EDITOR We tested a previous 12-disease version of the Han et al. (2018) algorithm and found limited external validity on a 100-image dataset of skin cancer (Navarrete-Dechent et al., 2018). Here, we evaluated the external validity and reliability of the newly updated 174disease Han et al. (2020b) algorithm and a recently published region-based 178-disease algorithm (Han et al., 2020a) using the same dataset. We used a public dataset of clinical images composed of sequentially biopsied cutaneous melanomas (n 1⁄4 37), basal cell carcinomas (n 1⁄4 40), and squamous cell carcinomas (14 in situ, 9 invasive). A total of 15 melanomas were in situ. The mean (minimumemaximum) thickness of invasive melanomas was 0.6 mm (0.1e3.1 mm). All lesions originated from Caucasian patients in the United States and were located on the head and neck (n 1⁄4 26), trunk (n 1⁄4 21), upper extremities (n 1⁄4 30), and lower extremities (n 1⁄4 23). The mean age of the cohort was 66.8 years (minimumemaximum, 25e85 years), and 67% were male. For testing of the 174-disease classifier (Han et al., 2020b), we uploaded images to https://modelderm.com/ on 7e9 April 2020. The web app allows users to select a 250-by-250 pixel square region of interest (ROI). The ROI selection is performed by scaling down the image (between 0 and 1 times magnification of the image) and panning within the image view window. This process changes the resolution of the images that are uploaded to the system. We conducted four sets of upload experiments. In the intended use upload, we selected an ROI that (i) centered the lesion and (ii) was w80% covered by the lesion. In the intended
