OT  - Computer science

PMID- 40522045
TI  - Integrating Clinical Insights and Methodological Refinements: A Commentary on Fall Risk Prediction in Parkinson's Disease
FAU - Ren B.
DP  - 2025
AID - 10.1111/ene.70254 [doi]
AB  - We read with great interest the article titled “Machine Learning Predicts Risk of Falls in Parkinson's Disease Patients in a Multicenter Observational Study,” recently published in the European Journal of Neurology [1]. This study presents an innovative and clinically relevant approach by employing machine learning (ML) models to predict fall risk in Parkinson's disease (PD) patients using routinely collected clinical data across multiple centers. We particularly commend the integration of explainable AI (SHAP) for model interpretability, and the effort to externally validate the model on a U.S.-based cohort, which adds valuable credibility and potential for generalizability. Despite these merits, we would like to highlight several methodological limitations not explicitly discussed by the authors. First, the study did not incorporate time-series or longitudinal data, despite aiming to predict falls over a 3-year period. Relying solely on baseline variables may miss dynamic patterns in disease progression. Incorporating sequential data or applying temporal models (e.g., LSTM) could enhance prediction accuracy [2]. Second, the ML algorithm choices were relatively basic (logistic regression and support vector classifier), while more robust ensemble methods such as XGBoost or Random Forest, or even neural network-based models, may offer superior performance and resilience to noise [3, 4]. Third, the analysis did not explore subgroup-specific risks, such as gender, age strata, cognitive status, or medication profiles, despite known heterogeneity in PD progression and fall etiology. Fourth, the model did not adjust for or explore potential interactions between variables (e.g., motor symptoms and medication side effects), which might affect fall risk in complex ways. Cultural and environmental factors, including living situation or caregiver support, were not considered but may substantially modulate real-world fall risks. Building upon the promising findings of the study, we believe it is essential to situate predictive modeling within a broader framework of fall prevention and clinical response. Specifically, we propose the development of a three-tiered fall risk management strategy tailored for PD patients. At the foundational level, predictive models such as the one presented in this study could be integrated into electronic medical records to serve as an early warning system during routine consultations, facilitating timely identification of high-risk individuals. The second level would involve the deployment of personalized secondary preventive measures, including medication adjustments, tailored physiotherapy programs, and cognitive-motor training, informed by both model predictions and clinician judgment. Finally, at the tertiary level, a coordinated post-fall response protocol should be established, encompassing rapid reassessment, rehabilitation planning, and social support engagement to mitigate long-term functional decline and recurrent falls. This hierarchical system not only aligns with the principles of predictive, preventive, and personalized medicine but also offers a practical framework to translate AI-driven insights into actionable clinical workflows. In conclusion, this study provides a robust foundation for AI-assisted risk stratification in PD patients, with clear implications for clinical care optimization. Our letter highlights avenues for methodological enhancement and advocates for embedding such predictive tools into a comprehensive, tiered management strategy to fully realize their potential in improving patient outcomes. All authors contributed to the study conception and design. Study design, BR; Writing – original draft, BR; Writing – review and editing, BR; Supervision: BR. The author has nothing to report. The author declares no conflicts of interest. Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.
